\id MAT - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Matthew
\toc1 The Good News According to Matthew
\toc2 Matthew
\toc3 Mat
\mt1 Matthew

\imt Introduction
\ip This book carries no author's name. The title "According to Matthew" comes from the early copies, not from anything inside the text. What the text does is put a tax collector twice into its own story: at 9:9 a man sitting at the toll booth is called and named Matthew, and in the list of the twelve at 10:3 he alone is tagged with the trade he left — "Matthew the tax collector." Past that, the writer stays out of sight.
\ip He is writing for people who live inside Jewish practice and need none of it explained. He can say "phylacteries," "the tradition of the elders," "a gift" meaning a thing vowed to the temple, "on a Sabbath" meaning a day you cannot travel, and move straight on. He avoids saying "God" where a devout Jew would avoid it, writing "the kingdom of the heavens" instead. And he argues from Israel's scriptures on nearly every page, with a formula he uses over and over — "so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled" — that ties the events of this man's life back into the story of Israel. His readers are expected to catch a whole passage from half a line of it.
\ip They are also in a fight. The synagogues in this book are "their synagogues" and "your synagogues"; the people who follow Jesus are flogged in them and chased from town to town (10:17; 23:34). At the end the writer notes that the story that the disciples stole the body is still being told "to this day" (28:15). These are Jewish followers of Jesus arguing with other Jews out of the same scriptures, and being pushed out of the same buildings. The one geographical hint the book drops is that word of him ran out "into all Syria" (4:24).
\ip Over the whole second half stands the temple. Jesus says that not one stone of it will be left on another (24:2), and in one of his parables a king burns the city of the men who murdered his messengers (22:7). Rome destroyed Jerusalem and burned the temple in the year 70. Whether this was written just before that or just after, it is written in its shadow, for people who have to work out what a Jew who follows Jesus does now.

\c 1
\s1 The Line from Abraham to the Christ
\p
\v 1 The book of the generations\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl biblos geneseōs\tl*, the very phrase the Greek Old Testament uses at Genesis 2:4 and again at Genesis 5:1, "the book of the generations of Adam." Opening this way announces not a family tree but a beginning, set in line with the beginnings in Genesis.\f* of Jesus Christ\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl Christos\tl*, translating Hebrew \tl mashiach\tl*, anointed one. Oil poured on the head was how a man was installed in office — king (1 Samuel 16:13), priest (Exodus 29:7). It is a job title, not a surname: the one God has installed to rule.\f*, son of David \add [i.e. heir to the throne God swore would never fail]\add*, son of Abraham \add [i.e. and heir to the promise that through this family every nation on earth would be blessed]\add*.
\v 2 Abraham fathered Isaac, Isaac fathered Jacob, Jacob fathered Judah and his brothers,
\v 3 Judah fathered Perez and Zerah by Tamar\f + \fr 1:3 \ft The first of four women named in a list that otherwise runs father to son. Tamar posed as a prostitute to get from Judah the child he owed her and was told, "she is more righteous than I" (Genesis 38). Rahab (v. 5) was a Canaanite prostitute in Jericho (Joshua 2). Ruth (v. 5) was a Moabite, out of a people shut out of the assembly (Deuteronomy 23:3). And the wife of Uriah (v. 6) is not given her name at all, so that what stands in the record is David's adultery and the murder of the foreigner who had served him. Three of the four are outsiders; all four are irregularities; the line runs straight through every one of them.\f*, Perez fathered Hezron, Hezron fathered Ram,
\v 4 Ram fathered Amminadab, Amminadab fathered Nahshon, Nahshon fathered Salmon,
\v 5 Salmon fathered Boaz by Rahab, Boaz fathered Obed by Ruth, Obed fathered Jesse,
\v 6 and Jesse fathered David the king. And David fathered Solomon by the wife of Uriah,
\v 7 Solomon fathered Rehoboam, Rehoboam fathered Abijah, Abijah fathered Asaph,
\v 8 Asaph fathered Jehoshaphat, Jehoshaphat fathered Joram, Joram fathered Uzziah,
\v 9 Uzziah fathered Jotham, Jotham fathered Ahaz, Ahaz fathered Hezekiah,
\v 10 Hezekiah fathered Manasseh, Manasseh fathered Amos, Amos fathered Josiah,
\v 11 and Josiah fathered Jeconiah and his brothers, at the deportation to Babylon \add [i.e. the catastrophe that ended everything — land, temple and throne lost together]\add*.
\v 12 And after the deportation to Babylon, Jeconiah fathered Shealtiel, Shealtiel fathered Zerubbabel,
\v 13 Zerubbabel fathered Abiud, Abiud fathered Eliakim, Eliakim fathered Azor,
\v 14 Azor fathered Zadok, Zadok fathered Achim, Achim fathered Eliud,
\v 15 Eliud fathered Eleazar, Eleazar fathered Matthan, Matthan fathered Jacob,
\v 16 and Jacob fathered Joseph the husband of Mary, from whom was born Jesus\f + \fr 1:16 \ft The chain of "fathered" breaks here and is never finished. "From whom" is feminine in the Greek: it points to Mary alone, not to Joseph. Forty generations of fathers, and then the list stops just short of saying that Joseph fathered this one. The paragraph that follows exists to say why.\f*, the one called Christ.
\v 17 So all the generations from Abraham to David are fourteen generations, and from David to the deportation to Babylon fourteen generations, and from the deportation to Babylon to the Christ fourteen generations.\f + \fr 1:17 \ft Fourteen is not the number history gives; four kings known from Israel's own records have been left out to reach it. In Hebrew the letters are also numbers, and the three letters of David's name add up to fourteen. The shape of the list is itself the argument, and so are its three parts: rise, from Abraham to the throne; fall, from the throne to Babylon; and return, from Babylon to the Christ.\f*
\s1 How Jesus Was Born
\p
\v 18 Now the birth of Jesus Christ happened this way. When his mother Mary had been pledged in marriage to Joseph \add [i.e. legally his wife already, though not yet living with him — a bond only death or divorce could break]\add*, before they came together she was found to be carrying a child by the Holy Spirit.
\v 19 And Joseph her husband, being a righteous man \add [i.e. a man who kept the Torah, and the Torah gave him every right to expose her]\add* and not willing to make a public show of her, decided to divorce her quietly.
\v 20 But while he was turning these things over, look — an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream, saying, "Joseph, son of David \add [i.e. and that is why this falls to you: the throne runs through your house]\add*, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife, for what has been conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit.
\v 21 She will bear a son, and you shall call his name Jesus\f + \fr 1:21 \ft Hebrew \tl Yeshua\tl*, "the LORD rescues." The sentence turns on the name: he is called Rescue because he will rescue. Naming the child is the father's act, and by doing it Joseph makes the child legally his — which is how a son of Mary becomes a son of David.\f*, for he will rescue his people \add [i.e. Israel]\add* from their sins."\f + \fr 1:21 \ft Not from Rome. Israel's hope ran toward a deliverer who would throw off the occupation; the prophets had put the trouble deeper down, in the covenant unfaithfulness that had sent the nation into exile in the first place, and had promised that God would deal with that (Jeremiah 31:31-34; Ezekiel 36:25-27; Daniel 9:24).\f*
\v 22 All this happened so that what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled:
\v 23 "Look, the virgin will conceive and will bear a son, and they will call his name Emmanuel,"\f + \fr 1:23 \ft Isaiah 7:14, quoted in its Greek form, which reads \tl parthenos\tl*, virgin, where the Hebrew has \tl almah\tl*, a young woman of marriageable age. Isaiah spoke it to king Ahaz as a sign inside his own crisis: before a child conceived now is old enough to tell right from wrong, the two kings threatening you will be gone.\f* which translated is, "God with us."\f + \fr 1:23 \ft Where this book starts is where it ends. Its last sentence is "I am with you all the days, until the completion of the age" (28:20).\f*
\v 24 And when Joseph woke from his sleep he did as the angel of the Lord had ordered him, and took his wife;
\v 25 and he did not know her \add [i.e. had no relations with her]\add* until she had borne a son. And he called his name Jesus.
\c 2
\s1 Magi from the East Come Looking for the King
\p
\v 1 Now when Jesus had been born in Bethlehem of Judea in the days of Herod the king \add [i.e. an Idumean whom Rome had installed, and no son of David at all]\add*, look — magi\f + \fr 2:1 \ft Greek \tl magoi\tl*, the priestly and scholarly class of Persia and Babylon, men who read the sky. "From the east" points back toward the country of the exile. Israel's own scriptures already had a star-oracle in the mouth of a seer from the east: Balaam, hired to curse Israel, said instead, "a star will come out of Jacob, and a scepter will rise out of Israel" (Numbers 24:17).\f* from the east arrived in Jerusalem,
\v 2 saying, "Where is the one born king of the Jews? For we saw his star at its rising and have come to bow down before him."\f + \fr 2:2 \ft Greek \tl proskyneō\tl*, to go down on the face before someone. It is what a subject does in front of a king and what a worshipper does in front of God, and this book uses it both ways without ever sorting them out.\f*
\v 3 When Herod the king heard this he was shaken, and all Jerusalem with him \add [i.e. the whole establishment of the city, which had learned to live with the king it had]\add*;
\v 4 and gathering together all the chief priests and scribes of the people, he kept asking them where the Christ was to be born.
\v 5 And they said to him, "In Bethlehem of Judea, for this is what has been written through the prophet:
\v 6 'And you, Bethlehem, land of Judah, are by no means least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'"\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Micah 5:2, with the last line brought in from 2 Samuel 5:2, where the elders of Israel tell David, "you will shepherd my people Israel." Micah had called Bethlehem too small to be counted among the clans of Judah; quoted here it is turned inside out — by no means least, because of who comes out of it. Note what the scholars do with the answer. They know it, they can find it, and not one of them goes.\f*
\v 7 Then Herod secretly called the magi and found out from them the exact time the star had appeared,
\v 8 and he sent them to Bethlehem and said, "Go and search carefully for the child, and when you have found him bring word back to me, so that I too may come and bow down before him."
\v 9 When they had heard the king they went; and look — the star they had seen at its rising went ahead of them until it came and stood over the place where the child was.
\v 10 And when they saw the star they rejoiced with very great joy.
\v 11 And going into the house they saw the child with Mary his mother, and they fell down and bowed before him; and opening their treasure chests they brought him gifts — gold and frankincense and myrrh.\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Gold and frankincense are what Isaiah says the nations will carry in when they come streaming to the light (Isaiah 60:6), and tribute brought to the son of David by foreign kings is what Psalm 72:10-11, 15 describes. The nations are doing here what the prophets said they would do.\f*
\v 12 And having been warned in a dream not to go back to Herod, they withdrew to their own country by another road.
\s1 The Escape into Egypt
\p
\v 13 When they had withdrawn, look — an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, saying, "Get up, take the child and his mother and flee into Egypt, and stay there until I tell you; for Herod is about to search for the child to destroy him."
\v 14 So he got up and took the child and his mother by night and withdrew into Egypt,
\v 15 and he was there until the death of Herod, so that what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled: "Out of Egypt I called my son."\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Hosea 11:1, where "my son" is Israel and nothing else: "when Israel was a child I loved him, and out of Egypt I called my son." God had called the nation his son from the start — "Israel is my firstborn son" (Exodus 4:22). Nothing in Hosea is a prediction about an individual, and the claim being made here is larger than prediction. This child goes down into Egypt and is called out of it because he is living Israel's story over again in his own person.\f*
\v 16 Then Herod, seeing that he had been outwitted by the magi, was enraged, and he sent and did away with all the boys in Bethlehem and in all its districts, from two years old and under, according to the time he had found out exactly from the magi \add [i.e. as Pharaoh had once ordered the Hebrew boys killed, and as the one who mattered had escaped him too]\add*.
\v 17 Then what was spoken through Jeremiah the prophet was fulfilled:
\v 18 "A voice was heard in Ramah, weeping and great mourning — Rachel weeping for her children, and she would not be comforted, because they are no more."\f + \fr 2:18 \ft Jeremiah 31:15. Rachel was buried near Bethlehem (Genesis 35:19), and Ramah was where the Babylonians penned the captives before marching them off (Jeremiah 40:1) — so the line is the sound of the exile itself. The quotation stops at the weeping. The next two lines in Jeremiah do not: "Hold back your voice from weeping and your eyes from tears... they will come back from the land of the enemy. There is hope for your future" (Jeremiah 31:16-17).\f*
\s1 The Return, and the Settling in Nazareth
\p
\v 19 When Herod had died, look — an angel of the Lord appeared in a dream to Joseph in Egypt,
\v 20 saying, "Get up, take the child and his mother and go into the land of Israel, for those who were seeking the child's life have died."\f + \fr 2:20 \ft Word for word what God says to Moses in Midian: "Go back to Egypt, for all those who were seeking your life have died" (Exodus 4:19). The sentence is quoted so exactly that the reader is meant to see Moses standing behind the child.\f*
\v 21 So he got up and took the child and his mother and went into the land of Israel.
\v 22 But hearing that Archelaus was reigning over Judea in place of his father Herod \add [i.e. the son who had inherited his father's cruelty along with his throne]\add*, he was afraid to go there; and having been warned in a dream he withdrew to the region of Galilee,
\v 23 and he came and settled in a town called Nazareth, so that what was spoken through the prophets might be fulfilled: "He will be called a Nazarene."\f + \fr 2:23 \ft No prophet says this, and the formula here is plural — "the prophets" — which points to a theme rather than a text. The likeliest thread is Isaiah 11:1, where the shoot that comes up out of the cut stump of Jesse is a \tl netzer\tl*, a word that sounds like Nazareth. A cut stump is what the house of David had become; a live shoot out of it is what was promised. And Nazareth was a village of no account, named in no writing anywhere before this one.\f*
\c 3
\s1 John in the Wilderness
\p
\v 1 In those days John the Immerser\f + \fr 3:1 \ft Greek \tl baptizō\tl*, to dip or plunge under. Washing by immersion was ordinary in Jewish practice — for ritual uncleanness, and for a Gentile being brought into Israel. What was not ordinary was doing it to Israelites. Calling the covenant people out to a river and putting them under treats them as though they were standing outside and had to come back in.\f* came, proclaiming in the wilderness of Judea
\v 2 and saying, "Turn back\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Greek \tl metanoia\tl*, rendered here by the Hebrew idea underneath it, \tl shub\tl* — to turn around and go back the way you came. It is what the prophets demanded of Israel (Hosea 14:1; Joel 2:12-13). Not a feeling of regret; a change of direction.\f*, for the kingdom of the heavens\f + \fr 3:2 \ft "The heavens" stands in for God — this writer's habitual way of not pronouncing the name. His readers heard "the kingdom of God," which is what he calls it outright at 12:28 and 19:24. It is not a place and not a territory. It is God reigning: God taking up his rule in the open and setting the world right, which is the thing Israel had been waiting for (Psalm 145:11-13; Daniel 2:44; Isaiah 52:7).\f* has come near."
\v 3 For this is the one spoken of through Isaiah the prophet, who said, "A voice of one shouting in the wilderness: 'Get the way of the Lord ready, make his paths straight.'"\f + \fr 3:3 \ft Isaiah 40:3, the opening of the great announcement that the exile is over and the LORD himself is coming home to his people along a road driven straight through the desert. Hung on John, it makes him the voice — and it makes the one whose road he is clearing the LORD.\f*
\v 4 Now John himself had his clothing of camel's hair and a leather belt around his loins, and his food was locusts and wild honey.\f + \fr 3:4 \ft This is a costume with a name on it. "A hairy man, with a leather belt around his loins" is how Elijah is identified in 2 Kings 1:8, and Malachi had promised that Elijah would be sent before the great and terrible day of the LORD (Malachi 4:5). Nobody needs to be told who this is; you see him and you know. Jesus says it outright at 11:14 and again at 17:12.\f*
\v 5 Then Jerusalem and all Judea and all the country around the Jordan were going out to him,
\v 6 and they were being immersed by him in the Jordan river \add [i.e. the very water Israel crossed to enter the land]\add*, confessing their sins out loud.
\s1 What John Says to the Pharisees and Sadducees
\p
\v 7 But when he saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees \add [i.e. the strict party of the law and the priestly aristocracy — the two sets of men with the most reason to think themselves safe]\add* coming to the immersion, he said to them, "Brood of vipers, who warned you to flee from the wrath that is coming?
\v 8 Produce fruit, then, that fits turning back \add [i.e. show me the change; do not tell me about it]\add*,
\v 9 and do not presume to say among yourselves, 'We have Abraham as our father.' \add [i.e. as though descent settled the matter]\add* For I tell you that God is able to raise up children for Abraham out of these stones.\f + \fr 3:9 \ft In Hebrew and Aramaic the two words are nearly the same sound: sons, \tl banim\tl*; stones, \tl abanim\tl*. He is standing in a riverbed full of them.\f*
\v 10 Already the axe is laid at the root of the trees \add [i.e. not raised to strike — already resting against the trunk]\add*; so every tree that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
\v 11 I immerse you in water for turning back, but the one coming after me is stronger than I, whose sandals I am not fit to carry.\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Handling a man's sandals was the one job so low that a Hebrew slave could not be made to do it; it was left to a Gentile slave. John has just put himself under that.\f* He will immerse you in the Holy Spirit and fire.
\v 12 His winnowing fork is in his hand, and he will clear his threshing floor and gather his wheat into the barn, but the chaff he will burn up with fire that is not put out."\f + \fr 3:12 \ft A threshing floor does not improve the grain; it separates what is already wheat from what is already chaff, and the wind does the sorting. Read back into the line before it, the Spirit and the fire are two outcomes for two groups, not one experience for everyone. The image is out of the prophets, and there it is aimed at Israel as often as at the nations (Isaiah 41:15-16; Jeremiah 15:7). "Not put out" means what it says: nothing stops the fire before it has finished what was thrown into it.\f*
\s1 Jesus Is Immersed
\p
\v 13 Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan, to John, to be immersed by him.
\v 14 But John tried to stop him, saying, "I am the one who needs to be immersed by you, and you come to me?"
\v 15 But Jesus answered and said to him, "Let it be so now, for it is fitting for us in this way to fulfill all righteousness."\f + \fr 3:15 \ft "Righteousness" in this book is not an inward condition but doing what God requires — it is what the hungry hunger for (5:6), what must go further than the scribes' (5:20), what is to be sought first (6:33), and the road John himself came in (21:32). And "fulfill" is the same word used a dozen times of the prophets being filled full. He is not confessing sin here; he is taking his place inside what God is doing, and the "us" puts John in it too.\f* Then he let him.
\v 16 And when Jesus had been immersed he came up immediately from the water; and look — the heavens were opened, and he saw the Spirit of God coming down like a dove and coming upon him \add [i.e. the anointing itself — this is where he is made in fact what the first line of this book calls him]\add*.
\v 17 And look — a voice out of the heavens saying, "This is my Son, the beloved, in whom I have taken delight."\f + \fr 3:17 \ft Two scriptures welded into one sentence. "You are my Son" is Psalm 2:7, spoken over the king of David's line at his crowning. "In whom I have taken delight" is Isaiah 42:1, the opening of the servant songs — and that servant's road runs through suffering (Isaiah 52:13-53:12). The voice names him king and servant in the same breath. Note also that it does not say "you are"; it says "this is." It is an announcement, made to whoever is standing there.\f*
\c 4
\s1 The Testing in the Wilderness
\p
\v 1 Then Jesus was led up into the wilderness by the Spirit to be tested\f + \fr 4:1 \ft Greek \tl peirazō\tl*, which runs both directions. God "tested" Israel in the wilderness to see whether they would walk in his instructions (Exodus 15:25; 16:4), and Israel "tested" God at Massah by demanding that he prove himself (Deuteronomy 6:16). Both senses are in play here, and the second one becomes the whole point of the second test.\f* by the devil.
\v 2 And after fasting forty days and forty nights \add [i.e. as Israel was forty years in the wilderness, and Moses forty days on the mountain]\add*, he was hungry.
\v 3 And the tester came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God \add [i.e. since you are — he is not doubting it, he is trading on it]\add*, tell these stones to become loaves of bread."
\v 4 But he answered and said, "It has been written: 'Man shall not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes out through the mouth of God.'"\f + \fr 4:4 \ft Deuteronomy 8:3, and the sentence before it in Deuteronomy is the key: God "let you be hungry, and fed you with manna... so that he might make you know that man does not live on bread alone." The hunger was the lesson. All three of his answers come out of the same few chapters of Deuteronomy (6-8), the section that goes back over Israel's wilderness testing and explains where they went wrong. He is fighting from inside the text that records the failure.\f*
\v 5 Then the devil took him along into the holy city and stood him on the pinnacle of the temple \add [i.e. its highest point, over a sheer drop into the valley]\add*
\v 6 and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down; for it has been written: 'He will give his angels orders about you,' and 'On their hands they will lift you up, so that you never strike your foot against a stone.'"\f + \fr 4:6 \ft Psalm 91:11-12, quoted accurately. The tester has picked up Jesus' own weapon. But the psalm promises protection to a man going about God's business in danger; it does not invite him to manufacture the danger in order to see whether the promise holds.\f*
\v 7 Jesus said to him, "Again it has been written: 'You shall not put the Lord your God to the test.'"\f + \fr 4:7 \ft Deuteronomy 6:16, which finishes, "as you tested him at Massah." At Massah, Israel had no water and turned their need into a demand for proof — "is the LORD among us or not?" (Exodus 17:1-7). He does not throw the tester's scripture out; he sets a second scripture beside it that governs how the first is to be taken.\f*
\v 8 Again the devil took him along to a very high mountain and showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory,
\v 9 and he said to him, "All these I will give you, if you will fall down and bow before me."\f + \fr 4:9 \ft He is being offered exactly what the Father has already promised him. Psalm 2, which the voice at the river had just quoted over him, runs on: "Ask of me, and I will give you the nations as your inheritance, and the ends of the earth as your possession" (Psalm 2:8). The nations are his either way. What is on offer is a shortcut, and the price on it is the one thing Israel was never to do.\f*
\v 10 Then Jesus said to him, "Get away, Satan!\f + \fr 4:10 \ft Hebrew \tl satan\tl*, the adversary — the one who stands against you, originally a courtroom word for the accuser.\f* For it has been written: 'You shall bow down to the Lord your God, and him only shall you serve.'"
\v 11 Then the devil left him, and look — angels came and were serving him \add [i.e. the very protection he had been dared to demand, given now that he has not demanded it]\add*.
\s1 Galilee, and the Light on the Road by the Sea
\p
\v 12 When he heard that John had been handed over, he withdrew into Galilee.
\v 13 And leaving Nazareth he came and settled in Capernaum by the sea, in the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali,
\v 14 so that what was spoken through Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled:
\v 15 "Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, the road by the sea, across the Jordan, Galilee of the nations —
\v 16 the people sitting in darkness have seen a great light, and on those sitting in the country and shadow of death a light has dawned."\f + \fr 4:16 \ft Isaiah 9:1-2. Zebulun and Naphtali were the northernmost tribes and the first to be swallowed — Assyria took them in 732 BC, a generation before the rest (2 Kings 15:29). They were first into the dark, and Isaiah says they will be first to see the light. A few lines later he says what the light is: "to us a child is born... and the government will be on his shoulder," on David's throne, for ever (Isaiah 9:6-7).\f*
\v 17 From then on\f + \fr 4:17 \ft Greek \tl apo tote\tl*, and this writer uses it only twice in the whole book, both times to turn the story on its hinge. Here the public proclamation of the kingdom begins. At 16:21 — "from then on Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go away to Jerusalem and suffer" — the road to the cross begins.\f* Jesus began to proclaim and to say, "Turn back, for the kingdom of the heavens has come near."
\s1 Four Fishermen
\p
\v 18 As he was walking beside the Sea of Galilee he saw two brothers, Simon called Peter and Andrew his brother, throwing a casting net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
\v 19 And he said to them, "Come after me, and I will make you fishers of men."\f + \fr 4:19 \ft The image comes out of the prophets with an edge on it: God sends for "many fishers" and they catch his people and haul them in (Jeremiah 16:16; compare Amos 4:2). It is the language of a great gathering-up, handed to two men who catch fish for a living.\f*
\v 20 And immediately they left the nets and followed him.
\v 21 And going on from there he saw two other brothers, James the son of Zebedee and John his brother, in the boat with Zebedee their father, mending their nets; and he called them.
\v 22 And immediately they left the boat and their father and followed him \add [i.e. the first two left their tools; these two left the business and the family with it]\add*.
\s1 Word of Him Spreads
\p
\v 23 And he went around the whole of Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and proclaiming the good news of the kingdom\f + \fr 4:23 \ft Greek \tl euangelion\tl*, good news. In the Greek Old Testament it is what a runner carries back from a battlefield — the war is won, the king is coming home: "how beautiful on the mountains are the feet of the one bringing good news... saying to Zion, your God reigns" (Isaiah 52:7). In the Roman world it was the word for an imperial bulletin. Either way it announces a reign.\f* and healing every disease and every sickness among the people.
\v 24 And the report of him went out into all Syria \add [i.e. Gentile country, north and outside Israel altogether]\add*, and they brought him all who were badly off, gripped by all kinds of diseases and torments, demon-possessed and moonstruck\f + \fr 4:24 \ft Greek \tl selēniazomai\tl*, moon-struck: fits that were thought to come and go with the phases of the moon. At 17:15 the same word describes a boy who falls into fire and into water.\f* and paralyzed; and he healed them.
\v 25 And large crowds followed him from Galilee and the Decapolis \add [i.e. the ten Greek cities east of the lake]\add* and Jerusalem and Judea and from across the Jordan.
\c 5
\s1 He Goes Up the Mountain and Sits Down
\p
\v 1 When he saw the crowds he went up on the mountain, and when he had sat down \add [i.e. the posture a teacher takes when he means to be heard as an authority]\add* his disciples came to him.
\v 2 And he opened his mouth and taught them, saying:\f + \fr 5:2 \ft A mountain, and a man going up it to deliver instruction, is Sinai. But Moses went up to receive; this one goes up to give, and what he gives he gives on his own say-so. The crowds catch it at the end of the discourse (7:29).\f*
\s1 Who Is Already in God's Favor
\p
\v 3 "Blessed\f + \fr 5:3 \ft Greek \tl makarios\tl*, translating Hebrew \tl ashre\tl*, the word that opens the Psalms — "blessed is the man who does not walk in the counsel of the wicked" (Psalm 1:1). It does not describe a feeling and it is not a wish. It is a verdict on where a person actually stands. What follows is not a list of virtues to work up; it is an announcement of who, now that the kingdom is arriving, turns out to be standing in the right place.\f* are the poor in spirit\f + \fr 5:3 \ft The \tl anawim\tl* of the Psalms and the prophets — people with no resources and no leverage, who therefore lean on God for everything. Isaiah's Spirit-anointed herald is sent to bring good news to exactly these (Isaiah 61:1). "In spirit" says it is the posture that counts, not the bank balance: they know they have nothing to bring.\f*, for theirs is the kingdom of the heavens \add [i.e. theirs now, not one day]\add*.
\v 4 Blessed are those who mourn \add [i.e. who grieve over what has become of God's people]\add*, for they will be comforted.\f + \fr 5:4 \ft Isaiah 61:2-3, the next lines of the same passage: to comfort all who mourn, and to give them "a garland instead of ashes, the oil of gladness instead of mourning." The mourning there is Israel's, over ruin and exile.\f*
\v 5 Blessed are the gentle, for they will inherit the land.\f + \fr 5:5 \ft Psalm 37:11 word for word. The whole psalm is about men who take by force and prosper at it, and it insists they will be cut off while those who wait on the LORD end up holding the ground. The Greek word can mean the land of Israel or the earth entire, and nothing here settles which.\f*
\v 6 Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness \add [i.e. who ache to see things set right]\add*, for they will be filled \add [i.e. fed until they can hold no more]\add*.
\v 7 Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy.
\v 8 Blessed are the clean in heart, for they will see God.\f + \fr 5:8 \ft "Who will go up the hill of the LORD, and who will stand in his holy place? The one with clean hands and a pure heart" (Psalm 24:3-4). A clean heart is an undivided one — no second agenda running underneath.\f*
\v 9 Blessed are the peacemakers \add [i.e. who do what God himself does]\add*, for they will be called sons of God.
\v 10 Blessed are those who have been persecuted for the sake of righteousness, for theirs is the kingdom of the heavens.
\v 11 Blessed are you when they insult you and persecute you and say every kind of evil against you, lying, for my sake \add [i.e. and notice that he has just put being persecuted for his sake and being persecuted for righteousness' sake in the same place]\add*.
\v 12 Rejoice and be glad, because your reward is great in the heavens; for that is how they persecuted the prophets who were before you \add [i.e. you stand in their line, and you may expect their treatment]\add*.
\s1 Salt and Light
\p
\v 13 You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt goes flat\f + \fr 5:13 \ft The Greek verb is built on the word for a fool — salt that has gone stupid. It turns up again for the man who builds on sand (7:26) and the young women with no oil (25:2-3). Salt in that world was what kept food from rotting; salt that has lost what makes it salt is not weak salt, it is gravel, and there is no way back.\f*, with what will it be salted? It is no longer good for anything except to be thrown outside and trampled by men.
\v 14 You are the light of the world.\f + \fr 5:14 \ft "A light for the nations" is the servant's job in Isaiah 42:6 and 49:6. He hands it to them.\f* A city set on a hill cannot be hidden.
\v 15 Nor do men light a lamp and put it under the measuring basket, but on the lampstand, and it gives light to everyone in the house.
\v 16 In the same way let your light shine in front of men, so that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father in the heavens \add [i.e. the point is not that they see you; it is that they see him]\add*.
\s1 Not to Tear Down but to Fill Full
\p
\v 17 Do not think that I came to tear down the Torah\f + \fr 5:17 \ft Hebrew \tl torah\tl*, which means instruction or teaching, not "law" in the sense of a statute book. "The Torah and the Prophets" is a way of saying the scriptures entire.\f* or the Prophets. I did not come to tear down but to fill full.\f + \fr 5:17 \ft The same word this book has used a dozen times of prophecy being filled full (1:22; 2:15; 4:14). Not to cancel and not merely to keep, but to bring the thing to the fullness it was always driving at. Everything from here to 5:48 shows what that looks like in practice, and none of it makes the commands smaller.\f*
\v 18 For Amen\f + \fr 5:18 \ft Hebrew \tl amen\tl*, "it stands." Ordinarily a man says it to endorse somebody else's words, at the end. He puts it in front of his own, which no one did.\f*, I say to you, until the sky and the earth pass away, not one yod, not one stroke of a letter \add [i.e. the smallest letter in the Hebrew alphabet, and the tick of ink that tells one letter from another]\add*, will pass away from the Torah until everything has happened.
\v 19 So whoever unties one of the least of these commands and teaches men so will be called least in the kingdom of the heavens; but whoever does them and teaches them will be called great in the kingdom of the heavens.
\v 20 For I say to you that unless your righteousness goes far beyond that of the scribes and Pharisees \add [i.e. the professional interpreters of the Torah and the men most serious in the land about keeping it]\add*, you will never go into the kingdom of the heavens.
\s1 Murder, and the Anger Underneath It
\p
\v 21 You have heard that it was said to the ancients \add [i.e. to the generation that stood at Sinai]\add*: 'You shall not murder,' and 'Whoever murders will be liable to the judgment.'
\v 22 But I say to you that everyone who is angry with his brother will be liable to the judgment; and whoever says to his brother, 'Raca,'\f + \fr 5:22 \ft An Aramaic insult, roughly "empty head."\f* will be liable to the council; and whoever says, 'Fool,' will be liable to the Gehenna of fire.\f + \fr 5:22 \ft Greek \tl geenna\tl*, from Hebrew \tl ge-hinnom\tl*, the Valley of Hinnom on the south side of Jerusalem. Children were burned there in sacrifice to Molech (2 Kings 23:10), and Jeremiah said God would rename it the Valley of Slaughter and fill it with corpses (Jeremiah 7:31-32). By this period the name had become shorthand for the place of final destruction. It is a real valley you could walk to, and that is part of the force of it.\f*
\v 23 So if you are bringing your gift to the altar and there remember that your brother has something against you,
\v 24 leave your gift there in front of the altar and go — first be reconciled to your brother, and then come and bring your gift \add [i.e. the offering waits; the man does not]\add*.
\v 25 Come to terms quickly with the one who has a case against you, while you are still on the road with him, or he may hand you over to the judge, and the judge to the officer, and you be thrown into prison.
\v 26 Amen, I say to you, you will never come out of there until you have paid back the last quadrans.\f + \fr 5:26 \ft The smallest coin in circulation, worth a quarter of an \tl assarion\tl* — a few minutes of a labourer's work.\f*
\s1 Adultery, and the Look That Comes Before It
\p
\v 27 You have heard that it was said: 'You shall not commit adultery.'
\v 28 But I say to you that everyone who looks at a woman in order to want her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.\f + \fr 5:28 \ft This is not a new commandment bolted on. The tenth of the ten already forbade wanting your neighbour's wife (Exodus 20:17); he is only saying out loud what that one always meant. In this book the heart is where the will sits, not the feelings.\f*
\v 29 If your right eye trips you up, tear it out and throw it away from you; for it is better for you that one of your parts be destroyed than that your whole body be thrown into Gehenna.
\v 30 And if your right hand trips you up, cut it off and throw it away from you; for it is better for you that one of your parts be destroyed than that your whole body go off into Gehenna \add [i.e. said the way a man says it when he wants you to feel the size of the stakes, not to reach for a knife]\add*.
\s1 Divorce
\p
\v 31 It was also said: 'Whoever divorces his wife, let him give her a certificate of divorce.'\f + \fr 5:31 \ft Deuteronomy 24:1. The certificate was a protection for the woman: it proved she was free and could remarry. He is not attacking the provision; he is asking what a man has to have done to reach for it. He comes back to this at 19:3-9.\f*
\v 32 But I say to you that everyone who divorces his wife, except on the ground of sexual immorality\f + \fr 5:32 \ft Greek \tl porneia\tl*, a wide word covering sexual wrongdoing of various kinds, not one specific act.\f*, makes her an adulteress; and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery.
\s1 Oaths
\p
\v 33 Again you have heard that it was said to the ancients: 'You shall not swear falsely, but you shall pay to the Lord what you have sworn.'\f + \fr 5:33 \ft Leviticus 19:12 and Numbers 30:2. A vow made to God was a debt owed to him.\f*
\v 34 But I say to you: do not swear at all — neither by heaven, for it is God's throne,
\v 35 nor by the earth, for it is the footstool of his feet, nor toward Jerusalem, for it is the city of the great King.
\v 36 Nor shall you swear by your own head, for you cannot make one hair white or black \add [i.e. you are staking what is not yours; not one thing you swear by belongs to you]\add*.
\v 37 Let your word be 'Yes, yes' or 'No, no'; anything beyond these comes from evil \add [i.e. an oath is only needed where a man's plain word is not trusted]\add*.
\s1 Paying Back
\p
\v 38 You have heard that it was said: 'An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth.'\f + \fr 5:38 \ft Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21. In the Torah this is a ceiling, not a licence — it was written to stop a feud escalating, by forbidding a man to take more than he lost.\f*
\v 39 But I say to you: do not stand up against the one who is evil; but whoever slaps you on your right cheek, turn the other to him as well.\f + \fr 5:39 \ft A right-handed man striking your right cheek is hitting you with the back of his hand, and the backhand was not a blow between equals. It was how a master corrected a slave, a Roman a subject, a patron a client — the injury was the insult, and later Jewish law fined it at double the rate of an open-handed blow for exactly that reason. Turn the left cheek and he cannot repeat it: to reach that side he must use a fist or an open palm, which are what men use on their equals. You have not hit back, you have not run, and you have made it impossible for him to put you back in your place without treating you as his equal.\f*
\v 40 And to the one who wants to take you to court and get your tunic, let him have your cloak as well.\f + \fr 5:40 \ft This is a courtroom, and the man being sued has nothing left but his clothes — the Torah assumed that a cloak taken in pledge had to be handed back by sundown, because a poor man slept in it (Exodus 22:26-27; Deuteronomy 24:10-13). Hand over the cloak too and you are standing naked in front of the court. In Jewish thinking the shame of nakedness falls on the man who caused it, not on the man exposed (Genesis 9:20-27; Habakkuk 2:15). The creditor walked in holding all the power and walks out the one everybody is looking at.\f*
\v 41 And whoever presses you into service for one mile, go with him two.\f + \fr 5:41 \ft Greek \tl angareuō\tl*, the technical word for a soldier's legal right to make a civilian carry his pack — and the right was limited to a set distance, with discipline for soldiers who abused it. For that mile the soldier gives orders and you are a tool. Walk into the second mile and he is the one exposed: if an officer sees it, he is the one who looks to have broken the rule. He may end up asking you to put the pack down.\f*
\v 42 Give to the one who asks you, and do not turn away from the one who wants to borrow from you.\f + \fr 5:42 \ft In that world a loan or a gift was rarely neutral; it bound the man who received it to the man who gave. "The borrower is slave to the lender" (Proverbs 22:7). Give without reckoning what comes back and the mechanism that turns generosity into a hold over somebody has been taken apart.\f*
\s1 Love of Enemies
\p
\v 43 You have heard that it was said: 'You shall love your neighbor and hate your enemy.'\f + \fr 5:43 \ft The first half is Leviticus 19:18. The second half is in no scripture at all; it is the inference people had drawn from it. The community at Qumran had drawn it in writing — its rule book orders members to love all the sons of light and hate all the sons of darkness.\f*
\v 44 But I say to you: love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you,
\v 45 so that you may become sons of your Father in the heavens; for he makes his sun rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous \add [i.e. he does not sort the field before he waters it]\add*.
\v 46 For if you love those who love you, what reward do you have? Do not even the tax collectors\f + \fr 5:46 \ft Men who bought the right to collect Rome's revenue from their own people and made their living on the margin. They were counted collaborators and thieves at once.\f* do the same?
\v 47 And if you greet only your brothers, what are you doing that is more than others? Do not even the nations do the same?
\v 48 So you shall be complete, as your heavenly Father is complete.\f + \fr 5:48 \ft Greek \tl teleios\tl*, from \tl telos\tl*, an end or goal: finished, entire, having arrived at what it was for. Not flawless. Coming where it does — straight after the sun and the rain falling on everyone — it means let your love be as undivided as his, not stopping at the edge of the people who love you back.\f*
\c 6
\s1 Doing Your Righteousness Where Only God Is Watching
\p
\v 1 Be careful not to do your righteousness in front of men in order to be watched by them; otherwise you have no reward with your Father in the heavens.\f + \fr 6:1 \ft He has just told them to let their light shine in front of men so that men see it (5:16). The difference is entirely in the last clause of each sentence: so that they glorify your Father, or so that they watch you. The verb here is the one behind the word "theatre."\f*
\v 2 So when you give to the poor, do not sound a trumpet ahead of you, as the hypocrites\f + \fr 6:2 \ft Greek \tl hypokritēs\tl*, a stage actor — a man speaking his lines from behind a mask. It is not a word for a liar. It is a word for a performer, and it is this book's standing name for the men who do religion for an audience.\f* do in the synagogues and in the streets so that they may be praised by men. Amen, I say to you, they have their reward paid in full.\f + \fr 6:2 \ft Greek \tl apechō\tl*, the word written across the bottom of a receipt: paid in full, nothing further owed. They wanted to be admired and they have been admired. The account is closed.\f*
\v 3 But when you give to the poor, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing,
\v 4 so that your giving may be in secret; and your Father who sees in secret will pay you back.
\p
\v 5 And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to stand and pray in the synagogues and on the street corners so that they may be seen by men. Amen, I say to you, they have their reward paid in full.
\v 6 But when you pray, go into your storeroom \add [i.e. the back room, the one place in the house with a door that shuts]\add* and shut your door and pray to your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees in secret will pay you back.
\v 7 And when you pray, do not pile up empty words\f + \fr 6:7 \ft One Greek word, and it sounds like what it means — \tl battalogeō\tl*, to babble, to stack up syllables. Pagan prayer worked by accumulation: get the god's attention with enough names, enough repetitions, the right formula.\f* as the nations do, for they think they will be heard for their many words.
\v 8 So do not be like them, for your Father knows what you need before you ask him \add [i.e. so prayer is not for informing him, and not for wearing him down]\add*.
\s1 How to Pray
\p
\v 9 So pray like this: 'Our Father\f + \fr 6:9 \ft Not "my." Whoever prays this is praying as one of a people. Israel did address God as Father — "you, LORD, are our Father" (Isaiah 63:16; 64:8) — but as the ordinary opening of ordinary prayer this is something new.\f* in the heavens, let your name be kept holy;\f + \fr 6:9 \ft A passive, and God is the one who does it: "I will make my great name holy, which has been profaned among the nations" (Ezekiel 36:23). In Ezekiel he does it by acting in history so that everyone can see what he is like. That is what is being asked for.\f*
\v 10 let your kingdom come; let your will be done, as in heaven so on earth \add [i.e. let what is already true where God is uncontested become true down here, where it is not]\add*.
\v 11 Give us today our bread for the day;\f + \fr 6:11 \ft The Greek word \tl epiousios\tl* is not found anywhere else in surviving Greek before this; it means something like "for the coming day" or "enough to live on." Either way it asks for today's ration and no more. It is the manna arrangement (Exodus 16), where enough came each morning and anything hoarded overnight bred worms.\f*
\v 12 and forgive us our debts\f + \fr 6:12 \ft Sin as a debt owed to God was ordinary Jewish speech, and this writer keeps the accounting language right through — see the parable at 18:23-35, where the same word carries the whole story.\f*, as we too have forgiven our debtors;
\v 13 and do not bring us into the testing, but rescue us from evil.'\f + \fr 6:13 \ft Some later copies close the prayer here with "For yours is the kingdom and the power and the glory forever, Amen" — which the earliest copies do not have. It is old all the same: the Didache, a manual of church practice from within living memory of the apostles, already carries a shorter form of it, and it follows the shape of Jewish prayer, which does not end on a bare request. The words are David's, at the assembling of the gifts for the temple: "Yours, O LORD, is the greatness and the power and the glory" (1 Chronicles 29:11).\f*
\v 14 For if you forgive men their false steps, your heavenly Father will forgive you too;
\v 15 but if you do not forgive men, neither will your Father forgive your false steps.\f + \fr 6:15 \ft Of the six petitions this is the only one he stops to explain, and the explanation has no third option in it. He returns to it once more, at length, at 18:35.\f*
\p
\v 16 And when you fast, do not be gloomy-faced like the hypocrites, for they disfigure their faces so that they may show men they are fasting. Amen, I say to you, they have their reward paid in full.
\v 17 But when you fast, put oil on your head and wash your face \add [i.e. groom yourself exactly as you would on any other day]\add*,
\v 18 so that you may not show men you are fasting, but your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees in secret will pay you back.
\s1 Where Your Treasure Is
\p
\v 19 Do not store up treasures for yourselves on the earth, where moth and worm destroy and where thieves dig through \add [i.e. straight through the mud-brick wall of a house]\add* and steal;
\v 20 but store up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor worm destroys and where thieves do not dig through or steal.
\v 21 For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.\f + \fr 6:21 \ft Note the order. He does not say the heart goes first and the money follows it. The money goes first and the heart follows the money. Which makes it a test you can run on yourself.\f*
\p
\v 22 The lamp of the body is the eye. So if your eye is generous, your whole body will be full of light;
\v 23 but if your eye is grudging, your whole body will be dark. So if the light in you is darkness — how great that darkness is!\f + \fr 6:23 \ft "A good eye" and "an evil eye" are Hebrew idiom for open-handed and tight-fisted (Deuteronomy 15:9; Proverbs 22:9). The Greek word behind "generous" also means single, undivided — one eye looking one way. Set as it is between the treasure and the two masters, the saying is about money and what the sight of it does to a man. If the organ you see by has gone bad, you have no other.\f*
\p
\v 24 No one can be a slave to two masters; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will hold to the one and think nothing of the other. You cannot be a slave to God and to Mammon.\f + \fr 6:24 \ft Aramaic \tl mamon\tl*, property, wealth, what a man has put by. Set beside God as the other master, it stops being a thing and becomes a rival lord with claims of his own. A slave was owned outright; the impossibility here is not that serving two is hard but that being owned by two is not a thing that exists.\f*
\s1 Do Not Be Anxious
\p
\v 25 For this reason I say to you: do not be anxious\f + \fr 6:25 \ft The Greek verb is not about planning or providing; it means to be pulled apart, the mind split off in pieces onto competing worries.\f* about your life, what you will eat or what you will drink, or about your body, what you will put on. Is not the life more than food, and the body more than clothing \add [i.e. and if he gave you the greater, will he stop short at the lesser]\add*?
\v 26 Look at the birds of the sky: they do not sow or reap or gather into barns \add [i.e. not one stage of the farming year]\add*, and your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not worth more than they?
\v 27 And which of you by being anxious can add one cubit to his span?\f + \fr 6:27 \ft A cubit is about eighteen inches, an odd unit for a lifetime and a natural one for height; the Greek will carry either. The point holds both ways — worry does not work.\f*
\v 28 And why are you anxious about clothing? Learn from the lilies of the field, how they grow: they do not work themselves weary or spin;
\v 29 yet I say to you that not even Solomon in all his glory was dressed like one of these \add [i.e. the richest man Israel ever had, outdressed by a weed]\add*.
\v 30 And if God so clothes the grass of the field, which is here today and thrown into the oven tomorrow, will he not much more clothe you — you of little trust?\f + \fr 6:30 \ft One word in Greek, \tl oligopistos\tl*, "little-trust," and it is this writer's own coinage; he uses it of the disciples in the boat (8:26), of Peter sinking (14:31), and twice more (16:8; 17:20). It never means unbelief. It means the real thing, in a quantity too small for what is happening.\f*
\v 31 So do not be anxious, saying, 'What shall we eat?' or 'What shall we drink?' or 'What shall we wear?'
\v 32 For the nations chase after all these things, and your heavenly Father knows that you need them all.
\v 33 But seek first the kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be added to you \add [i.e. not renounced — added, and by him]\add*.
\v 34 So do not be anxious about tomorrow, for tomorrow will be anxious about itself. The day's own trouble is enough for the day.
\c 7
\s1 Judging, and Being Judged by the Same Measure
\p
\v 1 Do not judge \add [i.e. do not set yourself up to pass sentence on a man]\add*, so that you are not judged.
\v 2 For with the judgment you judge you will be judged, and with the measure you measure it will be measured out to you.\f + \fr 7:2 \ft A saying already in circulation; the rabbis put it as "by the measure with which a man measures, it is measured to him" (Mishnah, Sotah 1:7). "It will be measured to you" is a way of saying God will measure it, without saying God. Appoint yourself judge and you have chosen the standard you will be tried under.\f*
\v 3 Why do you look at the speck in your brother's eye \add [i.e. a splinter, a bit of chaff]\add* but not notice the beam in your own eye \add [i.e. a roof timber]\add*?
\v 4 Or how will you say to your brother, 'Let me take the speck out of your eye,' and look — the beam is in your own eye?
\v 5 Hypocrite, first take the beam out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the speck out of your brother's eye.\f + \fr 7:5 \ft It does not end by forbidding you to help him. It ends with "and then." The order is what is being insisted on, not the silence.\f*
\p
\v 6 Do not give what is holy to dogs, and do not throw your pearls in front of pigs, or they will trample them with their feet, and turn and tear you to pieces.\f + \fr 7:6 \ft Dogs and pigs are both unclean, and "what is holy" is a phrase for meat set apart for sacrifice — you do not throw consecrated meat to the scavengers in the street. Placed where it is, the saying is the other side of the coin from what has just been said: do not appoint yourself judge over a brother, and do not hand out what is precious with no judgment at all.\f*
\s1 Ask, and It Will Be Given
\p
\v 7 Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened to you \add [i.e. keep asking, keep seeking, keep knocking]\add*.
\v 8 For everyone who asks receives, and the one who seeks finds, and to the one who knocks it will be opened.
\v 9 Or what man is there among you whose son will ask him for bread — will he give him a stone?
\v 10 Or he will ask for a fish — will he give him a snake?\f + \fr 7:10 \ft A round loaf and a stone are the same shape; so, near enough, are a fish and a snake. The picture is not of a father who refuses but of one who mocks — who hands over a thing that looks like the thing asked for and is useless or dangerous.\f*
\v 11 So if you, being evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your Father in the heavens give good things to those who ask him!
\v 12 So in everything, whatever you want men to do for you, do the same for them; for this is the Torah and the Prophets.\f + \fr 7:12 \ft Hillel, asked to give the whole Torah while a man stood on one foot, had answered: "what is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour; that is the whole Torah, the rest is commentary" (Talmud, Shabbat 31a). The form here is not the negative one. Not doing harm is a low bar; doing for another what you would want done is not. And with this line the frame closes: at 5:17 he said he came to fill the Torah and the Prophets full, and here is what they come to.\f*
\s1 The Narrow Gate
\p
\v 13 Go in through the narrow gate; for wide is the gate and broad is the road that leads away to destruction, and many are those going in through it.
\v 14 How narrow is the gate and hard pressed the road that leads away to life, and few are those who find it.\f + \fr 7:14 \ft "Hard pressed" is built on the word for pressure and affliction — the same root as the word used for what the followers of Jesus are put under (13:21; 24:9). The road is not merely narrow; it squeezes. And it has to be found: the wide gate needs no looking for, which is what makes it the wide gate.\f*
\s1 Known by Their Fruit
\p
\v 15 Watch out for the false prophets, who come to you in sheep's clothing but inside are ravaging wolves.\f + \fr 7:15 \ft Wolves in the flock is how the prophets described Israel's own leaders: "her officials in her midst are wolves tearing the prey" (Ezekiel 22:27; compare Zephaniah 3:3). The trouble with these ones is that they are inside, dressed as sheep, talking the language of the flock.\f*
\v 16 By their fruits you will recognize them. Do men gather grapes from thorn bushes, or figs from thistles?
\v 17 In the same way every good tree produces good fruit, but the rotten tree produces bad fruit.
\v 18 A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a rotten tree bear good fruit.
\v 19 Every tree that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire \add [i.e. the same sentence John had already passed at the river]\add*.
\v 20 So then, by their fruits you will recognize them.\f + \fr 7:20 \ft The test is not doctrine and it is not accuracy of prediction; it is what comes out of the man's life over time. That is why it works on a disguise. A costume can be kept up; a crop cannot be faked.\f*
\s1 "Lord, Lord" Is Not Enough
\p
\v 21 Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,'\f + \fr 7:21 \ft Greek \tl kyrios\tl*, and in the Greek Old Testament that is the word that stands where the divine name stands. Doubling it makes it more earnest, not less. These are not half-hearted people, and he is saying it is still not enough.\f* will go into the kingdom of the heavens, but the one who does the will of my Father in the heavens.
\v 22 Many will say to me on that day\f + \fr 7:22 \ft "That day" is the prophets' phrase for the day of the LORD, the day of decision (Isaiah 2:11, 17; Amos 8:9; Joel 2:31). Note who is sitting in the seat on it.\f*, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name, and in your name throw out demons, and in your name do many acts of power?'
\v 23 And then I will declare to them, 'I never knew you. Get away from me, you who work lawlessness.'\f + \fr 7:23 \ft "Knew" in the Hebrew sense: not knew about, but was bound to — "you only have I known of all the families of the earth" (Amos 3:2). And "get away from me, you who work lawlessness" is Psalm 6:8. Their résumé may be entirely true. Prophecy in his name, exorcism in his name, power in his name — and none of it is the thing being asked for, which is doing what the Father wants.\f*
\s1 Two Builders
\p
\v 24 So everyone who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a sensible man who built his house on the rock.
\v 25 And the rain came down and the rivers came and the winds blew and fell against that house, and it did not fall, for it had been founded on the rock.
\v 26 And everyone who hears these words of mine and does not do them will be like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
\v 27 And the rain came down and the rivers came and the winds blew and struck against that house, and it fell — and its fall was great."\f + \fr 7:27 \ft Both men heard the same words. Both got the same storm. Nothing separates them except what they did with what they heard, and until the weather came you could not have told the houses apart.\f*
\p
\v 28 And it happened, when Jesus had finished these words\f + \fr 7:28 \ft This sentence is a joint in the book. It comes back at 11:1, 13:53, 19:1 and 26:1, and each time it closes off one of five great blocks of teaching.\f*, that the crowds were astounded at his teaching,
\v 29 for he was teaching them as one who has authority, and not as their scribes \add [i.e. a scribe taught by citing which teacher had said what before him; this man cited no one]\add*.
\c 8
\s1 A Man No One Would Touch
\p
\v 1 When he had come down from the mountain, large crowds followed him.
\v 2 And look — a leper\f + \fr 8:2 \ft The Greek word covers a range of skin conditions, not only the disease we now call leprosy. Under the Torah such a man was ritually unclean: he lived outside the camp, went about with torn clothes and covered lip, and called out "unclean, unclean" as he came (Leviticus 13:45-46). He was cut off from the sanctuary, from the community, and from being touched. His coming this close is already a breach.\f* came and bowed down before him, saying, "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean." \add [i.e. I have no doubt you are able; what I do not know is whether you would want to touch me]\add*
\v 3 And stretching out his hand he touched him, saying, "I am willing; be clean." And immediately his leprosy was cleansed.\f + \fr 8:3 \ft He touches first and speaks second. Under the purity system, touching an unclean man makes the toucher unclean; here it runs the other way, and it is cleanness that moves.\f*
\v 4 And Jesus said to him, "See that you tell no one, but go, show yourself to the priest and bring the gift Moses laid down, as a witness to them."\f + \fr 8:4 \ft Leviticus 14 lays out the whole procedure — the priest's inspection, the two birds, the blood and the oil, the offerings over several days. It was the only way back into the community, and only a priest could certify it. He does not set the Torah aside; he sends the man into it, so that the priests themselves will have to sign off on what has happened.\f*
\s1 A Roman Officer Who Understands Orders
\p
\v 5 When he had gone into Capernaum, a centurion \add [i.e. a Roman officer over a hundred men — a Gentile, and one of the occupiers]\add* came to him and pleaded with him,
\v 6 saying, "Lord, my boy is lying at home paralyzed, terribly tormented."
\v 7 And he said to him, "I will come and heal him."
\v 8 But the centurion answered and said, "Lord, I am not worthy to have you come in under my roof \add [i.e. and I know what walking into a Gentile's house would cost you]\add*; but only say the word, and my boy will be healed.
\v 9 For I too am a man set under authority, with soldiers under me; and I say to this one, 'Go,' and he goes, and to another, 'Come,' and he comes, and to my slave, 'Do this,' and he does it."\f + \fr 8:9 \ft This is the whole of his argument, and it is a soldier's argument. Authority does not have to be present to work. An order carries because of what stands behind it, and distance is nothing to it. He has understood what Jesus' word is, from the inside of a chain of command.\f*
\v 10 When Jesus heard this he was amazed, and he said to those following, "Amen, I say to you, with no one in Israel have I found such trust \add [i.e. and Israel is where such trust was supposed to be]\add*.
\v 11 And I say to you that many will come from east and west and will recline at table with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of the heavens,\f + \fr 8:11 \ft The prophets had promised a feast on God's mountain for all peoples, where death itself would be swallowed up (Isaiah 25:6-8), and a gathering of the redeemed "from east and west" (Psalm 107:3). The men coming from east and west are Gentiles, like the one standing in front of him.\f*
\v 12 but the sons of the kingdom will be thrown out into the darkness outside; there will be weeping and grinding of teeth."\f + \fr 8:12 \ft "Sons of the kingdom" means the ones whose place at that table was never in question — it was theirs by birth. This is John's warning at the river worked out in a story (3:9). Inside is light, a table, and the fathers; outside is dark. Grinding the teeth is not only pain, it is fury: the rage of finding yourself on the wrong side of a door. The phrase recurs at 13:42, 13:50, 22:13, 24:51 and 25:30.\f*
\v 13 And Jesus said to the centurion, "Go; let it happen for you as you have trusted." And the boy was healed in that hour.
\s1 Healings at Evening
\p
\v 14 And when Jesus came into Peter's house he saw his mother-in-law laid out and burning with fever.
\v 15 And he touched her hand, and the fever left her; and she got up and served him \add [i.e. on her feet and waiting at the table, which is the measure of how completely it was gone]\add*.
\v 16 When evening came they brought him many who were demon-possessed, and he threw out the spirits with a word and healed all who were badly off,
\v 17 so that what was spoken through Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled: "He himself took our weaknesses and carried our diseases."\f + \fr 8:17 \ft Isaiah 53:4, out of the fourth servant song, and rendered here straight from the Hebrew rather than from the Greek version, which had already turned it toward sin-bearing. Read as it stands, the servant's work includes this: he takes the sickness up and carries it off. That is what the reader has just watched happen all evening.\f*
\s1 What It Costs to Follow
\p
\v 18 When Jesus saw a crowd around him he gave orders to go away to the other side.
\v 19 And a scribe came and said to him, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
\v 20 And Jesus said to him, "The foxes have dens and the birds of the sky have nests, but the son of man has nowhere to lay his head."\f + \fr 8:20 \ft The first time this book uses the phrase, and it will use it more than thirty times. "Son of man" does two jobs in Israel's scriptures. In Ezekiel it is simply how God addresses the prophet — mortal, creature, man. In Daniel 7:13-14 it is a figure who comes on the clouds to the Ancient of Days and is handed dominion, glory and a kingdom that will never end. Both are live here, and this first use takes the low road: the one to whom that kingdom belongs has nowhere to sleep, and the animals do better.\f*
\v 21 And another of the disciples said to him, "Lord, let me first go away and bury my father."
\v 22 But Jesus said to him, "Follow me, and leave the dead to bury their own dead."\f + \fr 8:22 \ft Burying your father was the most binding obligation a Jewish son had; a man occupied with it was released from other commands while he did it. When Elijah called Elisha, Elisha asked to go and kiss his parents goodbye, and Elijah let him (1 Kings 19:19-21). This is a heavier demand than Elijah made, and the only claim in Israel's scriptures that outranks burying a father is God's own.\f*
\s1 The Storm on the Lake
\p
\v 23 And when he had gotten into the boat, his disciples followed him.
\v 24 And look — a great shaking came over the sea,\f + \fr 8:24 \ft The word is \tl seismos\tl*, an earthquake — not the ordinary word for a squall. This writer uses it for the shakings that mark God's own arrival and judgment: at the end of the age (24:7), at the cross (27:54), and at the empty tomb (28:2).\f* so that the boat was being covered by the waves; but he was sleeping.
\v 25 And they came and woke him, saying, "Lord, rescue us — we are perishing!"
\v 26 And he said to them, "Why are you cowardly, you of little trust?" Then he got up and spoke harshly to the winds and the sea, and there was a great calm.\f + \fr 8:26 \ft "Spoke harshly" is the verb used for silencing a demon (17:18) — he addresses the weather as something under his jurisdiction. And in Israel's scriptures the stilling of the sea belongs to one person: "you rule the raging of the sea; when its waves rise, you still them" (Psalm 89:9); "he made the storm be still, and the waves of the sea were hushed" (Psalm 107:23-30); "here shall your proud waves be stopped" (Job 38:11).\f*
\v 27 And the men were amazed, saying, "What sort of man is this, that even the winds and the sea obey him?" \add [i.e. and they have no category to put him in]\add*
\s1 The Two Men Among the Tombs
\p
\v 28 And when he had come to the other side, to the region of the Gadarenes \add [i.e. Gentile country, across the lake]\add*, two demon-possessed men met him, coming out of the tombs \add [i.e. living among the dead, which made them unclean on top of everything else]\add*, so violent that no one was strong enough to pass by along that road.
\v 29 And look — they screamed out, "What is there between us and you, Son of God? Did you come here to torture us before the time?"\f + \fr 8:29 \ft "What is there between us and you" is a stock protest against unwanted interference (Judges 11:12; 1 Kings 17:18; 2 Kings 3:13). Watch what they know. The men in the boat have just asked what sort of man this is; the demons need no time at all. And "before the time" concedes the whole case — they do not dispute that a day of reckoning is set for them, only that it has not come yet.\f*
\v 30 Now far off from them a herd of many pigs was feeding \add [i.e. which no Jewish district would have kept]\add*.
\v 31 And the demons begged him, saying, "If you throw us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
\v 32 And he said to them, "Go." And they came out and went off into the pigs; and look — the whole herd rushed down the steep bank into the sea and died in the waters \add [i.e. what they had been doing to two men, done at speed to two thousand animals]\add*.
\v 33 And those tending them fled, and going into the city they reported everything, and what had happened to the demon-possessed men.
\v 34 And look — the whole city came out to meet Jesus, and when they saw him they begged him to move on from their district.\f + \fr 8:34 \ft Everyone in this chapter asks him for something. Make me clean. Heal my boy. Save us. Send us into the pigs. This city asks him to leave. They do not deny what he did; they have priced it, and they would rather have the pigs.\f*
\c 9
\s1 A Paralyzed Man, and the Right to Forgive
\p
\v 1 And getting into a boat he crossed over and came to his own city.
\v 2 And look — they were bringing him a paralyzed man lying on a bed. And when Jesus saw their trust \add [i.e. the trust of the men carrying him]\add* he said to the paralyzed man, "Take heart, child; your sins are forgiven."
\v 3 And look — some of the scribes said among themselves, "This man is speaking blasphemy."\f + \fr 9:3 \ft Their reasoning goes without saying, because every Jew in the room shared it: sins are against God, so only God can cancel them. "I, I am he who blots out your transgressions for my own sake" (Isaiah 43:25). They have heard him do something they believe no man may do.\f*
\v 4 And Jesus, seeing their thoughts, said, "Why are you thinking evil things in your hearts?
\v 5 For which is easier — to say, 'Your sins are forgiven,' or to say, 'Get up and walk'? \add [i.e. the first can never be checked; the second is settled in front of you within the minute]\add*
\v 6 But so that you may know that the son of man has authority on the earth to forgive sins" — then he said to the paralyzed man, "Get up, pick up your bed and go to your house."
\v 7 And he got up and went off to his house.
\v 8 When the crowds saw it they were afraid, and they gave glory to God, who had given such authority to men.
\s1 Eating with Tax Collectors and Sinners
\p
\v 9 And as Jesus was passing on from there he saw a man sitting at the tax booth, called Matthew, and he said to him, "Follow me." And he got up and followed him.\f + \fr 9:9 \ft Every list of the twelve names Matthew. Only this book's list adds what he had been: "Matthew the tax collector" (10:3).\f*
\v 10 And it happened, as he was reclining at table in the house — look, many tax collectors and sinners came and were reclining with Jesus and his disciples.\f + \fr 9:10 \ft "Sinners" was not a word for people who were imperfect; everyone was that. It named people whose way of life put them plainly outside the Torah's boundaries. And eating with a man was not a casual thing — to share a table was to accept him, to be counted with him. That is what is being objected to.\f*
\v 11 And when the Pharisees saw it they said to his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with the tax collectors and sinners?"
\v 12 But he heard and said, "Those who are strong have no need of a doctor, but those who are badly off.
\v 13 Go and learn what this means: 'I want mercy and not sacrifice.' For I did not come to call righteous men, but sinners."\f + \fr 9:13 \ft Hosea 6:6. "Go and learn" was a teacher's formula for sending an opponent back to a text he ought to have known — it stings. The word behind "mercy" is Hebrew \tl chesed\tl*, the loyal, covenant-keeping love God asks for and rarely gets. Hosea's point was that God will take that over ritual any day, and these men are objecting on ritual grounds. He quotes the same verse against them again at 12:7.\f*
\s1 Why His Disciples Do Not Fast
\p
\v 14 Then the disciples of John came to him, saying, "Why do we and the Pharisees fast much, but your disciples do not fast?"
\v 15 And Jesus said to them, "Can the sons of the wedding hall mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But days will come when the bridegroom is taken away from them, and then they will fast.\f + \fr 9:15 \ft In the prophets the bridegroom of Israel is God: "I will betroth you to me for ever" (Hosea 2:19-20); "your Maker is your husband" (Isaiah 54:5); "as a bridegroom rejoices over the bride, so will your God rejoice over you" (Isaiah 62:5). He puts himself in that place, and in the same breath drops the first shadow across the story: the verb for "taken away" is abrupt, the way a thing is snatched.\f*
\v 16 No one puts a patch of unshrunk cloth on an old garment, for the patch pulls away from the garment and the tear becomes worse \add [i.e. new cloth shrinks when it is washed, and takes the old cloth with it]\add*.
\v 17 Nor do men put new wine into old wineskins; otherwise the skins burst and the wine spills out and the skins are ruined. But they put new wine into fresh wineskins, and both are kept safe."\f + \fr 9:17 \ft New wine is still working, still swelling. An old skin has already been stretched once and has no give left. He is not saying the old skins were bad skins; he is saying what has now started cannot be poured into them without wrecking both.\f*
\s1 A Dead Girl, and a Woman Who Touches His Cloak
\p
\v 18 While he was saying these things to them — look, one of the rulers came and bowed down before him, saying, "My daughter has just now come to her end; but come and lay your hand on her, and she will live." \add [i.e. a man of rank on the ground in front of him, asking not for healing but for a reversal of death]\add*
\v 19 And Jesus got up and followed him, and so did his disciples.
\v 20 And look — a woman who had been bleeding for twelve years came up behind him and touched the fringe of his garment;\f + \fr 9:20 \ft Twelve years of a discharge of blood meant twelve years of ritual uncleanness under Leviticus 15:25-27 — everything she touched unclean, everyone who touched her unclean until evening, no temple, no ordinary contact with anybody. She comes from behind and takes hold of the tassel, the \tl tsitsit\tl* that every observant man wore on the corner of his cloak as a reminder of the commandments (Numbers 15:38-39). She reaches for the most Torah-keeping thing on him, and she reaches for it without asking, because asking would have meant admitting what she was.\f*
\v 21 for she was saying to herself, "If I only touch his garment I will be rescued."
\v 22 And Jesus turned, and seeing her he said, "Take heart, daughter; your trust has rescued you." And the woman was made well from that hour \add [i.e. and he calls her daughter, in front of everyone, which puts her back inside the family]\add*.
\v 23 And when Jesus came into the ruler's house and saw the flute players and the crowd making an uproar,\f + \fr 9:23 \ft Mourning was hired. Even the poorest household in Israel was expected to provide at least two flute players and a wailing woman at a funeral. Their being there is the proof that the girl is dead and the burial has begun.\f*
\v 24 he said, "Move back, for the girl did not die but is sleeping." And they laughed at him \add [i.e. they were professionals; they knew a corpse when they saw one]\add*.
\v 25 But when the crowd had been put outside he went in and took hold of her hand, and the girl got up.
\v 26 And the report of this went out into all that land.
\s1 Two Blind Men
\p
\v 27 And as Jesus was passing on from there, two blind men followed him, screaming out, "Have mercy on us, son of David!"\f + \fr 9:27 \ft The first time in this book that anyone says it out loud. "Son of David" was not a compliment about ancestry; it was the title of the king Israel was waiting for, the heir of the promise that David's house would hold the throne for ever (2 Samuel 7:12-16). Two blind beggars are the first to use it.\f*
\v 28 And when he had come into the house the blind men came to him, and Jesus said to them, "Do you trust that I am able to do this?" They said to him, "Yes, Lord."
\v 29 Then he touched their eyes, saying, "Let it happen to you according to your trust."
\v 30 And their eyes were opened. And Jesus spoke to them harshly, saying, "See that no one knows."
\v 31 But they went out and spread word of him through all that land.
\p
\v 32 As they were going out — look, they brought him a mute man who was demon-possessed.
\v 33 And when the demon had been thrown out, the mute man spoke; and the crowds were amazed, saying, "Never has anything like this been seen in Israel."
\v 34 But the Pharisees said, "He throws out the demons by the ruler of the demons."\f + \fr 9:34 \ft The same act, two verdicts, set side by side with no comment. The crowds measure him against Israel's whole history — Moses, Elijah, Elisha — and say nothing like it has ever been seen. The Pharisees say the power is real and the source is the devil. This is the first statement of a charge that will get its full hearing at 12:22-32.\f*
\s1 Sheep with No Shepherd
\p
\v 35 And Jesus went around all the cities and the villages, teaching in their synagogues and proclaiming the good news of the kingdom and healing every disease and every sickness.\f + \fr 9:35 \ft Almost word for word what was said at 4:23. The two sentences are a pair of brackets, and everything between them — the teaching on the mountain and the works that followed it — is what they enclose.\f*
\v 36 And when he saw the crowds his heart went out to them, because they were torn open and thrown down like sheep that have no shepherd.\f + \fr 9:36 \ft "Sheep with no shepherd" is Moses' phrase, said when he asked God to appoint someone to lead Israel after him (Numbers 27:17) — and the man appointed was Joshua, whose name is the same name as Jesus. It is also Ezekiel's charge sheet against Israel's leaders, who fed themselves and not the flock: "the weak you have not strengthened, the sick you have not healed... the lost you have not sought." God's answer there was to say, "I myself will search for my sheep... and I will set up over them one shepherd, my servant David" (Ezekiel 34:4, 11, 23). Two blind men have just called this one the son of David.\f*
\v 37 Then he said to his disciples, "The harvest is large, but the workers are few.
\v 38 So beg the Lord of the harvest to thrust workers out into his harvest."\f + \fr 9:38 \ft "Thrust out" is the same violent verb used for throwing a demon out of a man. Nobody strolls into this field. And they are told to pray for workers in this verse; in the next one they are the workers.\f*
\c 10
\s1 Twelve Men Are Named and Given His Authority
\p
\v 1 And calling his twelve disciples\f + \fr 10:1 \ft The number is the argument. Israel was twelve tribes from twelve sons, and to gather twelve men around himself is to say what is being rebuilt. The count was held to so tightly that after Judas the first thing done was to make it twelve again (Acts 1:15-26).\f* to himself he gave them authority over unclean spirits, to throw them out and to heal every disease and every sickness \add [i.e. the same list, word for word, of what he has been doing himself]\add*.
\v 2 Now the names of the twelve sent ones\f + \fr 10:2 \ft Greek \tl apostolos\tl*, one sent. Behind it is the Jewish \tl shaliach\tl*, a man's authorised agent, of whom it was said that "a man's agent is as the man himself." He does not act on his own authority; he carries the sender's. That principle is what verse 40 is built on.\f* are these: first Simon, called Peter, and Andrew his brother; and James the son of Zebedee and John his brother;
\v 3 Philip and Bartholomew; Thomas and Matthew the tax collector \add [i.e. and no other list of the twelve keeps that against his name]\add*; James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus;
\v 4 Simon the Cananaean\f + \fr 10:4 \ft From the Aramaic for "zealous" — a nationalist, of the temper that wanted Rome out by force. He is standing in the same group of twelve as a man who had collected Rome's taxes.\f* and Judas Iscariot, the one who handed him over \add [i.e. named as such here, twenty chapters before he does it]\add*.
\s1 Where to Go, What to Take, and How to Leave
\p
\v 5 These twelve Jesus sent out, ordering them: "Do not go off on a road of the nations, and do not enter a city of the Samaritans \add [i.e. the mixed people of the old northern kingdom, who worshipped on their own mountain and not in Jerusalem]\add*;
\v 6 but go instead to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.\f + \fr 10:6 \ft "My people have been lost sheep; their shepherds have led them astray" (Jeremiah 50:6). And behind that, Ezekiel 34, where the shepherds feed themselves and the flock scatters, and God says he will go looking for them himself. This is the errand he saw a chapter ago and wept over (9:36).\f*
\v 7 And as you go, proclaim, saying, 'The kingdom of the heavens has come near.' \add [i.e. the identical sentence John shouted at the river and he began with in Galilee]\add*
\v 8 Heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse lepers, throw out demons. You received without payment; give without payment.
\v 9 Do not get gold or silver or copper for your belts,
\v 10 no bag for the road, no second tunic, no sandals, no staff; for the worker is worth his food \add [i.e. go out with nothing and let the people you serve keep you — which is also how nobody can mistake you for a man selling something]\add*.
\v 11 And whatever city or village you enter, find out who in it is worthy \add [i.e. who will have you]\add*, and stay there until you go on \add [i.e. and do not shop around for a better house]\add*.
\v 12 And as you go into the house, greet it;
\v 13 and if the house is worthy, let your peace come on it, but if it is not worthy, let your peace return to you.\f + \fr 10:13 \ft The greeting is \tl shalom\tl*, and it is treated here as a solid thing that can be handed over, taken up, or handed back. A house that receives the messengers gets what they carry. A house that will not is left without it.\f*
\v 14 And whoever does not welcome you or listen to your words — as you go out of that house or that city, shake the dust off your feet.\f + \fr 10:14 \ft That was what a Jew did on coming home from Gentile country: you did not carry pagan soil back into the land. Done on the way out of an Israelite town, the gesture reclassifies it.\f*
\v 15 Amen, I say to you, it will be more bearable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah on the day of judgment than for that city.\f + \fr 10:15 \ft Sodom and Gomorrah are the standing example in Israel's memory of what God's judgment looks like when it falls (Genesis 19). Sodom never had messengers of the kingdom stand in its street. These towns have. That is the whole comparison.\f*
\s1 Sheep in the Middle of Wolves
\p
\v 16 Look — I am sending you out as sheep in the middle of wolves \add [i.e. and he has just given them power over demons and disease, and still this is the arrangement]\add*. So be shrewd as snakes and innocent as doves.\f + \fr 10:16 \ft Two things at once, and the tension is the instruction. The snake is the creature the Torah calls shrewd from its first appearance (Genesis 3:1); the dove is the bird that came down at the river (3:16). Be clever enough to survive them without picking up their methods.\f*
\v 17 And watch out for men, for they will hand you over to councils and flog you in their synagogues,\f + \fr 10:17 \ft Local Jewish courts, and the flogging is the community's own discipline — the thirty-nine lashes of Deuteronomy 25:3. The first persecution named here is not Roman. It comes from inside, from their own assemblies.\f*
\v 18 and you will be brought before governors and kings for my sake, as a witness to them and to the nations \add [i.e. so the dock becomes the platform]\add*.
\v 19 But when they hand you over, do not be anxious about how or what you are to say, for what you are to say will be given to you in that hour;
\v 20 for it is not you speaking, but the Spirit of your Father speaking in you.
\v 21 And brother will hand over brother to death, and a father his child, and children will rise up against parents and put them to death.
\v 22 And you will be hated by everyone because of my name; but the one who endures to the end — this one will be rescued.\f + \fr 10:22 \ft "Endures" is the word for staying under a weight without going out from under it. Not a single decision but a condition kept up. He does not say how far off the end is, and he says the same thing again at 24:13.\f*
\v 23 And when they persecute you in this city, flee to the next; for Amen, I say to you, you will not have finished the cities of Israel before the son of man comes \add [i.e. get out and go on — the job is urgent and there is not time to make a stand]\add*.
\p
\v 24 A disciple is not above the teacher, nor a slave above his master.
\v 25 It is enough for the disciple that he become like his teacher, and the slave like his master. If they called the master of the house Beelzebul, how much more the members of his household!\f + \fr 10:25 \ft Beelzebul, from a Hebrew name meaning something like "lord of the high place" — compare Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron in 2 Kings 1:2. It was a name for the chief of the demons, and it is the charge the Pharisees have just laid against him (9:34). He calls himself the master of the house and them his household: what is thrown at him lands on them.\f*
\s1 Whom to Be Afraid Of
\p
\v 26 So do not be afraid of them; for there is nothing covered that will not be uncovered, and nothing hidden that will not be made known.
\v 27 What I tell you in the dark, say in the light; and what you hear whispered in your ear, proclaim from the rooftops \add [i.e. from the flat roof where the whole street can hear you]\add*.
\v 28 And do not be afraid of those who kill the body but cannot kill the life;\f + \fr 10:28 \ft The word is \tl psychē\tl*, and behind it the Hebrew \tl nephesh\tl* — not a soul in the Greek philosophers' sense, a deathless part sealed inside a body, but the life, the self, the whole living person. What is being said is that a persecutor's reach stops at the body. There is a limit on him. There is no limit on God.\f* but rather be afraid of the one who is able to destroy both life and body in Gehenna.
\v 29 Are not two sparrows sold for an assarion?\f + \fr 10:29 \ft A small copper coin, a sixteenth of a day's wage. Two birds for that: this is the cheapest life on the market.\f* And not one of them will fall to the ground apart from your Father.
\v 30 But even the hairs of your head are all numbered \add [i.e. counted, and the count kept]\add*.
\v 31 So do not be afraid; you are worth more than many sparrows.
\v 32 So everyone who will say openly before men that he is mine, I too will say before my Father in the heavens that he is mine;
\v 33 but whoever denies me before men, I too will deny him before my Father in the heavens.\f + \fr 10:33 \ft The verb is the one used of Peter in the courtyard (26:70-75). Two courts are in view — the human one where the words are said, and the Father's, where they are answered.\f*
\s1 Not Peace but a Sword
\p
\v 34 Do not think that I came to throw peace on the earth; I did not come to throw peace but a sword.\f + \fr 10:34 \ft Not a weapon in his followers' hands — he will order one put away at 26:52. The sword is what a sword does: it divides, and it cuts through things that were joined.\f*
\v 35 For I came to divide a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a bride against her mother-in-law;
\v 36 and a man's enemies will be the members of his own household.\f + \fr 10:36 \ft Micah 7:6, quoted almost exactly. In Micah this is the description of a society that has rotted through, where no one can be trusted, not even at home. Here it is not decay that splits the household. It is him.\f*
\v 37 The one who loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me, and the one who loves son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me;
\v 38 and whoever does not take his cross and follow after me is not worthy of me.\f + \fr 10:38 \ft The first mention of a cross in this book, and it is not a symbol of anything yet. It is a Roman gallows, kept mainly for slaves and rebels, and a condemned man carried the crossbeam of it to the place where he would be nailed to it. Everyone hearing this had seen it done. He is describing a walk to an execution, and telling them to make it their own.\f*
\v 39 The one who finds his life will lose it, and the one who loses his life for my sake will find it.
\s1 Whoever Welcomes You
\p
\v 40 The one who welcomes you welcomes me, and the one who welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me \add [i.e. the chain runs all the way up, and it runs both ways]\add*.
\v 41 The one who welcomes a prophet because he is a prophet will get a prophet's reward, and the one who welcomes a righteous man because he is a righteous man will get a righteous man's reward.
\v 42 And whoever gives one of these little ones even a cup of cold water to drink because he is a disciple — Amen, I say to you, he will never lose his reward."\f + \fr 10:42 \ft "These little ones" is his name for the men he has just sent out with no money and no bag. The smallest possible act, done to the least impressive possible person, for the one reason that he belongs to Jesus — and that is what is guaranteed.\f*
\c 11
\p
\v 1 And it happened, when Jesus had finished giving orders to his twelve disciples, that he moved on from there to teach and to proclaim in their cities \add [i.e. the same cities he will name and condemn before this chapter is out]\add*.
\s1 John Sends from Prison to Ask
\p
\v 2 Now when John heard in the prison about the works of the Christ, he sent word through his disciples
\v 3 and said to him, "Are you the one who is coming, or should we look for another?"\f + \fr 11:3 \ft John is not being difficult. Look at what he had announced at the river: an axe already at the root, a winnowing fork in the hand, chaff burnt with fire that cannot be put out (3:10-12). What has reached him in his cell is that this man heals people and eats with tax collectors. The question is honest, and it is the question of a man whose expectation and the evidence will not line up.\f*
\v 4 And Jesus answered and said to them, "Go and report to John what you hear and see:
\v 5 blind men see again and lame men walk, lepers are cleansed and deaf men hear, dead men are raised and poor men have good news brought to them.\f + \fr 11:5 \ft He gives no title and no yes. He gives a list, and the list is stitched out of Isaiah: "the eyes of the blind will be opened and the ears of the deaf unstopped, then the lame man will leap like a deer" (Isaiah 35:5-6); "out of their gloom and darkness the eyes of the blind will see" (Isaiah 29:18); and, last and heaviest, "he has anointed me to bring good news to the poor" (Isaiah 61:1). These are the marks Isaiah said would come with the age of restoration. Go and tell John what is happening, and let Isaiah tell him what it means.\f*
\v 6 And blessed is whoever is not tripped up over me."\f + \fr 11:6 \ft The Greek word is built on the trigger-stick of a trap — the thing you catch your foot on that brings the whole snare down. He is conceding John's difficulty and answering it in the same breath: I am not what you were braced for, and there is a blessing on the man who can let the evidence correct him.\f*
\s1 What He Says About John
\p
\v 7 As these men were going away, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John: "What did you go out into the wilderness to look at? A reed shaken by the wind?\f + \fr 11:7 \ft A reed bending to whatever is blowing is a picture of a man with no spine. There may be a second edge on it: Herod Antipas, the man holding John in a cell, had put a reed on his coinage.\f*
\v 8 But what did you go out to see? A man dressed in soft clothes? Look — those who wear soft clothes are in the houses of kings \add [i.e. and John is in a king's house at this moment, in the cells under it]\add*.
\v 9 But what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
\v 10 This is the one about whom it has been written: 'Look, I am sending my messenger before your face, who will build your road ahead of you.'\f + \fr 11:10 \ft Malachi 3:1, with wording from Exodus 23:20 worked into it. In Malachi, God says the messenger goes before his own face — "and the Lord whom you seek will come suddenly to his temple." Here it is "before your face," said to Jesus. The one whose road John was clearing was the Lord himself.\f*
\v 11 Amen, I say to you, among those born of women no one greater than John the Immerser has risen; yet the least in the kingdom of the heavens is greater than he \add [i.e. not because John is small, but because standing on the threshold is a different thing from standing inside the door]\add*.
\v 12 And from the days of John the Immerser until now the kingdom of the heavens is under force, and men of force are seizing it.\f + \fr 11:12 \ft The Greek can be read two ways, and the translation holds both open. Either the kingdom is pressing forward violently and violent men are laying hold of it, or the kingdom is being violently set upon and violent men are trying to seize it. What surrounds the sentence points the second way: John is in a cell, this generation refuses everyone sent to it, and within a chapter the Pharisees will be planning a killing.\f*
\v 13 For all the Prophets and the Torah prophesied until John;
\v 14 and if you are willing to take it in, he is Elijah, the one who is to come.\f + \fr 11:14 \ft "Look, I am sending you Elijah the prophet before the great and terrible day of the LORD comes" (Malachi 4:5). If that is John, then the day Malachi meant is not coming — it is here. "If you are willing to take it in" is not a doubt about John. It is a doubt about them.\f*
\v 15 The one who has ears, let him hear.
\s1 A Generation That Will Not Play
\p
\v 16 But to what shall I compare this generation?\f + \fr 11:16 \ft "This generation" is not a date. In the Greek Old Testament it is what Moses calls the people who saw everything God did in the wilderness and still would not trust him — "a crooked and twisted generation" (Deuteronomy 32:5, 20; Psalm 95:10). Every time this book uses the phrase, that is the charge in it (12:39-45; 16:4; 17:17; 23:36).\f* It is like children sitting in the marketplaces who call out to the others,
\v 17 saying, 'We played the flute for you and you did not dance; we sang a lament and you did not beat your breasts.' \add [i.e. we played weddings and we played funerals, and you would not join in either]\add*
\v 18 For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, 'He has a demon.'
\v 19 The son of man came eating and drinking, and they say, 'Look — a glutton and a wine-drinker, a friend of tax collectors and sinners.'\f + \fr 11:19 \ft "A glutton and a drunkard" is not loose abuse. It is the wording of Deuteronomy 21:20, the charge parents bring against a stubborn and rebellious son before the elders of the town — and the sentence there is stoning. As for "friend of tax collectors and sinners," they mean it as filth, and it is an exact description of what he does (9:10-13).\f* And wisdom is shown to be right by her works."
\s1 Woe to the Towns That Saw Everything
\p
\v 20 Then he began to reproach the cities in which most of his acts of power had happened, because they did not turn back:
\v 21 "Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the acts of power that happened in you had happened in Tyre and Sidon, they would have turned back long ago in sackcloth and ashes.\f + \fr 11:21 \ft Chorazin and Bethsaida are small towns within an hour's walk of his base. Tyre and Sidon are the Phoenician ports on which the prophets emptied whole chapters of judgment (Isaiah 23; Ezekiel 26-28; Joel 3:4) — pagan, rich, and proverbially beyond hope. He says they would have gone into mourning, and these towns did not so much as look up.\f*
\v 22 But I say to you, it will be more bearable for Tyre and Sidon on the day of judgment than for you.
\v 23 And you, Capernaum — will you be lifted up to heaven? You will go down to Hades. For if the acts of power that happened in you had happened in Sodom, it would have remained to this day.\f + \fr 11:23 \ft "You said in your heart, I will go up to the heavens... but you are brought down to Sheol" — Isaiah's taunt over the king of Babylon at the height of his arrogance (Isaiah 14:13-15), turned now on a fishing village. Capernaum had no imperial pretensions. It had something else: it was where he lived and worked, and it saw more than anywhere else on earth. That is the whole of its exaltation and the whole of its charge.\f*
\v 24 But I say to you that it will be more bearable for the land of Sodom on the day of judgment than for you."
\s1 Hidden from the Wise
\p
\v 25 At that time Jesus answered and said, "I praise you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you hid these things from the wise and the clever and uncovered them to infants.\f + \fr 11:25 \ft The "wise and clever" are the men trained to read exactly this — the scribes and the Pharisees, who have the Torah and the Prophets at their fingertips and have concluded that he blasphemes (9:3), keeps bad company (9:11), and works for the devil (9:34). Their training has become the thing standing in the way: they know what the Messiah is supposed to look like. The "infants" are people with no such qualification — the crowds, a bleeding woman, two blind beggars — and nothing in front of their eyes.\f*
\v 26 Yes, Father, because this is what was pleasing in front of you.
\v 27 All things have been handed over to me by my Father, and no one fully knows the Son except the Father, nor does anyone fully know the Father except the Son and anyone to whom the Son wants to uncover him \add [i.e. so this is not knowledge that study reaches; it is handed over, or it is not had]\add*.
\s1 Come to Me
\p
\v 28 Come to me, all you who are worn out and loaded down, and I will give you rest.
\v 29 Take my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am gentle and lowly in heart, and you will find rest for yourselves.\f + \fr 11:29 \ft "The yoke of the Torah" and "the yoke of the kingdom" were ordinary phrases; to take a teacher's yoke was to put yourself under his reading of the law. Ben Sira had made the same invitation — "draw near to me, you who are untaught... put your neck under her yoke" (Sirach 51:23-26). And "you will find rest for yourselves" is Jeremiah 6:16: "ask for the ancient paths, where the good way is, and walk in it, and find rest for your souls" — a verse that ends, in Jeremiah, with the people answering, "we will not walk in it."\f*
\v 30 For my yoke is kind and my load is light."\f + \fr 11:30 \ft The word for the load here is the same one used at 23:4 of what the scribes and Pharisees tie up and lay on men's shoulders, and will not lift with a finger. Theirs is heavy; his is light. The question was never whether a man wears a yoke.\f*
\c 12
\s1 Grain Plucked on the Sabbath
\p
\v 1 At that time Jesus went through the grainfields on the Sabbath; and his disciples were hungry and began to pluck heads of grain and eat.
\v 2 But when the Pharisees saw it they said to him, "Look — your disciples are doing what is not permitted to do on a Sabbath."\f + \fr 12:2 \ft Nobody is accusing them of theft. The Torah expressly allows a man walking through his neighbour's standing grain to pull the ears off by hand (Deuteronomy 23:25). The whole dispute is the day. Pluck, rub between the palms, blow away the husk, and you have performed reaping, threshing and winnowing in miniature — and those were among the kinds of work the Sabbath forbade.\f*
\v 3 And he said to them, "Have you not read what David did when he was hungry, and those with him — \add [i.e. and he is asking men who have read nothing else]\add*
\v 4 how he went into the house of God and they ate the loaves of the Presence, which it was not permitted for him to eat, or for those with him, but only for the priests?\f + \fr 12:4 \ft 1 Samuel 21:1-6. The twelve loaves set out before the LORD each Sabbath were the priests' food and no one else's (Leviticus 24:5-9). David, on the run and hungry, ate them, and the record passes no sentence on him for it.\f*
\v 5 Or have you not read in the Torah that on the Sabbaths the priests in the temple treat the Sabbath as common and are not guilty?\f + \fr 12:5 \ft The Sabbath offerings are commanded in Numbers 28:9-10, and making them means slaughtering, carrying, and tending fire on the Sabbath. The Torah itself, then, contains a case where one obligation outranks the Sabbath. The argument is being made from inside the law, not against it.\f*
\v 6 But I say to you that something greater than the temple is here.\f + \fr 12:6 \ft The Greek is neuter — something, not someone. To a first-century Jew there was nothing greater than the temple; it was the place where God had put his name. He does not say what the something is. He says it is standing in this field.\f*
\v 7 And if you had known what this means — 'I want mercy and not sacrifice' — you would not have condemned the guiltless.\f + \fr 12:7 \ft Hosea 6:6 for the second time (see 9:13). Both times it is aimed at men who are defending a boundary, and both times the point is that God said which of the two he would rather have.\f*
\v 8 For the son of man is lord of the Sabbath."
\s1 A Withered Hand, and a Decision to Kill Him
\p
\v 9 And moving on from there he came into their synagogue;
\v 10 and look — there was a man there with a withered hand. And they asked him, "Is it permitted to heal on the Sabbath?" — so that they might bring a charge against him \add [i.e. the question is not a question; they are collecting evidence]\add*.
\v 11 And he said to them, "What man is there among you who has one sheep, and if it falls into a pit on the Sabbath will not take hold of it and lift it out?\f + \fr 12:11 \ft This was live ground. The rule was that a life at risk overrides the Sabbath, but a withered hand is not a life at risk — it would keep till sundown. As for the sheep, opinion was divided: the community at Qumran wrote down that you may not lift an animal out of a pit on the Sabbath. He assumes the more generous ruling and expects the room to assume it with him.\f*
\v 12 How much more valuable, then, is a man than a sheep! So it is permitted to do good on the Sabbath."
\v 13 Then he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and it was restored, sound as the other \add [i.e. and no work was done by anybody; a man was spoken to, and a man moved his arm]\add*.
\s1 He Withdraws, and Isaiah Says Why
\p
\v 14 But the Pharisees went out and took counsel against him, how they might destroy him \add [i.e. they have watched a crippled hand come back and their conclusion is that the healer must die]\add*.
\v 15 But Jesus knew it and withdrew from there. And many followed him, and he healed them all,
\v 16 and he ordered them not to make him known,
\v 17 so that what was spoken through Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled:
\v 18 "Look — my servant whom I chose, my beloved in whom my life has taken delight. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will announce judgment to the nations.
\v 19 He will not quarrel or shout, nor will anyone hear his voice in the streets.
\v 20 A bruised reed he will not break, and a smoldering wick he will not put out, until he brings judgment out to victory.
\v 21 And in his name the nations will hope."\f + \fr 12:20 \ft Isaiah 42:1-4, the first of the servant songs, and the longest thing this book quotes. It is put here to explain a retreat. The voice at the river had said "my beloved, in whom I have taken delight," and Isaiah's servant is the man that language came from. This one does not shout in the street, does not force a confrontation, and does not finish off what is nearly finished — a cracked reed is useless and he does not snap it, a wick with no flame left is only smoke and he does not pinch it out. Set that beside verse 14, where men look at a healed hand and start planning a death. And note where the quotation begins and ends: with the nations.\f*
\s1 By Whose Power He Throws Them Out
\p
\v 22 Then a demon-possessed man, blind and mute, was brought to him, and he healed him, so that the mute man spoke and saw.
\v 23 And all the crowds were beside themselves and said, "Could this be the son of David?" \add [i.e. asked the way a man asks when he expects to be told no, and cannot stop asking it]\add*
\v 24 But when the Pharisees heard it they said, "This man does not throw out the demons except by Beelzebul, the ruler of the demons."
\v 25 But knowing their thoughts he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is laid waste, and no city or house divided against itself will stand.
\v 26 And if Satan throws out Satan, he has been divided against himself; how then will his kingdom stand?
\v 27 And if I throw out the demons by Beelzebul, by whom do your sons throw them out? For this reason they will be your judges.\f + \fr 12:27 \ft Exorcism was not unique to him; there were Jewish exorcists, and some of them belonged to these men's own circles. If driving out a demon is proof of working for the devil, the proof convicts their own people first. They are running one rule for him and another for themselves.\f*
\v 28 But if I throw out the demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.\f + \fr 12:28 \ft "Has come upon you" — arrived, landed, reached you. Not near now, here. Every time a demon comes out, ground changes hands.\f*
\v 29 Or how can anyone go into the strong man's house and carry off his goods unless he first ties up the strong man? Then he will plunder his house.\f + \fr 12:29 \ft The strong man is Satan; the house is what he holds; the goods are people. "Can the prey be taken from the mighty, or the captives of a tyrant be rescued? Thus says the LORD: even the captives of the mighty will be taken, and the prey of the tyrant rescued" (Isaiah 49:24-25). The looting going on in front of them is the evidence that the owner has already been tied up.\f*
\v 30 The one who is not with me is against me, and the one who does not gather with me scatters \add [i.e. there is no third place to stand]\add*.
\s1 The One Thing That Is Not Forgiven
\p
\v 31 For this reason I say to you: every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven men, but blasphemy against the Spirit will not be forgiven.
\v 32 And whoever speaks a word against the son of man, it will be forgiven him; but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit, it will not be forgiven him — not in this age, nor in the one to come.\f + \fr 12:32 \ft Read it against what has just happened, because that is what it is about. A man may look at Jesus — an artisan's son from a village, eating with tax collectors — and fail to see anything, and be forgiven for it; his identity is not written on his face. What is being described here is different. It is standing in front of a man delivered from blindness, dumbness and a demon, knowing perfectly well what has been done, and naming it the work of the devil. That is not ignorance, it is inversion, and it is unforgivable for a reason that is not about the size of God's mercy: a man who has taught himself to call God's Spirit satanic has destroyed the one instrument by which mercy could reach him. There is nothing left in him to appeal to. "In this age and the age to come" is the standard Jewish way of saying: ever.\f*
\s1 What the Mouth Says About the Heart
\p
\v 33 Either make the tree good and its fruit good, or make the tree rotten and its fruit rotten; for the tree is known by its fruit \add [i.e. and you cannot have it both ways — good fruit off a devil's tree]\add*.
\v 34 Brood of vipers \add [i.e. John's words at the river, said again]\add*, how can you speak good things when you are evil? For out of the overflow of the heart the mouth speaks.
\v 35 The good man brings good things out of his good treasure, and the evil man brings evil things out of his evil treasure \add [i.e. the heart is a storeroom, and speech is what gets carried out of it]\add*.
\v 36 And I say to you that for every idle word men speak they will give an account of it on the day of judgment;\f + \fr 12:36 \ft An "idle" word is one thrown out without thought — and that is exactly why it counts. A prepared statement can be made to hide a man. An offhand one shows him.\f*
\v 37 for by your words you will be set right, and by your words you will be condemned."
\s1 The Sign of Jonah
\p
\v 38 Then some of the scribes and Pharisees answered him, saying, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
\v 39 But he answered and said to them, "An evil and adulterous generation looks for a sign, and no sign will be given it except the sign of Jonah the prophet.\f + \fr 12:39 \ft "Adulterous" is the prophets' word for Israel unfaithful to the God it was married to (Hosea 1-3; Jeremiah 3:6-10; Ezekiel 16). And notice what they are asking for. They have just watched a blind mute man healed and called it devilry. There is no sign that could be given to men who can do that with the evidence in front of them.\f*
\v 40 For just as Jonah was in the belly of the sea monster three days and three nights, so the son of man will be in the heart of the earth three days and three nights.\f + \fr 12:40 \ft Jonah 1:17. The fish swallowed him and did not keep him. It is the plainest word yet about how this is going to end, and it is given as a riddle, to men who asked for proof.\f*
\v 41 Men of Nineveh will stand up at the judgment with this generation and will condemn it, because they turned back at what Jonah proclaimed — and look, something greater than Jonah is here.\f + \fr 12:41 \ft Nineveh was the capital of Assyria, the empire that destroyed the northern kingdom. Pagan, brutal, and the last city on earth anyone expected to repent — and it did, at one reluctant prophet's preaching and no miracles at all (Jonah 3:5-10). The queen of the south, in the next line, is the queen of Sheba, who crossed a continent on a rumour of Solomon's wisdom (1 Kings 10:1-13). Two Gentiles, given far less, who moved.\f*
\v 42 The queen of the south will be raised at the judgment with this generation and will condemn it, because she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon — and look, something greater than Solomon is here.
\s1 The House Swept and Left Empty
\p
\v 43 When the unclean spirit goes out of a man it passes through waterless places looking for rest, and does not find it.\f + \fr 12:43 \ft Desert and ruin were where such things were thought to live (Isaiah 13:21; 34:14). The picture is of a spirit turned out of doors, houseless, going back to see whether the room is still free.\f*
\v 44 Then it says, 'I will go back to my house that I came out of.' And when it comes it finds it standing empty, swept and put in order.
\v 45 Then it goes and takes along with itself seven other spirits more evil than itself, and they go in and settle there; and the last state of that man becomes worse than the first. So it will be for this evil generation too."\f + \fr 12:45 \ft The fatal word is "empty." The room has been cleaned out and nothing has moved in. This generation has had its demons cast out, its sick healed, its house swept by everything he has been doing — and has put nothing in the place of what was removed. Being rid of an evil is not the same as being given a good, and a swept empty room invites a worse tenant than the one that left.\f*
\s1 Who His Family Is
\p
\v 46 While he was still speaking to the crowds — look, his mother and his brothers were standing outside, wanting to speak with him \add [i.e. outside, while the men he is teaching are inside]\add*.
\v 47 And someone said to him, "Look — your mother and your brothers are standing outside, wanting to speak with you."
\v 48 But he answered and said to the one telling him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
\v 49 And stretching out his hand toward his disciples he said, "Look — my mother and my brothers.
\v 50 For whoever does the will of my Father in the heavens — he is my brother and sister and mother."\f + \fr 12:50 \ft He is not cancelling the command to honour father and mother; he will use it as a club against these same opponents at 15:3-9. He is naming the one thing that decides who is inside the room. It is the test he has already given at 7:21 and will pray in the garden at 26:42, and it is open to anybody at all — including a tax collector, a Canaanite woman, and a Roman officer.\f*
\c 13
\s1 The Sower
\p
\v 1 On that day Jesus went out of the house and was sitting beside the sea;
\v 2 and large crowds gathered to him, so that he got into a boat and sat down, and the whole crowd stood on the shore \add [i.e. and the water between them is the shape of what is about to be said]\add*.
\v 3 And he spoke many things to them in parables,\f + \fr 13:3 \ft Greek \tl parabolē\tl*, standing in for the Hebrew \tl mashal\tl* — which covers a proverb, a riddle, a dark saying, a story with something buried in it. It is not a simple illustration. In the prophets it is speech that opens for some hearers and shuts for others, and that double action is the whole point of what follows.\f* saying: "Look — the sower went out to sow.
\v 4 And as he was sowing, some seed fell beside the road, and the birds came and ate it up.
\v 5 Other seed fell on the rocky places, where it did not have much soil, and immediately it sprang up because the soil was not deep;
\v 6 but when the sun rose it was scorched, and because it had no root it withered.
\v 7 Other seed fell among the thorns, and the thorns came up and choked it.
\v 8 But other seed fell on the good ground and produced fruit — this one a hundredfold, that one sixty, that one thirty.\f + \fr 13:8 \ft Nothing here is bad farming. Seed was broadcast across the whole field and ploughed in afterwards, so it went onto the footpath and the thin patches as well. "Rocky places" is not stony soil but a shelf of bedrock under a skin of earth: it warms fast, the seed comes up fast, and there is nowhere for a root to go. As for the yield, a hundredfold was the sort of harvest that got recorded as a mark of God's blessing (Genesis 26:12). Three failures and one success, and the success swallows the losses.\f*
\v 9 The one who has ears, let him hear."
\s1 Why He Speaks in Parables
\p
\v 10 And the disciples came and said to him, "Why do you speak to them in parables?"
\v 11 And he answered and said to them, "To you it has been given to know the secrets of the kingdom of the heavens, but to those it has not been given.\f + \fr 13:11 \ft "Secrets" does not mean doctrines kept back for an inner circle. It is the word used in Daniel for what God is doing that no one can work out by looking, and that has to be shown (Daniel 2:27-30). What is being handed over is the meaning of what is happening in front of everybody.\f*
\v 12 For whoever has, more will be given to him and he will have plenty; but whoever does not have, even what he has will be taken away from him \add [i.e. what a man already does with what he hears decides what more he will get of it]\add*.
\v 13 For this reason I speak to them in parables: because seeing they do not see, and hearing they do not hear or understand.\f + \fr 13:13 \ft The word is "because," not "so that." The parables are not a device for keeping people out; they are the form of speech that fits an audience already in that condition. To the ear that is open they open further. To the ear that has shut they sound like farming stories.\f*
\v 14 And in them the prophecy of Isaiah is being fulfilled, which says: 'With hearing you will hear and never understand, and looking you will look and never see.
\v 15 For the heart of this people has grown fat, and with their ears they have heard heavily, and their eyes they have shut — otherwise they might see with their eyes and hear with their ears and understand with their heart and turn back, and I would heal them.'\f + \fr 13:15 \ft Isaiah 6:9-10, the commission God gave Isaiah on the day he was called. Read the order: the heart thickens first, and the ears and eyes follow it — this book has said all along that the heart is the organ that decides. And read the verb: "their eyes they have shut." They shut them. Nobody shut them for them. And what they are avoiding is stated outright: they might see, and turn, and be healed — the healing is real and on offer, and shutting the eyes is how it is refused.\f*
\v 16 But blessed are your eyes, because they see, and your ears, because they hear.
\v 17 For Amen, I say to you, many prophets and righteous men longed to see what you see and did not see it, and to hear what you hear and did not hear it \add [i.e. men waited whole lifetimes for this and you are standing in it]\add*.
\s1 The Sower Explained
\p
\v 18 You, then, hear the parable of the sower.
\v 19 When anyone hears the word of the kingdom and does not understand it, the evil one comes and snatches away what was sown in his heart. This is the one sown beside the road.
\v 20 And the one sown on the rocky places — this is the one who hears the word and immediately takes it with joy;
\v 21 but he has no root in himself and lasts only a short time, and when pressure or persecution comes because of the word he is immediately tripped up.\f + \fr 13:21 \ft The response was real: he took it, and he took it gladly. What he did not have was depth, and the fault is located inside him — "no root in himself." The pressure named here is the pressure the twelve were warned about in chapter 10, and the people first reading this book had felt it. The pattern has a name because it was familiar: an enthusiasm that does not survive the first cost.\f*
\v 22 And the one sown among the thorns — this is the one who hears the word, and the anxiety of the age and the deceit of riches choke the word, and it becomes fruitless.\f + \fr 13:22 \ft "Anxiety" is the same word as in the sermon — do not be anxious about what you will eat (6:25-34). "The deceit of riches" says money lies: it promises a security it cannot hand over. Nothing dramatic happens to this man. Nobody persecutes him. The word is simply crowded out and slowly suffocated, and what is left is a life with no crop on it.\f*
\v 23 But the one sown on the good ground — this is the one who hears the word and understands it, who indeed bears fruit and produces, this one a hundredfold, that one sixty, that one thirty."
\s1 Wheat and Darnel
\p
\v 24 He put another parable in front of them, saying: "The kingdom of the heavens has become like a man who sowed good seed in his field.
\v 25 But while the men were sleeping his enemy came and sowed darnel in among the wheat and went away.\f + \fr 13:25 \ft Darnel is a grass that comes up looking so like wheat that until the ears form you cannot tell them apart, and it is poisonous. Sowing it in a man's field was a known way of ruining him — Roman law had to deal with the crime.\f*
\v 26 And when the blade sprouted and produced fruit, then the darnel appeared as well.
\v 27 And the slaves of the master of the house came and said to him, 'Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where then does the darnel come from?'
\v 28 And he said to them, 'A man who is an enemy did this.' And the slaves said to him, 'Do you want us, then, to go and gather them up?'
\v 29 But he said, 'No — otherwise, while gathering the darnel you may uproot the wheat along with it \add [i.e. the roots are tangled together under the ground, and you cannot pull one without the other]\add*.
\v 30 Let both grow together until the harvest, and at the time of the harvest I will say to the reapers: Gather the darnel first and tie it in bundles to burn it, but bring the wheat together into my barn.'"\f + \fr 13:30 \ft The question the parable answers is the one anybody asks who believes the kingdom has arrived: then why is the world still full of what it is full of? The answer is not that the sorting will not happen. It is that it happens at the harvest, and that the reapers are not the farmhands. Weeding is not this community's job.\f*
\s1 A Mustard Seed and a Handful of Yeast
\p
\v 31 He put another parable in front of them, saying: "The kingdom of the heavens is like a mustard seed, which a man took and sowed in his field.
\v 32 It is smaller than all the seeds, but when it has grown it is larger than the garden plants and becomes a tree, so that the birds of the sky come and nest in its branches."\f + \fr 13:32 \ft The mustard seed was the proverbial smallest thing (compare 17:20). And birds nesting in the branches is not decoration: it is how the prophets pictured a great empire giving shelter to the nations under it — God's own planting that "becomes a noble cedar, and under it will live every kind of bird" (Ezekiel 17:22-24), and the tree in Nebuchadnezzar's dream where "the birds of the heavens lived in its branches" (Daniel 4:12). At this moment the kingdom is one man, a dozen followers, and every authority in the country against him.\f*
\p
\v 33 He spoke another parable to them: "The kingdom of the heavens is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of flour until the whole of it was leavened."\f + \fr 13:33 \ft She hides it — that is the verb. Once it is in, nothing can be seen, and everything changes. Three measures is about forty pounds of flour, enough bread for a hundred people; it is also the amount Abraham told Sarah to take when the three visitors came to his tent (Genesis 18:6).\f*
\s1 He Said Nothing to Them Without a Parable
\p
\v 34 Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables, and he spoke nothing to them without a parable,
\v 35 so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled: "I will open my mouth in parables; I will pour out things hidden from the founding of the world."\f + \fr 13:35 \ft Psalm 78:2. That psalm is a long retelling of Israel's history — what God did, how the people forgot it, how he kept faith anyway — and the psalmist calls his own retelling a parable and a riddle. Israel's history was itself a thing that had to be read rightly, and had not been.\f*
\s1 The Darnel Explained
\p
\v 36 Then he left the crowds and went into the house. And his disciples came to him, saying, "Explain to us the parable of the darnel of the field."
\v 37 And he answered and said, "The one who sows the good seed is the son of man;
\v 38 the field is the world \add [i.e. not the community — the world]\add*; the good seed — these are the sons of the kingdom; the darnel are the sons of the evil one;
\v 39 the enemy who sowed them is the devil; the harvest is the completion of the age; and the reapers are angels.
\v 40 So just as the darnel is gathered and burned up with fire, so it will be at the completion of the age.
\v 41 The son of man will send out his angels, and they will gather out of his kingdom everything that trips men up, and those who work lawlessness,\f + \fr 13:41 \ft "Those who work lawlessness" is the exact phrase used at 7:23 of the people who prophesied and drove out demons in his name and were told, "I never knew you." Wheat and darnel look the same until the ear forms. So the sorting is not between people who look righteous and people who look wicked. Both grew in the same field.\f*
\v 42 and will throw them into the furnace of fire; there will be weeping and grinding of teeth.\f + \fr 13:42 \ft The furnace of fire is Daniel's — the one Nebuchadnezzar heated for the three men who would not bow (Daniel 3:6). There it was a pagan king's instrument against the faithful. Here it has changed hands.\f*
\v 43 Then the righteous will shine out like the sun in the kingdom of their Father.\f + \fr 13:43 \ft "Those who are wise will shine like the brightness of the sky, and those who turn many to righteousness like the stars for ever" (Daniel 12:3). Through the whole growing season you could not tell them from the darnel. This is the moment it becomes visible.\f* The one who has ears, let him hear.
\s1 Treasure, a Pearl, and a Dragnet
\p
\v 44 The kingdom of the heavens is like treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and hid; and in his joy he goes and sells everything he has and buys that field.\f + \fr 13:44 \ft With no banks worth the name and armies coming through, burying your wealth in the ground was ordinary prudence, and finding somebody else's was a recognised situation with rules attached. The load-bearing words are "in his joy." He is not counting the cost of what he sells. He cannot believe his luck.\f*
\p
\v 45 Again, the kingdom of the heavens is like a merchant looking for fine pearls.
\v 46 When he found one very valuable pearl he went away and sold everything he had and bought it.\f + \fr 13:46 \ft The first man was not looking for anything and tripped over it. This one has spent his life looking, and knows at a glance what he is holding. Both end up doing exactly the same thing, and gladly. The two parables are not about what the kingdom costs. They are about what it is worth.\f*
\p
\v 47 Again, the kingdom of the heavens is like a dragnet thrown into the sea that gathered together fish of every kind.\f + \fr 13:47 \ft A seine net, sometimes hundreds of feet of it, dragged between boats or hauled in from the beach. It does not select; it takes everything in its path, and the lake held plenty that the Torah counted unclean (Leviticus 11:9-12). The sorting happens on the sand, afterwards — never in the water.\f*
\v 48 When it was full they dragged it up on the shore, and they sat down and gathered the good ones into containers, but the rotten ones they threw out.
\v 49 So it will be at the completion of the age: the angels will go out and separate the evil from among the righteous
\v 50 and will throw them into the furnace of fire; there will be weeping and grinding of teeth \add [i.e. being in the net is not the same as being kept, and it is not the fishermen who decide]\add*.
\s1 Things New and Old
\p
\v 51 Have you understood all these things?" They said to him, "Yes."
\v 52 And he said to them, "For this reason every scribe who has been made a disciple for the kingdom of the heavens is like a master of a house who brings out of his storeroom things new and old."\f + \fr 13:52 \ft Everywhere else in this book a scribe is an opponent. Here is a scribe of another kind — a man trained in the scriptures who has now been apprenticed to the kingdom, and who therefore has two things in his storeroom and does not have to choose between them. He does not throw out the old, and he does not pretend the new has not happened. He brings out both. It is a fair description of what the writer of this book is doing on every page of it.\f*
\p
\v 53 And it happened, when Jesus had finished these parables, that he moved on from there.
\s1 Rejected Where He Grew Up
\p
\v 54 And coming into his hometown he taught them in their synagogue, so that they were astounded and said, "Where did this man get this wisdom and these acts of power?
\v 55 Is this not the carpenter's son?\f + \fr 13:55 \ft Greek \tl tektōn\tl*, a worker in wood and stone — a builder, a jobbing craftsman. They are not sneering. They are producing his file: father's trade, mother's name, brothers by name, sisters still living down the road. They know everything about where he came from, and that is precisely what stops them.\f* Is his mother not called Mary, and his brothers James and Joseph and Simon and Judas?
\v 56 And his sisters — are they not all with us? Where then did this man get all these things?"
\v 57 And they were tripped up over him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his hometown and in his own house."\f + \fr 13:57 \ft Two chapters ago the Pharisees tripped over his power and called it the devil's. Here his neighbours trip over his ordinariness. Both have a category ready that lets them keep not seeing him.\f*
\v 58 And he did not do many acts of power there, because of their lack of trust \add [i.e. the seed and the sower do not change from field to field; the ground does]\add*.
\c 14
\s1 Herod Kills John
\p
\v 1 At that time Herod the tetrarch\f + \fr 14:1 \ft Not a king, whatever verse 9 calls him. A tetrarch was a ruler of a quarter, and Antipas held Galilee and Perea, a fragment of what his father had held. His father is the Herod of chapter 2, who tried to have the child killed. The family's answer to God turning up in its territory does not change.\f* heard the report of Jesus,
\v 2 and he said to his servants, "This is John the Immerser. He has been raised from the dead, and that is why the acts of power are at work in him." \add [i.e. a guilty man's explanation; the one he killed has come back]\add*
\v 3 For Herod had seized John and tied him up and put him away in prison, because of Herodias, the wife of Philip his brother;
\v 4 for John kept saying to him, "It is not permitted for you to have her."\f + \fr 14:4 \ft The Torah forbids a man to take his brother's wife (Leviticus 18:16; 20:21). Antipas had put away his own wife, the daughter of the Nabatean king, to marry Herodias, who had been married to his half-brother. John said so in public. The shape of it is Elijah's: a prophet confronting a weak king who is being driven by his wife (1 Kings 18-21). Ahab was sorry too.\f*
\v 5 And though he wanted to kill him he was afraid of the crowd, because they held him to be a prophet \add [i.e. he is not restrained by conscience; he is restrained by arithmetic]\add*.
\v 6 But when Herod's birthday came, the daughter of Herodias danced in the middle of them and pleased Herod,
\v 7 so he promised with an oath to give her whatever she asked.\f + \fr 14:7 \ft Set this beside 5:34-37, where they were told to swear no oaths at all and let yes be yes. Herod's oath is what kills John. It does not make him truthful; it makes him unable to stop.\f*
\v 8 And she, pushed forward by her mother, said, "Give me here on a platter the head of John the Immerser."
\v 9 And the king was grieved, but because of his oaths and those reclining with him he ordered it to be given \add [i.e. sorry, and not sorry enough to be embarrassed in front of his guests]\add*,
\v 10 and he sent and had John beheaded in the prison.
\v 11 And his head was brought on a platter and given to the girl, and she brought it to her mother \add [i.e. carried in on a serving dish, at a birthday dinner]\add*.
\v 12 And his disciples came and took the body and buried it, and they went and reported it to Jesus.
\s1 He Withdraws, and the Crowds Come After Him
\p
\v 13 When Jesus heard it he withdrew from there by boat to a deserted place by himself; and when the crowds heard it they followed him on foot from the cities.
\v 14 And when he came out he saw a large crowd, and his heart went out to them, and he healed their sick \add [i.e. he had gone away to be alone with this news, and instead spent the day on other people's grief]\add*.
\s1 Five Loaves and Two Fish
\p
\v 15 When evening came the disciples came to him, saying, "The place is deserted and the hour has already gone by; send the crowds away, so that they may go into the villages and buy food for themselves."
\v 16 But Jesus said to them, "They have no need to go away; you give them something to eat." \add [i.e. "you" — he puts it back into their hands]\add*
\v 17 And they said to him, "We have nothing here except five loaves and two fish."
\v 18 And he said, "Bring them here to me."
\v 19 And he ordered the crowds to recline on the grass; and taking the five loaves and the two fish he looked up into the sky and spoke the blessing, and he broke the loaves and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the crowds.\f + \fr 14:19 \ft Took, blessed, broke, gave. It is the ordinary sequence of a Jewish host at any table — and it is word for word what he does with the bread on the last night, in the upper room (26:26). Note also that he does not hand the food to the crowd. He hands it to the twelve, and they carry it.\f*
\v 20 And they all ate and were filled, and they picked up what was left over of the broken pieces — twelve baskets full.\f + \fr 14:20 \ft Bread in the wilderness, for a whole people, is the manna (Exodus 16). Nearer still is Elisha, who fed a hundred men with twenty barley loaves, and "they ate and had some left over" (2 Kings 4:42-44) — twenty loaves for a hundred, against five for five thousand. And the number of baskets is twelve, which is the number of the tribes: what is left over is more than what was brought, and there is a basket for each of them.\f*
\v 21 And those who ate were about five thousand men, apart from women and children \add [i.e. so the real count is three or four times that]\add*.
\s1 Walking on the Sea
\p
\v 22 And immediately he made the disciples get into the boat and go on ahead of him to the other side, while he sent the crowds away.
\v 23 And when he had sent the crowds away he went up on the mountain by himself to pray. When evening came he was there alone \add [i.e. doing exactly what he told them to do at 6:6]\add*,
\v 24 but the boat was already many stadia away from the land, being battered by the waves, for the wind was against them.\f + \fr 14:24 \ft A stadion is about two hundred yards, so they are a good way out with no way back. The word for "battered" is the one used elsewhere in this book for being tortured (8:6, 29).\f*
\v 25 And in the fourth watch of the night he came to them, walking on the sea.\f + \fr 14:25 \ft The Romans cut the night into four watches; the fourth is roughly three to six in the morning. They have been fighting the wind all night, and he comes at the far end of it. As for what he is doing: "he alone stretched out the heavens and treads on the waves of the sea" (Job 9:8); "your way was through the sea, your path through the great waters, and your footprints were not seen" (Psalm 77:19). Walking on water is not a thing men do in Israel's scriptures. It is a thing God does.\f*
\v 26 And when the disciples saw him walking on the sea they were shaken, saying, "It is a ghost!" And they screamed out in fear.
\v 27 But immediately Jesus spoke to them, saying, "Take heart — I am. Do not be afraid."\f + \fr 14:27 \ft On the surface it means "it's me." But the Greek is \tl egō eimi\tl*, and that is what stands in the Greek Old Testament where God names himself to Moses at the bush — "I am who I am" (Exodus 3:14) — and where he says through Isaiah, "so that you may know and trust me and understand that I am he" (Isaiah 43:10). Put it together with what he is standing on, and with "do not be afraid," which is how these appearances always open (Genesis 15:1; Isaiah 41:10), and the sentence is a great deal larger than an introduction.\f*
\v 28 And Peter answered him and said, "Lord, if it is you, order me to come to you on the waters." \add [i.e. not "let me try" — order me; he will not step out on his own word]\add*
\v 29 And he said, "Come." And Peter got down from the boat and walked on the waters and came toward Jesus.
\v 30 But seeing the wind he was afraid, and beginning to sink he screamed out, "Lord, rescue me!"\f + \fr 14:30 \ft "Save me, God — the waters have come up to my neck. I sink in deep mire where there is no foothold" (Psalm 69:1-2). The prayer has been stripped down to three words, which is all a drowning man has.\f*
\v 31 And immediately Jesus stretched out his hand and took hold of him, and he said to him, "You of little trust, why did you doubt?"\f + \fr 14:31 \ft It is worth watching where he failed. Not when he stepped out; he walked. He went under when he looked at the weather instead of at the man who had said "come." The word for "doubt" is used only once more in this book — of the disciples on the mountain in Galilee at the very end, looking at the risen Jesus (28:17).\f*
\v 32 And when they had gotten into the boat the wind died down.
\v 33 And those in the boat bowed down before him, saying, "Truly you are God's Son."\f + \fr 14:33 \ft The first time the disciples say it. Until now the words have been used by the voice from heaven (3:17), by the tester in the wilderness (4:3, 6), and by demons (8:29). What they have got hold of here is the power. What it will mean when the same Son goes to a cross is still two chapters away, and Peter will fight him over it (16:22).\f*
\s1 Gennesaret
\p
\v 34 And when they had crossed over they came to land at Gennesaret.
\v 35 And when the men of that place recognized him they sent word into all that surrounding country and brought him all who were badly off,
\v 36 and they begged him that they might only touch the fringe of his garment; and all who touched it were brought safely through \add [i.e. word of the woman who did that has clearly gone round the whole plain]\add*.
\c 15
\s1 The Tradition of the Elders
\p
\v 1 Then Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem came to Jesus, saying, \add [i.e. sent down from the capital — this is no longer a local quarrel]\add*
\v 2 "Why do your disciples step over the tradition of the elders? For they do not wash their hands when they eat bread."\f + \fr 15:2 \ft The Torah says nothing about washing your hands before an ordinary meal. It was a priestly rule, for priests handling holy food (Leviticus 22:1-9), which some teachers had extended to everybody at every table as a mark of seriousness. "The tradition of the elders" is the body of such rulings handed down alongside the written law, and the charge assumes it carries the same weight.\f*
\v 3 And he answered and said to them, "And why do you step over the command of God because of your tradition? \add [i.e. he does not defend the disciples; he changes what is on trial]\add*
\v 4 For God said, 'Honor your father and your mother,' and, 'The one who speaks evil of father or mother shall come to his end in death.'\f + \fr 15:4 \ft The fifth of the ten commandments (Exodus 20:12), put beside the law that made cursing a parent a capital matter (Exodus 21:17). He pairs them to show what weight the obligation carries — and he says "God said," not "Moses said."\f*
\v 5 But you say, 'Whoever says to his father or his mother, "Whatever help you might have had from me is a gift" —
\v 6 he shall not honor his father.' And so you have made the word of God void because of your tradition.\f + \fr 15:6 \ft The word for "gift" is the technical one: the money is declared dedicated to the temple, and once declared it is out of reach for anything else — including feeding your parents. Whether it ever reached the temple is another question. What it reliably did was release a man from an obligation the law had laid on him, with a religious formula. That is what "made void" means: not bent, cancelled.\f*
\v 7 Hypocrites! Well did Isaiah prophesy about you, saying:
\v 8 'This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far away from me;
\v 9 and in vain do they worship me, teaching as teachings the commands of men.'"\f + \fr 15:9 \ft Isaiah 29:13. "In vain" is the sharpest word in it: not defective worship, worthless worship — it does nothing at all. And it names the anatomy of the thing. The mouth is right and the heart is somewhere else.\f*
\s1 What Really Makes a Man Common
\p
\v 10 And calling the crowd to himself he said to them, "Hear and understand:
\v 11 it is not what goes into the mouth that makes a man common, but what comes out of the mouth — this makes a man common."\f + \fr 15:11 \ft "Common" is the opposite of holy: to make a thing common is to take it out of the set-apart sphere. The whole apparatus of Leviticus 11-15 works on the assumption that certain contacts and certain foods do this to a person from outside. He moves the source of the trouble inside, and a first-century listener would have felt the ground shift.\f*
\p
\v 12 Then the disciples came and said to him, "Do you know that the Pharisees were tripped up when they heard this word?" \add [i.e. and the disciples are nervous about it]\add*
\v 13 And he answered and said, "Every plant that my heavenly Father did not plant will be uprooted.\f + \fr 15:13 \ft Israel is God's planting all through the prophets — "the shoot of my planting, the work of my hands" (Isaiah 60:21), "oaks of righteousness, the planting of the LORD" (Isaiah 61:3). To say of these men that the Father did not plant them is to say where their authority came from, and where it is going.\f*
\v 14 Leave them alone; they are blind guides. And if a blind man guides a blind man, both will fall into a pit \add [i.e. and the followers go into the pit with them]\add*."
\p
\v 15 And Peter answered and said to him, "Explain the parable to us."
\v 16 And he said, "Are you also still without understanding? \add [i.e. the crowd may be excused; you have been standing next to me]\add*
\v 17 Do you not see that everything that goes into the mouth passes into the belly and is thrown out into the latrine?
\v 18 But the things that come out of the mouth come out of the heart, and these make a man common.
\v 19 For out of the heart come evil reasonings, murders, adulteries, sexual immoralities, thefts, false testimonies, slanders.\f + \fr 15:19 \ft Run the list against the ten commandments and it tracks them: murder, adultery, theft, false witness. And at the head of the list, before any of the acts, stands the thing that produces them — the reasoning that goes on inside a man. The argument is not that the law's moral demands have been relaxed. It is that these men are washing their hands while the commandments go unkept.\f*
\v 20 These are the things that make a man common; but eating with unwashed hands does not make a man common."
\s1 A Canaanite Woman
\p
\v 21 And going out from there Jesus withdrew to the parts of Tyre and Sidon \add [i.e. straight across the border, into Gentile country]\add*.
\v 22 And look — a Canaanite woman\f + \fr 15:22 \ft The word is deliberately old. Mark, telling the same story, calls her a Syrophoenician, which is what you would have called her at the time. "Canaanite" reaches back to the people who held the land before Israel took it, the nations Israel was told to drive out (Deuteronomy 7:1-2) — as far outside as a person can be put. And out of her mouth come the two titles this book cares most about.\f* from that territory came out and screamed, "Have mercy on me, Lord, son of David! My daughter is badly demon-possessed."
\v 23 But he did not answer her a word. And his disciples came and asked him, "Send her away, for she is screaming after us."
\v 24 But he answered and said, "I was not sent except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel." \add [i.e. word for word the order he gave the twelve at 10:6]\add*
\v 25 But she came and bowed down before him, saying, "Lord, help me."
\v 26 And he answered and said, "It is not good to take the children's bread and throw it to the little dogs."\f + \fr 15:26 \ft The Greek is a diminutive — not the scavengers of the street, which is what "dog" usually meant when it was aimed at a Gentile, but the dogs that live in the house and lie under the table. That is the crack she gets her fingers into.\f*
\v 27 And she said, "Yes, Lord — and yet the little dogs eat from the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."\f + \fr 15:27 \ft She does not argue with the picture, and she does not claim a seat. She takes the picture and follows it one step further than he did: a table that generous has crumbs under it. And "masters" is the same word she has been calling him — Lord. She is arguing from how much there is, not from what she deserves, and the man in front of her has just picked up twelve baskets of leftovers.\f*
\v 28 Then Jesus answered and said to her, "Woman, your trust is great. Let it happen for you as you want." And her daughter was healed from that hour.\f + \fr 15:28 \ft Twice in this book someone's trust is praised outright: a Roman officer, of whom he says he has found nothing like it in Israel (8:10), and this woman. Both are Gentiles, and between them stand the men from Jerusalem who have just been told their worship is worth nothing.\f*
\s1 On the Mountain by the Sea
\p
\v 29 And moving on from there Jesus came alongside the Sea of Galilee, and he went up on the mountain and sat down there.
\v 30 And large crowds came to him, bringing with them lame, crippled, blind, mute, and many others, and they laid them down at his feet; and he healed them,
\v 31 so that the crowd was amazed as they saw mute men speaking, crippled men made sound, lame men walking and blind men seeing; and they gave glory to the God of Israel.\f + \fr 15:31 \ft The list is Isaiah's: "then the eyes of the blind will be opened and the ears of the deaf unstopped, then the lame man will leap like a deer and the tongue of the mute sing" (Isaiah 35:5-6). And note how the crowd names God — "the God of Israel." That is not how an Israelite says it. It is how you say it about somebody else's God, when you have just seen what he can do.\f*
\s1 Seven Loaves
\p
\v 32 And Jesus called his disciples to himself and said, "My heart goes out to the crowd, because they have already stayed with me three days and have nothing to eat; and I do not want to send them away hungry, or they may give out on the road."
\v 33 And the disciples said to him, "Where in a deserted place will we get enough loaves to fill such a crowd?" \add [i.e. asked by men who watched him do it once already]\add*
\v 34 And Jesus said to them, "How many loaves do you have?" And they said, "Seven, and a few little fish."
\v 35 And ordering the crowd to recline on the ground,
\v 36 he took the seven loaves and the fish, and giving thanks he broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the crowds.
\v 37 And they all ate and were filled; and they picked up what was left over of the broken pieces — seven hampers full.\f + \fr 15:37 \ft Twelve baskets the first time, seven hampers this time, and the Greek uses a different word for the container in each — this book keeps the two occasions carefully apart, and Jesus will make the disciples count them both back to him at 16:9-10. Twelve is the number of the tribes. Seven is the number of completeness, and this feeding happens on the border, in front of a crowd that praises "the God of Israel" as though he were not theirs.\f*
\v 38 And those who ate were four thousand men, apart from women and children.
\v 39 And when he had sent the crowds away he got into the boat and came to the districts of Magadan.
\c 16
\s1 They Ask for a Sign Out of the Sky
\p
\v 1 And the Pharisees and Sadducees came, and to test him they asked him to show them a sign out of heaven.\f + \fr 16:1 \ft These two parties agreed on almost nothing — the Sadducees denied the resurrection, rejected the oral tradition, and ran the temple; the Pharisees held all three against them. Here they arrive together. And note the verb: "to test him" is the same word used of the devil in the wilderness (4:1). As for what they want, a sign out of the sky is precisely what he has just given — bread in a wilderness, which is where the manna came from (Exodus 16:4). They are asking for the thing they have already been shown and refused.\f*
\v 2 But he answered and said to them, "When evening comes you say, 'Fair weather, for the sky is fiery red';
\v 3 and in the morning, 'Stormy today, for the sky is fiery red and gloomy.' You know how to judge the face of the sky, but you cannot judge the signs of the times.\f + \fr 16:3 \ft These two verses are missing from several of the oldest copies. Reading the weather takes no training; every farmer can do it. What they cannot read is the season they are standing in.\f*
\v 4 An evil and adulterous generation looks for a sign, and no sign will be given it except the sign of Jonah." And he left them and went away \add [i.e. the same answer as at 12:39, and this time he does not stay to argue]\add*.
\s1 The Leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees
\p
\v 5 And when the disciples came to the other side they had forgotten to take loaves.
\v 6 And Jesus said to them, "Watch out and be on guard against the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees."\f + \fr 16:6 \ft Leaven is chosen for what it does: a very little of it works right through the batch, and once it is mixed in you cannot see it. He used the same picture of the kingdom at 13:33. Here the point is the same and the substance is the opposite.\f*
\v 7 And they were reasoning among themselves, saying, "We took no loaves."
\v 8 But Jesus knew it and said, "Why are you reasoning among yourselves, you of little trust, that you have no loaves?
\v 9 Do you not yet understand, or remember the five loaves of the five thousand, and how many baskets you picked up?
\v 10 Or the seven loaves of the four thousand, and how many hampers you picked up?\f + \fr 16:9 \ft He makes them count it back to him, and he keeps the two occasions apart down to the different word for the basket. The number that matters both times is the number left over. Men who have twice gathered up more than was there to begin with should not be able to worry about a forgotten loaf.\f*
\v 11 How do you not see that I was not speaking to you about loaves? Be on guard against the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
\v 12 Then they understood that he had not said to be on guard against the leaven of bread, but against the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees \add [i.e. it is the teaching that is the danger, not the men]\add*.
\s1 Who Do You Say I Am?
\p
\v 13 When Jesus came into the parts of Caesarea Philippi\f + \fr 16:13 \ft About as far from Jerusalem as a man could get and still be in the territory of Israel's rulers. The town sat at the foot of Mount Hermon by a cave sacred to the god Pan, and Philip the tetrarch had rebuilt it and named it for Caesar. The confession that follows is made in front of a pagan shrine, in a town named after a man who was called a god.\f* he asked his disciples, "Who do men say the son of man is?"
\v 14 And they said, "Some say John the Immerser, others Elijah, and others Jeremiah or one of the prophets."\f + \fr 16:14 \ft Every answer puts him in a category that already exists: a prophet, of one kind or another. Jeremiah is named only in this book, and the choice is not idle — Jeremiah was the man who prophesied the temple's fall, wept over a people who would not listen, and was beaten and thrown in a pit for it.\f*
\v 15 He said to them, "But you — who do you say I am?"
\v 16 And Simon Peter answered and said, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."\f + \fr 16:16 \ft "The living God" is how Israel's scriptures distinguish him from gods that are carved: David facing Goliath speaks of "the armies of the living God" (1 Samuel 17:26); the psalmist thirsts "for God, for the living God" (Psalm 42:2). Said here, a few hundred yards from Pan's cave, it is not a decoration.\f*
\v 17 And Jesus answered and said to him, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, because flesh and blood did not uncover this to you \add [i.e. you did not work it out, and nobody taught it to you]\add*, but my Father in the heavens.
\v 18 And I also say to you that you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my assembly, and the gates of Hades will not overpower it.\f + \fr 16:18 \ft In Greek the name and the noun are two forms of one word — Petros, a rock; petra, the rock. In the Aramaic he was speaking it is not even two forms: \tl kepha\tl* both times. You are Rock, and on this rock. "Assembly" is Greek \tl ekklēsia\tl*, which in the Greek Old Testament is the word for the \tl qahal\tl* — the assembled people of Israel, called together before God (Deuteronomy 4:10). It is not a building and not an institution; it is a gathering. What is startling is the possessive: not the assembly of the LORD, but "my assembly." The word occurs only twice in this book, here and at 18:17. As for the gates of Hades, a city's gates were where its power sat — where the armies mustered and the elders gave judgment. Hades is the realm of the dead. Death will not hold what he is building, and within three verses he will say why he knows.\f*
\v 19 I will give you the keys of the kingdom of the heavens, and whatever you tie up on the earth will have been tied up in the heavens, and whatever you untie on the earth will have been untied in the heavens."\f + \fr 16:19 \ft "I will put the key of the house of David on his shoulder; he will open and no one will shut, and he will shut and no one will open" — said of Eliakim, the steward put over the royal household (Isaiah 22:22). A steward holds keys; he does not own the house. And "tie up" and "untie" are the ordinary Jewish terms for a teacher's rulings: to bind is to declare a thing forbidden, to loose is to declare it permitted. The same authority is handed to the whole community at 18:18.\f*
\v 20 Then he ordered the disciples to tell no one that he was the Christ \add [i.e. because the word means something to them that it does not mean, and he is about to say what it does mean]\add*.
\s1 The First Word About the Cross
\p
\v 21 From then on Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go away to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and on the third day be raised.\f + \fr 16:21 \ft The second and last time this book turns on "from then on" — the first was 4:17, where the proclaiming began. From here the story is walking to Jerusalem. Note who is named: elders, chief priests, scribes. That is the council, and it is precise. Not Rome, not Herod, not a mob.\f*
\v 22 And Peter took him aside and began to speak harshly to him, saying, "God be merciful to you, Lord! This shall never happen to you." \add [i.e. the same verb used of Jesus silencing demons, now used on Jesus, by the man who has just been called the rock]\add*
\v 23 But he turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stone I trip over, because you are not thinking the thoughts of God but the thoughts of men."\f + \fr 16:23 \ft "Get behind me, Satan" is word for word what he said in the wilderness (4:10), and the offer on the table is the same offer: all the kingdoms of the world, and no cross. It came last time from the adversary and this time from the closest friend, which is what makes it dangerous. And "a stone I trip over" is \tl skandalon\tl*, the trigger of a trap — said to the man he has just named Rock, in the same conversation.\f*
\s1 Taking Up a Cross
\p
\v 24 Then Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone wants to come after me, let him deny himself and pick up his cross and follow me \add [i.e. what he has just said must happen to him, he now says must happen to them]\add*.
\v 25 For whoever wants to rescue his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
\v 26 For what will it profit a man if he gains the whole world but forfeits his life? Or what will a man give in exchange for his life?\f + \fr 16:26 \ft "No man can ransom another or give God the price of his life; the ransom of a life is costly, and can never be enough" (Psalm 49:7-9). Once it is gone there is nothing you could offer to buy it back, and the whole world was the highest bid available.\f*
\v 27 For the son of man is about to come in the glory of his Father with his angels, and then he will pay back each man according to what he has done.
\v 28 Amen, I say to you, there are some of those standing here who will never taste death until they see the son of man coming in his kingdom."\f + \fr 16:28 \ft "Taste death" is a Hebrew way of saying to die. Whatever else this saying reaches, read what comes next in the book: six days later three of the men standing there go up a mountain and see him.\f*
\c 17
\s1 Changed in Form on the Mountain
\p
\v 1 And after six days Jesus took along Peter and James and John his brother and brought them up a high mountain by themselves.\f + \fr 17:1 \ft "The cloud covered the mountain. The glory of the LORD settled on Sinai, and the cloud covered it six days; and on the seventh day he called to Moses out of the middle of the cloud" (Exodus 24:15-16). Six days, a high mountain, a cloud, a voice out of it: everything in this scene is set up as Sinai.\f*
\v 2 And he was changed in form in front of them, and his face shone like the sun, and his clothes became white as the light.\f + \fr 17:2 \ft When Moses came down from Sinai the skin of his face was shining, because of where he had been (Exodus 34:29-35). That was reflected light and it faded. This is not described as reflection. And "shone like the sun" is the phrase used at 13:43 for what the righteous will be at the end of the age — what is coming then is showing through here.\f*
\v 3 And look — Moses and Elijah appeared to them, talking with him.\f + \fr 17:3 \ft The two men in Israel's story who met God on this mountain: Moses on Sinai, Elijah at Horeb (1 Kings 19:8-18). The two whose leaving was strange — God buried Moses and no one knows where (Deuteronomy 34:5-6), and Elijah went up in a whirlwind and did not die (2 Kings 2:11). And between them, the Law and the Prophets. They are not teaching him. They are talking with him.\f*
\v 4 And Peter answered and said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you want, I will make three shelters here — one for you and one for Moses and one for Elijah."\f + \fr 17:4 \ft Booths, of the kind Israel lived in at the feast of Tabernacles to remember the wilderness (Leviticus 23:42-43). His instinct is to house the glory and keep it. But count the shelters: three, one each, as though these were three of a kind. The voice out of the cloud is about to correct exactly that.\f*
\v 5 While he was still speaking, look — a bright cloud overshadowed them, and look — a voice out of the cloud saying, "This is my Son, the beloved, in whom I have taken delight. Listen to him."\f + \fr 17:5 \ft The cloud is the cloud that covered Sinai and filled the tent of meeting (Exodus 40:34-38). The first two lines of the voice are what was said at the river (3:17) — Psalm 2:7 over the king, Isaiah 42:1 over the servant. The third line is new, and it is Deuteronomy 18:15, Moses promising a prophet like himself: "it is to him you shall listen." So it answers Peter twice over. Not three shelters — one voice, and you are to obey it. And the last thing Peter would not listen to was that this man is going to die (16:22).\f*
\v 6 And when the disciples heard it they fell on their faces and were terrified.
\v 7 And Jesus came and touched them and said, "Get up, and do not be afraid."
\v 8 And lifting their eyes they saw no one except Jesus alone \add [i.e. the Law and the Prophets have said their piece and stepped back out of the picture]\add*.
\s1 Elijah Has Already Come
\p
\v 9 And as they were coming down from the mountain Jesus ordered them, saying, "Tell no one what you have seen until the son of man has been raised from the dead \add [i.e. told before that, this can only be heard as glory without a cross]\add*."
\v 10 And the disciples asked him, "Why then do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
\v 11 And he answered and said, "Elijah does come, and he will restore all things.
\v 12 But I say to you that Elijah has already come, and they did not recognize him, but did to him whatever they wanted. So also the son of man is about to suffer at their hands."\f + \fr 17:12 \ft "I am sending you Elijah the prophet before the great and terrible day of the LORD comes; and he will turn the hearts of fathers to their children" (Malachi 4:5-6). Jesus does not deny the expectation; he says it has already happened and nobody noticed. "Did to him whatever they wanted" is a birthday party, a dancing girl, an oath, and a head on a dish (14:6-11). And then the sentence that matters: what was done to the forerunner is the shape of what will be done to him.\f*
\v 13 Then the disciples understood that he had spoken to them about John the Immerser.
\s1 A Boy the Disciples Could Not Heal
\p
\v 14 And when they came to the crowd, a man came to him, kneeling in front of him
\v 15 and saying, "Lord, have mercy on my son, because he is moonstruck and suffers badly; for he often falls into the fire and often into the water.
\v 16 And I brought him to your disciples, and they were not able to heal him." \add [i.e. and they had been given authority to do exactly this, at 10:1]\add*
\v 17 And Jesus answered and said, "O generation without trust and twisted, how long will I be with you? How long will I put up with you? Bring him here to me."\f + \fr 17:17 \ft The words come out of the Song of Moses, the great indictment of Israel: "a crooked and twisted generation... children in whom there is no faithfulness" (Deuteronomy 32:5, 20). And the weariness in the question is God's weariness in the wilderness: "how long will this people despise me, and how long will they not trust me, for all the signs I have done among them?" (Numbers 14:11). He is not only speaking to the twelve.\f*
\v 18 And Jesus spoke harshly to it, and the demon came out of him, and the boy was healed from that hour.
\p
\v 19 Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, "Why were we not able to throw it out?"
\v 20 And he said to them, "Because of your little trust. For Amen, I say to you, if you have trust like a mustard seed you will say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move; and nothing will be impossible for you."\f + \fr 17:20 \ft "Uprooter of mountains" was what people called a teacher who could get through a problem nobody else could shift; moving a mountain is the standing phrase for the impossible. And the seed is the proverbial smallest thing there is. So the answer is not that they need more of what they had. A very little of the real thing does everything; a great deal of the divided, anxious sort does nothing at all. Some later copies add a verse 21 here — "but this kind does not go out except by prayer and fasting" — which the earliest copies do not have.\f*
\s1 The Second Word About the Cross
\p
\v 22 As they were gathering together in Galilee, Jesus said to them, "The son of man is about to be handed over into the hands of men,\f + \fr 17:22 \ft The sentence turns on one word said twice: the son of man given into the hands of men. And "handed over" is the verb that will carry the rest of this book — Judas hands him over, the council hands him over, Pilate hands him over.\f*
\v 23 and they will kill him, and on the third day he will be raised." And they were deeply grieved \add [i.e. an improvement on last time, when Peter argued; but they have heard the killing and not the raising]\add*.
\s1 The Temple Tax
\p
\v 24 When they came into Capernaum, those who collect the two drachmas came to Peter and said, "Does your teacher not pay the two drachmas?"\f + \fr 17:24 \ft The half-shekel every Jewish man over twenty owed each year toward the running of the temple, commanded in Exodus 30:11-16, where it is called a ransom for his life — the rich no more, the poor no less. Paying it was a way of saying you belonged. Given the arguments he has been having, the question has an edge on it, and Peter answers before he thinks.\f*
\v 25 He said, "Yes." And when he came into the house Jesus spoke first, saying, "What do you think, Simon? From whom do the kings of the earth take tolls or the poll tax — from their own sons, or from others?"
\v 26 And when he said, "From others," Jesus said to him, "So then the sons are free.\f + \fr 17:26 \ft The temple is his Father's house, and a king does not tax his own children. Note the plural: not "the Son is free" but "the sons are." Whatever exemption he is claiming, he has just extended it to the man he is talking to.\f*
\v 27 But so that we do not trip them up, go to the sea, throw in a hook, and take the first fish that comes up; and when you open its mouth you will find a stater. Take that and give it to them for me and for you."\f + \fr 17:27 \ft A stater was worth four drachmas — two men's tax exactly, and no more. The whole point of the episode is in the words "so that we do not trip them up": he owes nothing and pays anyway, rather than put a stumbling block in front of anyone. It is the same shape as everything he has just said about losing your life to keep it, worked out over a coin. And the coin covers Peter as well as himself.\f*
\c 18
\s1 Who Is Greatest?
\p
\v 1 In that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying, "Who then is greatest in the kingdom of the heavens?" \add [i.e. three of them have just been up a mountain without the rest, and one of them has been given a coin out of a fish]\add*
\v 2 And calling a child to himself he stood him in the middle of them\f + \fr 18:2 \ft Not a picture of innocence — that is a much later way of looking at children. In this world a child had no legal standing, no property, no voice, and no claim on anybody. He is holding up the least important person in the room.\f*
\v 3 and said, "Amen, I say to you, unless you turn around and become like children, you will never go into the kingdom of the heavens.
\v 4 So whoever makes himself low like this child — this one is the greatest in the kingdom of the heavens \add [i.e. they asked for the top of the ladder and he has turned the ladder over]\add*.
\v 5 And whoever welcomes one such child in my name welcomes me;
\v 6 but whoever trips up one of these little ones who trust in me, it would be better for him to have a donkey's millstone hung around his neck and to be sunk in the open sea.\f + \fr 18:6 \ft Not the hand-mill a woman turns but the great upper stone that a donkey walks in circles to drive. And drowning in open water meant no body and no grave. He is naming the worst end he can put into a sentence, and saying it would be the better outcome.\f*
\s1 Cut It Off
\p
\v 7 Woe to the world for the things that trip men up! It must be that such things come, but woe to the man through whom the tripping comes \add [i.e. that they are inevitable does not make anyone innocent of causing them]\add*.
\v 8 And if your hand or your foot trips you up, cut it off and throw it away from you. It is better for you to go into the life maimed or lame than to have two hands or two feet and be thrown into the fire of the age.\f + \fr 18:8 \ft Greek \tl aiōnios\tl*, the adjective from \tl aiōn\tl*, an age. Jewish thinking ran on two ages — this present age, \tl olam hazeh\tl*, and the age to come, \tl olam haba\tl*. The word says which age a thing belongs to before it says anything about how long it lasts. "The fire of the age" and "the life of the age" (19:16, 29) are the two outcomes on the far side of the turn.\f*
\v 9 And if your eye trips you up, tear it out and throw it away from you. It is better for you to go into the life with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the Gehenna of fire \add [i.e. the same words he used in the sermon, now turned toward what one member of a community can do to another]\add*.
\s1 Do Not Think Little of One of These
\p
\v 10 See that you do not think little of one of these little ones; for I say to you that their angels in the heavens always look on the face of my Father in the heavens.\f + \fr 18:10 \ft To be permitted to see a king's face was the highest privilege at any court; it was granted to a handful of officials and to nobody else (Esther 1:14). Whatever else is being said here about angels, this is being said: the people your community is inclined to overlook have standing representation in the throne room, and it never leaves. Some later copies add a verse 11 here — "for the son of man came to save what was lost" — which the earliest copies do not have.\f*
\s1 The Sheep That Wandered Off
\p
\v 12 What do you think? If a man has a hundred sheep and one of them wanders off, will he not leave the ninety-nine on the mountains and go and look for the one that is wandering?
\v 13 And if he happens to find it, Amen, I say to you, he rejoices over it more than over the ninety-nine that did not wander off.
\v 14 In the same way it is not the will of your Father in the heavens that one of these little ones should be lost.\f + \fr 18:14 \ft The charge God brought against Israel's shepherds was this exactly: "the weak you have not strengthened... the strayed you have not brought back, the lost you have not sought." And his answer was, "I myself will search for my sheep... I will seek the lost and bring back the strayed" (Ezekiel 34:4, 11, 16). The community is being told to do what God does, and told what God wants, which settles the question of whether going after the one is worth the trouble.\f*
\s1 If Your Brother Sins
\p
\v 15 If your brother sins against you, go and show him his fault between you and him alone. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother \add [i.e. and the object is to get the man back, not to be proved right]\add*.
\v 16 But if he does not listen, take one or two others along with you, so that by the mouth of two or three witnesses every matter may stand.\f + \fr 18:16 \ft Deuteronomy 19:15, the Torah's rule of evidence: one witness is not enough to establish anything against a man. The procedure is built out of the law, and it is built to be slow.\f*
\v 17 And if he refuses to listen to them, tell it to the assembly; and if he refuses to listen even to the assembly, let him be to you as the man of the nations and the tax collector.\f + \fr 18:17 \ft "Assembly" is \tl ekklēsia\tl* again, the word from 16:18 and the only other place this book uses it — the gathered people, not a building. And note who "the man of the nations and the tax collector" turns out to be in this book. A Roman officer with more trust than anyone in Israel. A Canaanite woman. A man at a toll booth who got up and followed. Being put outside is a real thing and a serious one; it is also, in this book, exactly where he goes looking.\f*
\p
\v 18 Amen, I say to you, whatever you tie up on the earth will have been tied up in heaven, and whatever you untie on the earth will have been untied in heaven \add [i.e. what was said to Peter alone at 16:19 is now said to all of them]\add*.
\v 19 Again, Amen, I say to you, if two of you agree on the earth about any matter they may ask, it will happen for them from my Father in the heavens.
\v 20 For where two or three have been gathered together in my name, there I am in the middle of them."\f + \fr 18:20 \ft The rabbis said that where two sit together with words of Torah between them, God's own presence rests on them. He says the same thing about himself. It is what the whole procedure rests on: the assembly is not deciding on its own.\f*
\s1 How Many Times?
\p
\v 21 Then Peter came and said to him, "Lord, how many times shall my brother sin against me and I forgive him? As many as seven times?" \add [i.e. and seven is already generous — three was the usual measure of a man's patience]\add*
\v 22 Jesus said to him, "I do not say to you as many as seven times, but as many as seventy-seven times.\f + \fr 18:22 \ft The number is lifted out of Genesis 4:24, where Lamech boasts, "if Cain is avenged seven times, then Lamech seventy-seven times" — the high-water mark of human vengeance, revenge multiplying on itself down the generations. Jesus takes that exact number and hangs it on forgiveness instead. It is not a quota. It is Lamech turned inside out.\f*
\s1 The Slave Who Would Not Forgive
\p
\v 23 For this reason the kingdom of the heavens has become like a man, a king, who wanted to settle accounts with his slaves.
\v 24 And when he had begun to settle, one debtor of ten thousand talents was brought to him.\f + \fr 18:24 \ft Do the arithmetic, because the parable depends on it. A talent was about seventy-five pounds of silver, or six thousand denarii; a denarius was a day's wage. Ten thousand talents is sixty million days of work — something like two hundred thousand years. The whole annual revenue of Herod Antipas' territories ran to two hundred talents. This is not a large debt. It is a number chosen because no such debt could exist, and "ten thousand" was as high as Greek had a word for.\f*
\v 25 And since he did not have the means to pay it back, the master ordered him to be sold, along with his wife and children and everything he had, and payment to be made.
\v 26 So the slave fell down and bowed low before him, saying, 'Be patient with me, and I will pay you back everything.' \add [i.e. which is not true and cannot be true; he is asking for time he could never use]\add*
\v 27 And the master of that slave, his heart going out to him, let him go and forgave him the loan \add [i.e. and he was not asked for this — he was asked for patience]\add*.
\v 28 But that slave went out and found one of his fellow slaves who owed him a hundred denarii, and he took hold of him and began to choke him, saying, 'Pay back whatever you owe.'\f + \fr 18:28 \ft A hundred denarii is real money — three or four months of a labourer's wages. It is also six hundred thousand times less than what this man was let off ten minutes earlier. And the second debt, unlike the first, could actually have been paid.\f*
\v 29 So his fellow slave fell down and begged him, saying, 'Be patient with me, and I will pay you back.' \add [i.e. word for word what he himself had said to the king]\add*
\v 30 But he refused; instead he went and threw him into prison until he should pay back what was owed \add [i.e. where he cannot earn a thing]\add*.
\v 31 So when his fellow slaves saw what had happened they were deeply grieved, and they went and made clear to their master everything that had happened.
\v 32 Then his master called him in and said to him, 'Evil slave! I forgave you all that debt because you begged me.
\v 33 Should you not also have had mercy on your fellow slave, as I had mercy on you?'
\v 34 And his master was angry and handed him over to the torturers until he should pay back everything he owed.\f + \fr 18:34 \ft The cancelled debt is put back on him. And "until he should pay back everything he owed" is not a sentence with a term on it: the sum is sixty million days' wages and he is in a cell. The forgiveness was real, and it was withdrawn, and what withdrew it was his refusal to pass it on.\f*
\v 35 So also my heavenly Father will do to you, unless each of you forgives his brother from your hearts."\f + \fr 18:35 \ft This is the prayer he taught them, told as a story: "forgive us our debts, as we too have forgiven our debtors" — and the one line of it he stopped to explain, "if you do not forgive men, neither will your Father forgive" (6:12, 15). "From your hearts" closes the last door. Saying the words is not it.\f*
\c 19
\p
\v 1 And it happened, when Jesus had finished these words, that he moved on from Galilee and came into the territory of Judea across the Jordan \add [i.e. he has left Galilee, and everything from here runs toward Jerusalem]\add*.
\v 2 And large crowds followed him, and he healed them there.
\s1 Divorce
\p
\v 3 And Pharisees came to him, testing him and saying, "Is it permitted for a man to divorce his wife for any cause at all?"\f + \fr 19:3 \ft "For any cause at all" is the giveaway: they are asking him to take a side in a live quarrel between the two great schools. Deuteronomy 24:1 lets a man divorce his wife if he finds "something indecent" in her, and the school of Shammai read that narrowly, as unfaithfulness, while the school of Hillel read it as wide as you like — Hillel's followers were prepared to allow a ruined dinner. Either answer makes enemies. And they are standing in Herod's territory, where the last man to speak about a marriage lost his head for it (14:3-4).\f*
\v 4 And he answered and said, "Have you not read that the one who made them from the beginning made them male and female,
\v 5 and said, 'For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and will be joined to his wife, and the two will be one flesh'?\f + \fr 19:5 \ft Genesis 1:27 and 2:24. He will not argue about Deuteronomy 24, because he is going behind it. The question the schools were asking is what the law permits; the question he answers is what God made. The Greek word for "joined" is the one for gluing two things into one.\f*
\v 6 So they are no longer two but one flesh. What God has yoked together, then, let no man separate."
\v 7 They said to him, "Why then did Moses order that a man give a certificate of divorce and send her away?"
\v 8 He said to them, "Because of your hard hearts Moses allowed you to divorce your wives, but from the beginning it was not so.\f + \fr 19:8 \ft Note the correction. They said Moses ordered it; he says Moses allowed it. The provision is not God's design being described but human failure being contained — a hard heart is the standing charge against Israel through the prophets (Deuteronomy 10:16; Jeremiah 4:4; Ezekiel 3:7).\f*
\v 9 And I say to you that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another commits adultery." \add [i.e. because the bond a certificate ends on paper was not ended in fact]\add*
\p
\v 10 The disciples said to him, "If this is how it is for a man with his wife, it is better not to marry."
\v 11 But he said to them, "Not everyone can make room for this word, but only those to whom it has been given.
\v 12 For there are eunuchs who were born that way from their mother's womb, and there are eunuchs who were made eunuchs by men, and there are eunuchs who made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of the heavens. The one who can make room for it, let him make room."\f + \fr 19:12 \ft The third kind is meant as a figure, not an instruction; the Torah forbade the mutilation and barred a man so damaged from the assembly (Deuteronomy 23:1). Which is what makes the word so violent to use. A eunuch was the man with no descendants and therefore no future — and Isaiah had already promised him a name in God's house "better than sons and daughters" (Isaiah 56:3-5). He is not saying everyone should live this way; he says twice that it is only for those who can.\f*
\s1 Children
\p
\v 13 Then children were brought to him, so that he might lay his hands on them and pray; but the disciples spoke harshly to them \add [i.e. one chapter after being told what a child is worth]\add*.
\v 14 But Jesus said, "Let the children alone, and do not stop them from coming to me, for the kingdom of the heavens belongs to such as these."
\v 15 And he laid his hands on them and went on from there.
\s1 The Man Who Had Many Things
\p
\v 16 And look — someone came to him and said, "Teacher, what good thing shall I do so that I may have the life of the age?" \add [i.e. one deed, he thinks, that will settle the account]\add*
\v 17 And he said to him, "Why do you ask me about what is good? There is one who is good. But if you want to enter into the life, keep the commands."
\v 18 He said to him, "Which ones?" And Jesus said, "You shall not murder, you shall not commit adultery, you shall not steal, you shall not testify falsely,
\v 19 honor your father and mother, and you shall love your neighbor as yourself."\f + \fr 19:19 \ft The second half of the ten, the commandments about how you treat people, with Leviticus 19:18 added on the end. One of the ten is missing from the list, and it is the tenth: you shall not covet.\f*
\v 20 The young man said to him, "All these I have kept. What am I still lacking?"
\v 21 Jesus said to him, "If you want to be complete, go, sell what you have and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me." \add [i.e. "complete" is the word from the end of the sermon, 5:48 — not one more rule kept, but the whole man turned around]\add*
\v 22 But when the young man heard this word he went away grieved, for he had many possessions.\f + \fr 19:22 \ft He is the only person in this book who is called and does not come. Fishermen dropped their nets, a tax collector got up from his table; this man is devout, honest, and grieving, and he cannot do it. The trouble is not that he is wicked. It is that he cannot make himself let go, which is the point the next paragraph turns into a rule.\f*
\s1 Who Then Can Be Rescued?
\p
\v 23 And Jesus said to his disciples, "Amen, I say to you, it will be hard for a rich man to go into the kingdom of the heavens.
\v 24 Again I say to you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to go into the kingdom of God."\f + \fr 19:24 \ft The largest animal anyone there had seen and the smallest hole in daily use. There was no small gate in Jerusalem called the Needle's Eye; that story was invented much later to soften the sentence. He has moved from "hard" in one verse to "impossible" in the next, and he means to. Note also that "the kingdom of God" here and "the kingdom of the heavens" in the line before are the same thing said two ways.\f*
\v 25 When the disciples heard it they were utterly astounded and said, "Who then can be rescued?"\f + \fr 19:25 \ft Their shock makes sense once you see what they assumed. Wealth was widely read as evidence of God's favour (Psalm 112:1-3). If the men who are visibly blessed cannot get in, the whole ledger has been torn up — which is what has happened.\f*
\v 26 And Jesus looked at them and said to them, "With men this is impossible, but with God all things are possible."\f + \fr 19:26 \ft "Is anything impossible for the LORD?" — said to Abraham and Sarah about a child neither of them could produce (Genesis 18:14). He is not saying they should try harder. He is saying the door does not open from this side.\f*
\s1 What Will There Be for Us?
\p
\v 27 Then Peter answered and said to him, "Look — we have left everything and followed you. What then will there be for us?" \add [i.e. the thing the young man would not do, we did; now what]\add*
\v 28 And Jesus said to them, "Amen, I say to you, in the renewal, when the son of man sits on the throne of his glory, you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.\f + \fr 19:28 \ft "The renewal" is one Greek word, \tl palingenesia\tl* — a being-born-again of everything, the world made new. It is the prophets' hope: "look, I am creating new heavens and a new earth" (Isaiah 65:17). And "judging" in Israel's usage is what the judges did in the book of Judges — not sitting on a bench passing sentences, but governing. Twelve men over twelve tribes: the nation put back together.\f*
\v 29 And everyone who has left houses or brothers or sisters or father or mother or children or fields for the sake of my name will receive a hundred times as much and will inherit the life of the age.
\v 30 But many who are first will be last, and the last first \add [i.e. said to Peter, immediately after promising him a throne]\add*.
\c 20
\s1 Workers in the Vineyard
\p
\v 1 For the kingdom of the heavens is like a man, a master of a house, who went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.\f + \fr 20:1 \ft The sentence opens with "for," and it is doing real work: this parable is the explanation of the line just spoken — many who are first will be last (19:30) — and it ends by saying that line again (20:16). It is Peter's question that is being answered. And a vineyard, in the prophets, is Israel: "the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel" (Isaiah 5:7).\f*
\v 2 And when he had agreed with the workers on a denarius for the day, he sent them into his vineyard \add [i.e. a full day's wage for a full day, contracted and shaken on]\add*.
\v 3 And going out around the third hour \add [i.e. nine in the morning]\add* he saw others standing idle in the marketplace,
\v 4 and he said to them, 'You go into the vineyard too, and whatever is right I will give you.' So they went \add [i.e. on no agreed figure at all]\add*.
\v 5 Again he went out around the sixth and the ninth hour and did the same.
\v 6 And around the eleventh hour he went out and found others standing, and he said to them, 'Why have you stood here all day idle?'
\v 7 They said to him, 'Because no one hired us.' He said to them, 'You go into the vineyard too.' \add [i.e. they were not unwilling; they were unwanted]\add*
\v 8 When evening came the lord of the vineyard said to his foreman, 'Call the workers and pay them their wages, beginning from the last ones to the first.'\f + \fr 20:8 \ft Paying at sundown is the Torah's requirement — a hired man's wage must not be held overnight (Leviticus 19:13; Deuteronomy 24:15). Paying the last men first is not required by anything, and it is the whole engine of the story: it means the men hired at dawn have to stand and watch.\f*
\v 9 And when those hired around the eleventh hour came, they each received a denarius.
\v 10 And when the first came they thought they would receive more, but they too each received a denarius.
\v 11 And when they received it they grumbled against the master of the house,\f + \fr 20:11 \ft The verb is the one the Greek Old Testament uses over and over for Israel grumbling in the wilderness against Moses and against God, after being fed (Exodus 16:7; Numbers 14:27). They have been paid in full and on time.\f*
\v 12 saying, 'These last ones worked one hour, and you made them equal to us, who bore the weight of the day and the burning heat.' \add [i.e. and the complaint is not that they were cheated; it is that somebody else was not]\add*
\v 13 But he answered one of them and said, 'Friend, I am doing you no wrong. Did you not agree with me on a denarius?\f + \fr 20:13 \ft "Friend" here is a cool, formal word, not a warm one, and this book uses it exactly three times: here, to the man with no wedding clothes (22:12), and to Judas in the garden (26:50). Every time, something is about to be exposed.\f*
\v 14 Take what is yours and go. I want to give to this last one the same as to you.
\v 15 Is it not permitted for me to do what I want with what is mine? Or is your eye grudging because I am good?'\f + \fr 20:15 \ft Literally, is your eye evil because I am good — the Hebrew idiom for a tight fist and a resentful heart (Deuteronomy 15:9; Proverbs 23:6), the same idiom used at 6:23. And "good" is the word Jesus reserved for God alone three paragraphs ago (19:17). The men were not wronged. They were made to watch generosity, and could not stand it.\f*
\v 16 So the last will be first, and the first last."\f + \fr 20:16 \ft Some later copies add "for many are called, but few chosen" — which the earliest copies do not have. The line is genuine, but it belongs at the close of a different parable (22:14), and a copyist has carried it back to this one.\f*
\s1 The Third Word About the Cross
\p
\v 17 And as Jesus was going up to Jerusalem he took the twelve disciples aside privately, and on the road he said to them,
\v 18 "Look — we are going up to Jerusalem, and the son of man will be handed over to the chief priests and scribes, and they will condemn him to death
\v 19 and will hand him over to the nations to be mocked and flogged and crucified; and on the third day he will be raised."\f + \fr 20:19 \ft The third time he has said this, and each time it comes into sharper focus. Now the word crucified is in it, and the Gentiles are in it. Handed over twice: by Israel's council to Rome. Both sets of hands are named.\f*
\s1 A Request for the Two Best Seats
\p
\v 20 Then the mother of the sons of Zebedee came to him with her sons, bowing down before him and asking something of him \add [i.e. immediately after the sentence you have just read]\add*.
\v 21 And he said to her, "What do you want?" She said to him, "Say that these two sons of mine may sit, one at your right and one at your left, in your kingdom."\f + \fr 20:21 \ft The right hand and the left hand of a throne are the two highest offices in a court — Bathsheba was seated at Solomon's right (1 Kings 2:19). He has just described being flogged and nailed up, and the request that follows is for the cabinet.\f*
\v 22 But Jesus answered and said, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am about to drink?" They said to him, "We can."\f + \fr 20:22 \ft In the prophets the cup is not a metaphor for privilege. It is what God hands a people to drink when judgment falls: "you who have drunk from the hand of the LORD the cup of his wrath" (Isaiah 51:17); "take this cup of the wine of wrath from my hand and make all the nations drink it" (Jeremiah 25:15). It is the cup he will beg to have taken away from him in the garden (26:39). They answer as though they have been asked whether they are up to the job.\f*
\v 23 He said to them, "You will indeed drink my cup, but to sit at my right and at my left is not mine to give; it is for those for whom it has been prepared by my Father." \add [i.e. and they will drink it — James is the first of the twelve to be killed]\add*
\s1 Not So Among You
\p
\v 24 And when the ten heard it they were indignant about the two brothers \add [i.e. not because the request was wrong, but because it was made first]\add*.
\v 25 But Jesus called them to himself and said, "You know that the rulers of the nations lord it over them, and their great men throw their weight around over them.
\v 26 It is not so among you. But whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant,
\v 27 and whoever wants to be first among you shall be your slave —\f + \fr 20:27 \ft The two Greek words go down, not up: a \tl diakonos\tl* is the man who waits at the table, and a \tl doulos\tl* is a slave, who owns nothing, not even himself. He does not abolish greatness among them. He turns it upside down and leaves the words standing.\f*
\v 28 just as the son of man did not come to be served but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many."\f + \fr 20:28 \ft Two things are packed into this. "Ransom" is Greek \tl lytron\tl*, the price paid to get a man out — the word-group used in the Greek Old Testament for God buying Israel out of Egypt (Deuteronomy 7:8). And Psalm 49 had said flatly that no man can pay it: "no one can ransom another, or give God the price of his life; it costs too much, and he must let it alone for ever" (Psalm 49:7-8). "For many" is Isaiah's servant, who "bore the sin of many" and "made many righteous" (Isaiah 53:11-12). And the figure doing it is Daniel's son of man, the one to whom all nations were to give service (Daniel 7:14) — who has come to do the serving.\f*
\s1 Two Blind Men Outside Jericho
\p
\v 29 And as they were going out from Jericho a large crowd followed him \add [i.e. fifteen miles of uphill road left, and then Jerusalem]\add*.
\v 30 And look — two blind men were sitting beside the road, and when they heard that Jesus was passing by they screamed out, "Have mercy on us, Lord, son of David!"
\v 31 And the crowd spoke harshly to them, that they should keep quiet; but they screamed all the more, "Have mercy on us, Lord, son of David!" \add [i.e. the same verb the disciples used on the people bringing children]\add*
\v 32 And Jesus stopped and called them and said, "What do you want me to do for you?"\f + \fr 20:32 \ft Word for word what he asked the mother of Zebedee's sons eleven verses ago. Set the two answers side by side: give my sons the two thrones, and let our eyes be opened. The men who cannot see have asked for the right thing from the right person, and the men who can see have not.\f*
\v 33 They said to him, "Lord, let our eyes be opened."
\v 34 And Jesus' heart went out to them, and he touched their eyes; and immediately they saw again and followed him \add [i.e. and where he is going is Jerusalem]\add*.
\c 21
\s1 The King Comes In on a Donkey
\p
\v 1 And when they came near to Jerusalem and came to Bethphage, to the Mount of Olives, then Jesus sent two disciples,\f + \fr 21:1 \ft Not any hill. "On that day his feet will stand on the Mount of Olives, which lies east of Jerusalem" — and then, in Zechariah, the LORD comes and is king over all the earth (Zechariah 14:4, 9).\f*
\v 2 saying to them, "Go into the village opposite you, and immediately you will find a donkey tied and a colt with her. Untie them and bring them to me.
\v 3 And if anyone says anything to you, say, 'The Lord has need of them,' and immediately he will send them."
\v 4 This happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled:
\v 5 "Say to the daughter of Zion, 'Look — your king is coming to you, gentle and mounted on a donkey, and on a colt, the foal of a beast of burden.'"\f + \fr 21:5 \ft Zechariah 9:9, with its opening line borrowed from Isaiah 62:11. Everything hangs on one word: gentle — the same word he used of himself at 11:29 and pronounced blessed at 5:5. A conquering general rides a horse. This is the animal a farmer rides, and Zechariah's next line says why: "I will cut off the chariot from Ephraim and the war horse from Jerusalem, and the battle bow will be cut off, and he will speak peace to the nations."\f*
\v 6 And the disciples went and did as Jesus had ordered them.
\v 7 They brought the donkey and the colt and put their garments on them, and he sat on them \add [i.e. making a saddle, and a throne, out of what they had on their backs — as Jehu's officers did with their cloaks, 2 Kings 9:13]\add*.
\v 8 And most of the crowd spread their own garments on the road, while others were cutting branches from the trees and spreading them on the road.\f + \fr 21:8 \ft This is how a city welcomes a king. When Simon Maccabeus took the citadel of Jerusalem, the people came in "with praise and palm branches, with harps and cymbals and songs" (1 Maccabees 13:51).\f*
\v 9 And the crowds going ahead of him and those following were screaming out, "Hosanna to the son of David! Blessed is the one who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!"\f + \fr 21:9 \ft "Hosanna" is Hebrew \tl hoshia-na\tl* — save, now! It comes from Psalm 118:25, and the line the crowd shouts next is the verse after it: "blessed is the one who comes in the name of the LORD" (Psalm 118:26). It was a pilgrim's psalm, sung going up to the feasts. What has changed is who it is being sung at.\f*
\p
\v 10 And when he came into Jerusalem the whole city was shaken, saying, "Who is this?"\f + \fr 21:10 \ft The verb is the one used for an earthquake — this writer uses it again when the ground shakes at the cross (27:51) and at the tomb (28:2). The last time Jerusalem reacted to this man's arrival it was also disturbed, and also asked who he was, and he was an infant at the time (2:3).\f*
\v 11 And the crowds were saying, "This is the prophet Jesus, from Nazareth of Galilee."
\s1 In the Temple
\p
\v 12 And Jesus went into the temple and threw out all those selling and buying in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those selling doves,\f + \fr 21:12 \ft All of this trade was legitimate in itself and necessary to the system. The temple tax had to be paid in Tyrian coin, so pilgrims needed a changer; doves were the offering the Torah set for the poor (Leviticus 5:7; 12:8). The problem is where it was all happening: the outer court, which was the one place a non-Jew was permitted to come and pray.\f*
\v 13 and he said to them, "It has been written: 'My house will be called a house of prayer,' but you are making it a cave of bandits."\f + \fr 21:13 \ft Two prophets in one sentence. The first is Isaiah 56:7, and the half of the line he does not quote is the half that bites: "my house will be called a house of prayer for all the nations" — which is the court they have filled with tables. The second is Jeremiah 7:11, out of the sermon Jeremiah preached standing in the temple gate, telling people it would not save them. A cave is where bandits go afterwards, to be safe. The word is not shoplifter; it is the word used of the two men crucified beside him (27:38).\f*
\v 14 And blind and lame men came to him in the temple, and he healed them.\f + \fr 21:14 \ft After David took Jerusalem a saying went round: "the blind and the lame will not come into the house" (2 Samuel 5:8). The son of David clears the traders out of the court and fills it with exactly those people.\f*
\v 15 But when the chief priests and the scribes saw the wonderful things he did, and the children screaming out in the temple, "Hosanna to the son of David!", they were indignant
\v 16 and said to him, "Do you hear what these are saying?" And Jesus said to them, "Yes. Have you never read, 'Out of the mouth of infants and nursing babies you have prepared praise'?"\f + \fr 21:16 \ft Psalm 8:2, where the praise coming out of the mouths of babies is praise God himself has arranged, and it is arranged to silence his enemies. He is not defending the children. He is telling the priests whose voice they are trying to stop.\f*
\v 17 And leaving them he went out of the city to Bethany and spent the night there \add [i.e. two miles over the hill, out of reach for the night]\add*.
\s1 The Fig Tree
\p
\v 18 Early in the morning, as he was going back into the city, he was hungry.
\v 19 And seeing one fig tree by the road he came to it and found nothing on it except leaves only, and he said to it, "Let no fruit ever come from you again." And immediately the fig tree withered.\f + \fr 21:19 \ft This is not temper; it is the kind of thing the prophets did with their bodies and their props — Jeremiah smashing a jar in the valley (Jeremiah 19), Ezekiel lying on his side for a year. And the fig tree is Israel in the prophets, over and over, always at the moment of coming up empty: "no grapes on the vine, no figs on the fig tree, and the leaf withered" (Jeremiah 8:13); "there is no cluster to eat, no first-ripe fig that I crave" (Micah 7:1). Note what the tree does have. Leaves, and nothing but leaves — everything that looks like fruitfulness and no fruit. He has just walked out of a temple like that.\f*
\v 20 And when the disciples saw it they were amazed, saying, "How did the fig tree wither immediately?"
\v 21 And Jesus answered and said to them, "Amen, I say to you, if you have trust and do not waver, you will not only do what was done to the fig tree, but even if you say to this mountain, 'Be lifted up and thrown into the sea,' it will happen \add [i.e. and he is standing on the Mount of Olives, looking at the temple mount]\add*.
\v 22 And everything you ask in prayer, trusting, you will receive."
\s1 By What Authority?
\p
\v 23 And when he came into the temple, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him as he was teaching and said, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?" \add [i.e. by whose warrant — because it was not ours]\add*
\v 24 And Jesus answered and said to them, "I too will ask you one thing, and if you tell me, I too will tell you by what authority I do these things.
\v 25 The immersion of John — where was it from? From heaven or from men?" And they reasoned among themselves, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say to us, 'Why then did you not trust him?'\f + \fr 21:25 \ft It is not a dodge; it is the same question. John announced him, John immersed him, and Jesus has called John the Elijah who was to come (11:14). Settle where John's authority came from and you have settled where his does.\f*
\v 26 But if we say, 'From men,' we are afraid of the crowd, for they all hold John to be a prophet."
\v 27 So they answered Jesus and said, "We do not know." And he said to them, "Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things.\f + \fr 21:27 \ft Watch what they actually do. They do not weigh the question; they count the cost of each answer and pick the one that costs least. "We do not know" is not ignorance. It is a refusal to be on record.\f*
\s1 Two Sons
\p
\v 28 But what do you think? A man had two children. He came to the first and said, 'Child, go work today in the vineyard.'
\v 29 And he answered and said, 'I do not want to'; but later he changed his mind and went.
\v 30 And he came to the other and said the same thing. And he answered and said, 'I will, sir,' and he did not go \add [i.e. the right words, the respectful address, and nothing else]\add*.
\v 31 Which of the two did the will of the father?" They said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Amen, I say to you, the tax collectors and the prostitutes are going ahead of you into the kingdom of God.\f + \fr 21:31 \ft They have just passed sentence on themselves, and the sentence is the one from the end of the sermon: "not everyone who says to me, Lord, Lord, will go into the kingdom, but the one who does the will of my Father" (7:21). The first son is the collectors and the prostitutes, who said no with their lives and then turned. The second son is the men he is speaking to.\f*
\v 32 For John came to you in the way of righteousness, and you did not trust him; but the tax collectors and the prostitutes trusted him. And you, seeing it, did not even change your minds later and trust him \add [i.e. you watched it happen to people you despised, and it moved you not at all]\add*.
\s1 The Vineyard and the Tenants
\p
\v 33 Hear another parable. There was a man, a master of a house, who planted a vineyard and put a fence around it and dug a winepress in it and built a tower; and he leased it to farmers and went away on a journey.\f + \fr 21:33 \ft Line for line out of Isaiah 5:1-2 — the digging, the planting, the tower, the winepress — and Isaiah says outright what the vineyard is: "the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel" (Isaiah 5:7). Everyone listening knows the song. But he has changed one thing, and it is the thing that matters. In Isaiah the vineyard itself is the failure. Here the vineyard is fine. It is the men left in charge of it who are the problem.\f*
\v 34 When the time of the fruits came near he sent his slaves to the farmers to get his fruits.
\v 35 And the farmers took his slaves; one they flogged, one they killed, one they stoned.\f + \fr 21:35 \ft The prophets, and their history. Zechariah son of Jehoiada was stoned in the temple court itself (2 Chronicles 24:20-21); Jeremiah was beaten and put in the stocks and let down into a cistern; Elijah's complaint was that Israel had killed the prophets with the sword (1 Kings 19:10). Jesus will lay the whole record out at 23:29-37.\f*
\v 36 Again he sent other slaves, more than the first, and they did the same to them.
\v 37 And finally he sent his son to them, saying, 'They will show respect for my son.'
\v 38 But when the farmers saw the son they said among themselves, 'This is the heir. Come, let us kill him and get his inheritance.' \add [i.e. they know exactly who he is; that is the reason they kill him]\add*
\v 39 And they took him and threw him out of the vineyard and killed him \add [i.e. thrown out first, killed after — as he will be taken outside the city walls]\add*.
\v 40 So when the lord of the vineyard comes, what will he do to those farmers?"
\v 41 They said to him, "He will bring those evil men to an evil end, and he will lease the vineyard to other farmers who will give him the fruits in their seasons." \add [i.e. and they have done it again — passed their own sentence out loud]\add*
\v 42 Jesus said to them, "Have you never read in the scriptures: 'The stone the builders rejected — this one has become the head of the corner. This came about from the Lord, and it is wonderful in our eyes'?\f + \fr 21:42 \ft Psalm 118:22-23 — the same psalm the crowd was shouting three days ago at the gate. The men whose whole office is to build have thrown out the one stone the building turns on, and God has put it at the corner anyway. Their verdict on him and God's verdict on him are in the same sentence, pointing opposite ways.\f*
\v 43 For this reason I say to you that the kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a nation producing its fruits.\f + \fr 21:43 \ft The vineyard is not destroyed; the tenancy changes hands. And the word for "nation" here is singular — a people — and it is not defined by descent. It is defined by the one thing this whole chapter has been about: whether anything grows. Which is what John said at the river, standing in the same country: God can raise up children for Abraham out of the stones (3:9).\f*
\v 44 And the one who falls on this stone will be broken to pieces, and the one it falls on it will crush to powder."\f + \fr 21:44 \ft Two more stones out of the scriptures are laid on top of the first. Isaiah's, which the LORD himself becomes — "a stone of stumbling and a rock of offence... and many will stumble on it and fall and be broken" (Isaiah 8:14-15). And Daniel's, the stone cut out by no human hand that smashes the statue of the empires to dust and grows into a mountain filling the earth (Daniel 2:34-35, 44-45).\f*
\p
\v 45 And when the chief priests and the Pharisees heard his parables they knew that he was speaking about them;
\v 46 and though they were seeking to seize him they were afraid of the crowds, because they held him to be a prophet.\f + \fr 21:46 \ft They understood the story perfectly, and what they did about it was start looking for a way to lay hands on him — which is the story. The only thing holding them back is a head-count in the street, the same calculation that produced "we do not know" twenty verses earlier.\f*
\c 22
\s1 The Wedding Feast
\p
\v 1 And Jesus answered and spoke to them again in parables, saying:
\v 2 "The kingdom of the heavens is like a man, a king, who made a wedding feast for his son.\f + \fr 22:2 \ft A wedding is how the prophets pictured God and his people: "I will betroth you to me for ever" (Hosea 2:19); "your Maker is your husband" (Isaiah 54:5); "as a bridegroom rejoices over the bride, so will your God rejoice over you" (Isaiah 62:5). And where the last two parables had a landowner, this one has a king, and the feast is for his son.\f*
\v 3 And he sent his slaves to call those who had been called to the wedding feast, and they did not want to come.\f + \fr 22:3 \ft An invitation went out twice: once well in advance, so the guests knew, and again on the day, when the food was on the table. These people have already accepted. The slaves are the second summons, and they are refused.\f*
\v 4 Again he sent other slaves, saying, 'Tell those who have been called: Look, I have prepared my dinner; my bulls and fattened cattle have been slaughtered and everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast.' \add [i.e. and this is after they have already said no once]\add*
\v 5 But they paid no attention and went off, one to his own field, another to his business,\f + \fr 22:5 \ft Nothing wicked here — a farm and a shop. That is the point. The invitation is not refused by rebellion but by other things being more pressing, which is how most refusals are made.\f*
\v 6 and the rest seized his slaves, treated them shamefully, and killed them.
\v 7 And the king was enraged, and he sent his soldiers and destroyed those murderers and burned their city.\f + \fr 22:7 \ft Foreign armies as God's instrument against his own people is an old pattern in Israel's records: Babylon "burned the house of the LORD and the king's house and all the houses of Jerusalem" (2 Kings 25:9); Assyria is "the rod of my anger" (Isaiah 10:5); "I will give this city into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he will burn it with fire" (Jeremiah 34:2). Rome burned Jerusalem in the year 70.\f*
\v 8 Then he said to his slaves, 'The wedding feast is ready, but those who were called were not worthy.
\v 9 So go to the crossroads of the highways and call whomever you find to the wedding feast.'
\v 10 And those slaves went out into the roads and gathered together everyone they found, both evil and good; and the wedding hall was filled with those reclining at table.\f + \fr 22:10 \ft "Both evil and good," and nobody at the door is sorting them. It is the dragnet again (13:47-48), which takes everything in the water and leaves the separating until the beach.\f*
\v 11 But when the king came in to look at those reclining, he saw there a man not dressed in wedding clothes,
\v 12 and he said to him, 'Friend, how did you come in here without wedding clothes?' And he had nothing to say.\f + \fr 22:12 \ft Men hauled in off a road do not own banquet clothes; a host on this scale supplied them, as Jehu supplied vestments to everyone entering the house of Baal (2 Kings 10:22). So the man has not failed to afford something. He has declined to put on what he was handed, and come in on his own terms. And when he is asked about it he has no answer at all — the verb means muzzled, and it is the same word used of the Sadducees three paragraphs later.\f*
\v 13 Then the king said to the servants, 'Tie him hand and foot and throw him out into the darkness outside; there will be weeping and grinding of teeth.'
\v 14 For many are called, but few chosen."\f + \fr 22:14 \ft The line is a summary of the story just told, not a doctrine dropped on the end of it. Everyone in the parable was called: the first guests, and then every man on the road. Some would not come. One came and would not wear what he was given. Being invited is not the same as being at the table when the king walks through.\f*
\s1 Caesar's Coin
\p
\v 15 Then the Pharisees went and took counsel how they might trap him in a word.
\v 16 And they sent their disciples to him with the Herodians, saying, "Teacher, we know that you are true and that you teach the way of God in truth, and it is no concern to you what anyone thinks, for you do not look at the face of men.\f + \fr 22:16 \ft The Herodians were the Herod family's party, and therefore Rome's. They and the Pharisees agreed on virtually nothing; the one thing that could put them in the same delegation is standing in front of them. And the flattery is part of the machine: having said out loud that he tells the truth no matter who is listening, they make it harder for him to give a careful answer.\f*
\v 17 So tell us, what do you think? Is it permitted to give the poll tax to Caesar, or not?"\f + \fr 22:17 \ft The head tax, paid by every person in the province straight into the Roman treasury. It was the most hated thing in the country: when it was first imposed, Judas the Galilean raised a revolt on the argument that paying it conceded that somebody other than God was king of Israel. Say yes and the crowd is gone. Say no and the men from Herod's party have what they came for.\f*
\v 18 But Jesus knew their evil and said, "Why are you testing me, hypocrites?
\v 19 Show me the coin of the poll tax." And they brought him a denarius \add [i.e. he does not have one; they do, in the temple]\add*.
\v 20 And he said to them, "Whose image is this, and whose inscription?"
\v 21 They said to him, "Caesar's." Then he said to them, "So give back to Caesar what is Caesar's, and to God what is God's."\f + \fr 22:21 \ft The word for image is the word used when God said, "let us make man in our image, according to our likeness" (Genesis 1:26-27). Ask whose image is on the coin and there is a second question standing behind it that he does not need to ask out loud. The coin carries Caesar's face, so give Caesar his coin back. The man holding it carries God's, so give God the man. The two claims are not the same size, and the question about the tax has stopped being the question.\f*
\v 22 And when they heard it they were amazed; and they left him and went away.
\s1 The Sadducees and the Resurrection
\p
\v 23 On that day Sadducees came to him — those who say there is no resurrection — and they questioned him,\f + \fr 22:23 \ft The priestly aristocracy, the men who ran the temple. They took only the five books of Moses as binding, rejected the oral traditions the Pharisees lived by, and held that there is no resurrection, no angel, and no spirit. His answer will come out of the books of Moses, because those are the only ones they will accept.\f*
\v 24 saying, "Teacher, Moses said, 'If someone dies having no children, his brother shall marry his wife and raise up seed for his brother.'\f + \fr 22:24 \ft Deuteronomy 25:5-6. If a man died childless his brother was to take the widow, and the first son of that marriage counted as the dead man's, so his name and his land were not lost out of Israel. It is the law behind the story of Ruth.\f*
\v 25 Now there were seven brothers among us. And the first married and came to his end, and having no seed he left his wife to his brother.
\v 26 So also the second and the third, up to the seventh.
\v 27 And last of all the woman died.
\v 28 In the resurrection, then, of the seven, whose wife will she be? For they all had her." \add [i.e. a puzzle built to make the whole idea look ridiculous, using their own Torah to build it]\add*
\v 29 And Jesus answered and said to them, "You are wandering off, because you do not know the scriptures or the power of God.
\v 30 For in the resurrection they neither marry nor are given in marriage, but are like angels in heaven \add [i.e. and the Sadducees do not believe in angels either]\add*.
\v 31 But about the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what was spoken to you by God, saying,
\v 32 'I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob'? He is not the God of the dead but of the living."\f + \fr 22:32 \ft Exodus 3:6, at the burning bush, and the whole argument is in the tense. God does not say I was their God. He says I am — and by then all three men had been in the ground for centuries. A man does not go on being the God of people who have ceased to exist. The resurrection is not being argued from philosophy; it is being argued from God's own faithfulness to men he made promises to.\f*
\v 33 And when the crowds heard it they were astounded at his teaching.
\s1 The Great Command
\p
\v 34 But when the Pharisees heard that he had muzzled the Sadducees, they gathered together in one place,
\v 35 and one of them, a lawyer, asked him a question, testing him:
\v 36 "Teacher, which command is great in the Torah?" \add [i.e. a real question among the teachers — six hundred and thirteen commands, and which one holds the rest]\add*
\v 37 And he said to him, "'You shall love the Lord your God with your whole heart and with your whole life and with your whole mind.'\f + \fr 22:37 \ft Deuteronomy 6:4-5, out of the Shema — the sentence every Jew said twice a day, the confession that made you a member of Israel. It is not one command among many; it is the ground the rest stand on.\f*
\v 38 This is the great and first command.
\v 39 And a second is like it: 'You shall love your neighbor as yourself.'\f + \fr 22:39 \ft Leviticus 19:18, and in Leviticus it sits in the middle of a list of very concrete things: pay a labourer the same day, do not curse the deaf, do not pervert justice for rich or poor, do not go about spreading tales. Loving a neighbour there is a set of actions, not a feeling. He was asked for one command and gives two, and calls the second like the first — which welds them together and means neither one can be had without the other.\f*
\v 40 On these two commands hangs the whole Torah, and the Prophets." \add [i.e. hangs, the way everything in a house hangs off the beam — take these away and the rest comes down]\add*
\s1 David's Son and David's Lord
\p
\v 41 While the Pharisees were gathered together, Jesus questioned them \add [i.e. and for the first time he is the one asking]\add*,
\v 42 saying, "What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he?" They said to him, "David's."
\v 43 He said to them, "How then does David in the Spirit call him Lord, saying,
\v 44 'The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet'?\f + \fr 22:44 \ft Psalm 110:1, taken by everyone in the room as being about the Christ. Count the people in the line: the LORD, who speaks; "my Lord," who is spoken to and told to sit at God's right hand; and David, who is listening and writing it down. A father in that world does not call his own descendant "my lord." The honour runs the other way.\f*
\v 45 If David, then, calls him Lord, how is he his son?"\f + \fr 22:45 \ft He is not denying that the Christ is David's son; this book opened by saying he is (1:1). He is saying that the title does not reach far enough. Whatever the Christ's authority is, David did not hand it down to him — God gave it, at his own right hand. Which is also, at last, the answer to the question the chief priests asked him in this temple two days ago: by what authority (21:23).\f*
\v 46 And no one was able to answer him a word, nor did anyone dare from that day on to question him any further \add [i.e. and from here to the end, nobody interrupts him again]\add*.
\c 23
\s1 They Sit on Moses' Seat
\p
\v 1 Then Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples,
\v 2 saying: "The scribes and the Pharisees have sat down on Moses' seat.\f + \fr 23:2 \ft An actual chair. Synagogues had a stone seat facing the congregation, from which the Torah was expounded; one has been dug up at Chorazin. To sit on it is to speak with Moses' authority, and he grants them that in the next breath.\f*
\v 3 So do and keep everything they tell you, but do not do according to their works, for they say and do not do \add [i.e. the second son of the parable, in office]\add*.
\v 4 They tie up heavy loads and put them on men's shoulders, but they themselves are not willing to move them with their finger.\f + \fr 23:4 \ft The same word he used of himself: "my load is light" (11:30). The charge is not that they teach the law but that they pile requirements on top of it and will not lift a finger to help anyone carry the pile — no leniency, no interpretation that eases it, no hand under the other end.\f*
\v 5 They do all their works to be watched by men: they make their phylacteries wide and their fringes long,\f + \fr 23:5 \ft The phylacteries are the small leather boxes of scripture bound on the arm and forehead for prayer, in obedience to "bind them as a sign on your hand" (Deuteronomy 6:8); the fringes are the tassels every man wore on the corners of his cloak, so that seeing them he would "remember all the commands of the LORD and do them" (Numbers 15:38-39). Jesus wore the fringes himself — a woman took hold of one (9:20). Both objects exist to remind the wearer. Enlarged, they remind everybody else.\f*
\v 6 and they love the place of honor at dinners and the front seats in the synagogues
\v 7 and the greetings in the marketplaces, and to be called 'Rabbi' by men.\f + \fr 23:7 \ft "Rabbi" is from the Hebrew for great: my great one. Line up the four things loved here and they are all the same thing — the couch nearest the host, the bench facing the room, the salute in the street, the title. Every one of them is a way of being seen to be senior.\f*
\v 8 But you are not to be called 'Rabbi,' for one is your teacher, and you are all brothers.
\v 9 And call no one on the earth your father, for one is your Father, the heavenly one.\f + \fr 23:9 \ft It is the title that is forbidden, not the parent; revered teachers were addressed as father. Three titles are banned in three verses, and each time for the same reason: the office named belongs to somebody else. One teacher, one Father, one leader — and everybody left over is a brother, which is a community with no rungs on it at all.\f*
\v 10 Nor are you to be called leaders, because your leader is one, the Christ.
\v 11 The greatest among you shall be your servant.
\v 12 And whoever lifts himself up will be brought low, and whoever brings himself low will be lifted up.
\s1 Woe to You, Scribes and Pharisees
\p
\v 13 Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you shut the kingdom of the heavens in front of men! For you do not go in yourselves, nor do you let those going in enter.\f + \fr 23:13 \ft "Woe" is the prophets' form — Isaiah rolls out six of them in a row (Isaiah 5:8-23), and Habakkuk five. It is not a curse. It is an announcement that the men addressed are standing somewhere very dangerous. There are seven here, and this is the first: men whose office was to open the scriptures are standing in the doorway. Note the tense of "those going in" — there are people on their way, and they are being blocked. Some later copies add a verse 14 at this point, about devouring widows' houses, which the earliest copies do not have.\f*
\p
\v 15 Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you cross sea and dry land to make one convert, and when he becomes one you make him twice as much a son of Gehenna as yourselves! \add [i.e. and the effort is real — the trouble is what he is being converted into]\add*
\p
\v 16 Woe to you, blind guides, who say, 'Whoever swears by the sanctuary, it is nothing; but whoever swears by the gold of the sanctuary is bound.'
\v 17 Fools and blind men! For which is greater, the gold or the sanctuary that made the gold holy?
\v 18 And, 'Whoever swears by the altar, it is nothing; but whoever swears by the gift on it is bound.'
\v 19 Blind men! For which is greater, the gift or the altar that makes the gift holy?
\v 20 So the one who swears by the altar swears by it and by everything on it,
\v 21 and the one who swears by the sanctuary swears by it and by the one who dwells in it,
\v 22 and the one who swears by heaven swears by the throne of God and by the one sitting on it.\f + \fr 23:22 \ft What is being described is a system of oaths with escape hatches built into them: swear one way and you are held to it, swear the other way and you are not, and only the expert knows which is which. He takes it apart by following the holiness back to its source. The gold is holy because of the sanctuary; the sanctuary is holy because of who lives in it. Every oath, traced back, ends at God — so there is no such thing as an oath that does not bind. Which is what he said in the sermon: do not swear at all, let yes be yes (5:33-37).\f*
\p
\v 23 Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you tithe mint and dill and cumin, and you have left aside the weightier matters of the Torah — justice and mercy and faithfulness. These you had to do, without leaving those aside.\f + \fr 23:23 \ft The tithe was owed on the harvest; extending it to the pot herbs on the windowsill was going beyond what was asked, and he does not tell them to stop. He tells them what they walked past to get there. The three he names are the prophets' three: "he has told you, man, what is good, and what the LORD asks of you — to do justice, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God" (Micah 6:8), and "mercy" is the word from Hosea 6:6, which he has now thrown at these men three times (9:13; 12:7).\f*
\v 24 Blind guides, straining out the gnat and gulping down the camel!\f + \fr 23:24 \ft You strained your wine through a cloth so as not to swallow an insect, because insects were unclean (Leviticus 11:20-23). A camel is also unclean, and it is the largest animal in the country. In Aramaic the two words sound almost the same — \tl galma\tl*, a gnat, and \tl gamla\tl*, a camel.\f*
\p
\v 25 Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you clean the outside of the cup and the dish, but inside they are full of plunder and lack of self-control.
\v 26 Blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup, so that its outside may become clean as well \add [i.e. and it only runs that way — scouring the outside does nothing to what is in it]\add*.
\p
\v 27 Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you are like whitewashed tombs, which on the outside look beautiful but inside are full of dead men's bones and every kind of uncleanness.\f + \fr 23:27 \ft Tombs were whitewashed before Passover for a practical reason: touching a grave made a man unclean for seven days (Numbers 19:16), and the pilgrims coming up to the feast had to be able to see them and keep clear. So the whitewash is a warning sign that happens to look handsome. What is inside is exactly what a man must not touch.\f*
\v 28 In the same way you also on the outside look righteous to men, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness \add [i.e. lawlessness — said of the most careful keepers of the law in the country]\add*.
\p
\v 29 Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the graves of the righteous,
\v 30 and you say, 'If we had been in the days of our fathers, we would not have shared with them in the blood of the prophets.'
\v 31 So you testify against yourselves that you are sons of those who murdered the prophets.\f + \fr 23:31 \ft They said "our fathers," and that is the whole case. In Hebrew usage a son of something shares its nature, not just its bloodline. They are honouring dead prophets with masonry while arranging the death of the living one standing in front of them (21:46; 22:15).\f*
\v 32 Fill up, then, the measure of your fathers!\f + \fr 23:32 \ft There is such a thing in the scriptures as a measure that has to be full before judgment falls: God tells Abraham that his descendants must wait, "for the iniquity of the Amorites is not yet complete" (Genesis 15:16). Finish it, he says. Finish what they started.\f*
\v 33 Snakes, brood of vipers, how will you flee from the judgment of Gehenna? \add [i.e. John's words at the river, and John's question too: who warned you to flee (3:7)]\add*
\s1 The Blood of the Prophets
\p
\v 34 For this reason, look — I am sending you prophets and wise men and scribes. Some of them you will kill and crucify, and some of them you will flog in your synagogues and chase from city to city,\f + \fr 23:34 \ft "I am sending" — he says it himself, and the categories are Israel's own. What he describes is what happened: Stephen stoned, the assemblies scattered, men flogged in synagogues and run out of one town after another. The people first reading this book had watched it.\f*
\v 35 so that on you may come all the righteous blood poured out on the land, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Barachiah, whom you murdered between the sanctuary and the altar.\f + \fr 23:35 \ft Abel is the first man murdered in the scriptures (Genesis 4:8). Zechariah, stoned in the temple court on the king's orders and crying out as he died, "may the LORD see it and call it to account," is in 2 Chronicles 24:20-22 — and in the Hebrew arrangement of the scriptures, Chronicles is the last book. From Abel to Zechariah is not a span of years. It is the span of the Bible, front cover to back. (The Zechariah killed in the temple was the son of Jehoiada; the prophet Zechariah was the son of Berechiah. The two names have run together.)\f*
\v 36 Amen, I say to you, all these things will come on this generation.
\s1 Jerusalem, Jerusalem
\p
\v 37 Jerusalem, Jerusalem, who kills the prophets and stones those sent to her — how often I wanted to gather your children together, the way a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you were not willing!\f + \fr 23:37 \ft The wings are God's wings. "Hide me in the shadow of your wings" (Psalm 17:8); "he will cover you with his feathers, and under his wings you will find refuge" (Psalm 91:4); Ruth came "under whose wings you have come to take refuge" (Ruth 2:12). He puts himself in that place — and then says the one thing that has been true through this entire book: I wanted to, and you would not.\f*
\v 38 Look — your house is left to you deserted.\f + \fr 23:38 \ft Two days ago, standing in it, he called the temple "my house" (21:13). Now it is yours. Ezekiel watched this happen once before: the glory of the LORD lifting off the cherubim, moving to the threshold, then to the east gate, then out of the city to the mountain on the east — which is the mountain he is about to sit down on (Ezekiel 10-11; Matthew 24:3). "I have forsaken my house, I have abandoned my heritage" (Jeremiah 12:7).\f*
\v 39 For I say to you, you will never see me from now on until you say, 'Blessed is the one who comes in the name of the Lord.'"\f + \fr 23:39 \ft The very words the crowds were shouting at the gate a few days ago (21:9), out of Psalm 118:26. He gives them back as a condition. The word to weigh is "until": what is announced is not an ending but an absence with a term on it, and the term is that this city says of him what it once shouted and did not mean.\f*
\c 24
\s1 Not One Stone
\p
\v 1 And Jesus went out from the temple and was going away, and his disciples came to point out to him the buildings of the temple \add [i.e. he walks out of it, which is what he said would happen to it]\add*.
\v 2 But he answered and said to them, "Do you not see all these things? Amen, I say to you, not one stone will be left here on another that will not be thrown down."\f + \fr 24:2 \ft Herod's temple was one of the wonders of the world — white stone and gold, visible for miles, with foundation blocks weighing many tons. There was a saying that a man who had not seen it had never seen a beautiful building. The disciples are pointing at the most permanent-looking thing in their world. The verb "thrown down" is the one Jesus used at 5:17 of what he did not come to do to the Torah. The law stands; the building does not.\f*
\p
\v 3 And as he was sitting on the Mount of Olives the disciples came to him privately, saying, "Tell us, when will these things be, and what will be the sign of your coming and of the completion of the age?"\f + \fr 24:3 \ft The hill east of the city, looking straight across at the temple — and the place where Ezekiel watched the glory of the LORD come to rest after it left the house (Ezekiel 11:23). "Your coming" is Greek \tl parousia\tl*, the word for a king's state arrival at a city, with all that went with it: the reception, the judgments, the taking up of authority. The disciples ask three things in one breath, and they almost certainly assume the three are one event. Whether they are is what the answer sorts out.\f*
\s1 Do Not Be Led Astray
\p
\v 4 And Jesus answered and said to them, "Watch out that no one leads you astray.
\v 5 For many will come in my name, saying, 'I am the Christ,' and they will lead many astray.\f + \fr 24:5 \ft The first thing he says is not a prediction but a warning, and it is about them. The country was full of such men in these decades: Theudas, who took a crowd out to the Jordan promising to part it; the Egyptian, who led thousands to the Mount of Olives and said the walls of Jerusalem would fall at his word. Their appearing is not the sign that the end has come. Their appearing is part of what has to be got through.\f*
\v 6 And you are going to hear of wars and reports of wars. See that you are not shaken, for it must happen, but the end is not yet.
\v 7 For nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom, and there will be famines and shakings in place after place.
\v 8 But all these are the beginning of birth pains.\f + \fr 24:8 \ft The prophets' image, and it is chosen carefully (Isaiah 13:8; 26:17; Jeremiah 4:31; Micah 4:9-10). Labour pains are not the pain of something falling apart. They are the pain of something being born, they get worse before they are over, and they mean the thing is coming. "Beginning" is the load-bearing word: this is the early part.\f*
\s1 What You Will Go Through
\p
\v 9 Then they will hand you over to pressure and will kill you, and you will be hated by all the nations because of my name \add [i.e. the same verb used of what is done to him — handed over]\add*.
\v 10 And then many will be tripped up, and they will hand one another over and will hate one another.
\v 11 And many false prophets will rise up and lead many astray.
\v 12 And because lawlessness is multiplied, the love of the many will grow cold.\f + \fr 24:12 \ft Read verses 10-12 as one thing happening. The pressure comes from outside; what it does is on the inside. People fall away, and then start handing each other over, and the love that held the community together cools like a fire nobody is feeding. That is the real danger being described, not the armies.\f*
\v 13 But the one who endures to the end — this one will be rescued.\f + \fr 24:13 \ft The identical sentence he gave them at 10:22, and the centre of this whole discourse. Not a promise of being spared any of it; a promise attached to staying under it. "Endure" means to remain where you are put and not go out from under the weight.\f*
\v 14 And this good news of the kingdom will be proclaimed in the whole inhabited world, as a witness to all the nations, and then the end will come \add [i.e. the first firm marker he gives — not wars, not famines; this]\add*.
\s1 When You See the Abomination
\p
\v 15 So when you see the abomination of desolation, spoken of through Daniel the prophet, standing in the holy place — let the reader understand —\f + \fr 24:15 \ft The phrase is Daniel's (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11), and it had already happened once. In 167 BC Antiochus Epiphanes set up an altar to Zeus on the altar of burnt offering in the temple and sacrificed a pig on it (1 Maccabees 1:54-59). "Abomination" is the standing word for an idolatrous thing put where it does not belong. He is telling them to watch for the pattern happening again. And "let the reader understand" is an aside over the heads of the men on the hillside, to whoever is holding this book: work out what this means in your own time.\f*
\v 16 then let those in Judea flee to the mountains.
\v 17 Let the one on the roof not go down to take the things out of his house,
\v 18 and let the one in the field not turn back to get his cloak \add [i.e. down the outside stair and go; do not go in for anything]\add*.
\v 19 And woe to those who are pregnant and to those nursing in those days!
\v 20 Pray that your flight may not happen in winter or on a Sabbath.\f + \fr 24:20 \ft In winter the dry valleys of Judea run as torrents and the hills are bitter. On a Sabbath, how far a man may walk is limited by law and the town gates may be shut. Both details assume people who keep the Sabbath, in Judea, with somewhere to run to.\f*
\v 21 For then there will be great pressure, such as has not happened from the beginning of the world until now, nor ever will.\f + \fr 24:21 \ft Daniel's words: "there will be a time of trouble such as never has been since there was a nation" (Daniel 12:1). Josephus, who was there for the siege of Jerusalem, wrote that no other city ever suffered so much, using almost the same phrase.\f*
\v 22 And unless those days were cut short, no flesh would be rescued; but for the sake of the chosen those days will be cut short.\f + \fr 24:22 \ft "The chosen" is the Greek Old Testament's word for Israel — "my chosen people" (Isaiah 43:20). What is said is that the length of the disaster is decided by God, and decided with these people in view. The severity is not softened; the clock is.\f*
\s1 Do Not Believe It
\p
\v 23 Then if anyone says to you, 'Look, here is the Christ!' or, 'Here!' — do not trust it.
\v 24 For false christs and false prophets will rise up and will give great signs and wonders so as to lead astray, if possible, even the chosen.\f + \fr 24:24 \ft Moses had settled this long before: a prophet may give you a sign, and the sign may even come off, and if what he then tells you is to go after other gods you are not to listen to him (Deuteronomy 13:1-3). Wonders prove nothing on their own. Note also "if possible" — the attempt is certain, the success is not.\f*
\v 25 Look — I have told you ahead of time.
\v 26 So if they say to you, 'Look, he is in the wilderness,' do not go out; 'Look, he is in the inner rooms,' do not trust it \add [i.e. out in the desert, where the pretenders led their crowds, or hidden in a back room, known only to insiders]\add*.
\v 27 For just as the lightning comes out from the east and shines as far as the west, so will be the coming of the son of man \add [i.e. nobody has to point lightning out to you]\add*.
\v 28 Wherever the corpse is, there the vultures will be gathered.\f + \fr 24:28 \ft A proverb, and in this position it makes the same point as the lightning: you do not need an insider to tell you where it is happening. You can see the birds from a long way off. The Greek word can be eagles as well as vultures, and the eagle was what the Roman legions carried on their standards.\f*
\s1 The Son of Man Appears
\p
\v 29 And immediately after the pressure of those days the sun will be darkened and the moon will not give its light, and the stars will fall from the sky, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken.\f + \fr 24:29 \ft This is not new language and it is not, in the prophets, about astronomy. "The stars of the heavens will not give their light, the sun will be dark at its rising" is Isaiah on the fall of Babylon (Isaiah 13:10). "I will cover the heavens and make their stars dark" is Ezekiel on the fall of Egypt (Ezekiel 32:7). Joel uses it too (Joel 2:10). It is the standing idiom for a world order coming down — the lights that stood for a regime's permanence going out.\f*
\v 30 And then the sign of the son of man will appear in the sky, and then all the tribes of the land will beat their breasts, and they will see the son of man coming on the clouds of the sky with power and great glory.\f + \fr 24:30 \ft They asked for the sign (v. 3); the sign turns out to be him. "Coming on the clouds" is Daniel 7:13-14 — and in Daniel the one like a son of man is coming toward the Ancient of Days, to be handed dominion and glory and a kingdom that will not pass away. It is a throne scene. The mourning is Zechariah's: "they will look on me, on him whom they pierced, and they will mourn... each family by itself" (Zechariah 12:10-12), and there it is the families of the land, mourning what they have done.\f*
\v 31 And he will send out his angels with a great trumpet, and they will gather together his chosen from the four winds, from one end of the heavens to the other.\f + \fr 24:31 \ft "In that day a great trumpet will be blown, and those who were lost in Assyria and those driven out to Egypt will come" (Isaiah 27:13). The trumpet is the exiles' trumpet, and what follows it is the scattered being brought home.\f*
\s1 The Fig Tree, and This Generation
\p
\v 32 Learn the parable from the fig tree: when its branch has already become tender and it puts out leaves, you know that summer is near.
\v 33 So also you, when you see all these things, know that he is near, at the doors \add [i.e. the fig is the last tree in the country to leaf; when it goes, there is no doubt left]\add*.
\v 34 Amen, I say to you, this generation will not pass away until all these things have happened.\f + \fr 24:34 \ft "This generation" is not a loose phrase in this book. It is what he calls the people in front of him, every time, and always as an accusation: 11:16; 12:39-45; 16:4; 17:17; and 23:36, where "all these things will come on this generation" is said of the blood of the prophets. The negative here is the strongest Greek has. The temple came down about forty years after he said it.\f*
\v 35 The sky and the earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away.
\s1 No One Knows the Day
\p
\v 36 But about that day and hour no one knows — not even the angels of the heavens, nor the Son — but the Father only.\f + \fr 24:36 \ft Something changes here, and the "but" is where it turns. Up to now he has been describing things that can be seen coming, with instructions for what to do when you see them, inside a stated span. From here on there is a day with no signs at all attached to it and no timetable, which not even he is given. Whatever else follows from that, this follows: nobody who claims to have worked out the date is telling the truth.\f*
\v 37 For just as the days of Noah were, so will be the coming of the son of man.
\v 38 For as in those days before the flood they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day Noah went into the ark,
\v 39 and they did not know until the flood came and took them all away — so will be the coming of the son of man.\f + \fr 24:39 \ft The charge against Noah's generation here is not wickedness. It is that they had no idea. Eating, drinking and marrying are not sins; they are what ordinary life is made of, and it went on right up to the day the door shut. The one man who had been warned had acted on the warning years before.\f*
\v 40 Then two men will be in the field; one is taken and one is left.
\v 41 Two women will be grinding at the mill; one is taken and one is left.\f + \fr 24:41 \ft Two people at the same work, side by side, and one of them goes. Which one is the fortunate one is not stated. Read against the sentence just before it, where the flood "took them all away," being taken is not obviously the better half of the pair.\f*
\s1 Stay Awake
\p
\v 42 So stay awake, because you do not know on what day your Lord is coming.
\v 43 But know this: if the master of the house had known in which watch the thief was coming, he would have stayed awake and would not have let his house be dug through \add [i.e. and no householder has ever known that]\add*.
\v 44 For this reason you too must be ready, because the son of man is coming at an hour you do not expect.
\s1 The Slave Left in Charge
\p
\v 45 Who then is the trustworthy and sensible slave whom the master set over his household to give them their food at the right time?\f + \fr 24:45 \ft It is a real question, put to the men listening. The slave in it is not a slave among slaves; he has been put over the others, and his one job is to see that they are fed. Everything that follows turns on what a man does with authority when the person who gave it to him is not in the house.\f*
\v 46 Blessed is that slave whom his master finds doing so when he comes.
\v 47 Amen, I say to you, he will set him over all his belongings.
\v 48 But if that evil slave says in his heart, 'My master is taking a long time,' \add [i.e. the same slave, and this is the sentence that turns him]\add*
\v 49 and begins to beat his fellow slaves and eats and drinks with the drunkards,
\v 50 the master of that slave will come on a day he does not expect and at an hour he does not know,
\v 51 and he will cut him in two and put his portion with the hypocrites; there will be weeping and grinding of teeth.\f + \fr 24:51 \ft The delay did not corrupt him; it uncovered him. The man put in charge of feeding people is found beating them and drinking with the wrong company. And where he ends up is named precisely: with the hypocrites — the actors, the word that has been ringing through the last two chapters. He was an insider, and that is why the sentence is what it is.\f*
\c 25
\s1 Ten Young Women
\p
\v 1 Then the kingdom of the heavens will be like ten young women who took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.\f + \fr 25:1 \ft The bridegroom came at night to fetch the bride from her house, and her companions waited with lamps to meet the party and go in with it. There is no scene change here — he is still on the hillside, still answering the same question.\f*
\v 2 Five of them were foolish and five were sensible.\f + \fr 25:2 \ft The two words come straight out of the end of the sermon: the sensible man built on rock because he heard the words and did them, and the foolish man built on sand because he heard them and did not (7:24-27). In this book the difference between wise and foolish is never brains. It is whether hearing turned into doing.\f*
\v 3 For the foolish ones took their lamps and took no oil with them,
\v 4 but the sensible ones took oil in flasks along with their lamps \add [i.e. all ten had lamps, all ten went out, and until the wait got long nobody could have told them apart]\add*.
\v 5 And while the bridegroom was taking a long time they all became drowsy and were sleeping \add [i.e. all ten — the sensible ones fall asleep too, and nothing is said against it]\add*.
\v 6 But in the middle of the night there was a shout: 'Look — the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!'
\v 7 Then all those young women got up and put their lamps in order.
\v 8 And the foolish ones said to the sensible ones, 'Give us some of your oil, because our lamps are going out.'
\v 9 But the sensible ones answered, saying, 'There may not be enough for us and for you. Go instead to those who sell, and buy for yourselves.'\f + \fr 25:9 \ft This is the point of the whole thing, and it is not meanness. Whatever the oil stands for — the readiness that lasts, the doing that goes with the hearing — it cannot be handed across at the last moment. Each has to have brought her own, and the time for getting it was earlier. "Go and buy" is not advice; at midnight there is nobody to buy from.\f*
\v 10 And while they were going away to buy, the bridegroom came, and those who were ready went in with him to the wedding feast, and the door was shut.
\v 11 Later the rest of the young women came too, saying, 'Lord, lord, open for us!'
\v 12 But he answered and said, 'Amen, I say to you, I do not know you.'\f + \fr 25:12 \ft Almost word for word what was said at the end of the sermon: "many will say to me on that day, Lord, Lord..." and the answer, "I never knew you" (7:21-23). Both times the people are certain they belong. Both times the reply is not "you were late" but "there is no relationship here" — which is what a teacher said to sever a connection with a student.\f*
\v 13 So stay awake, because you know neither the day nor the hour.\f + \fr 25:13 \ft Some later copies add "in which the son of man comes" — which the earliest copies do not have.\f*
\s1 The Talents
\p
\v 14 For it is like a man going away on a journey, who called his own slaves and handed over his belongings to them.
\v 15 To one he gave five talents, to another two, to another one\f + \fr 25:15 \ft A talent was about seventy-five pounds of silver, or six thousand denarii — twenty years of a labourer's wages. Even the man with one has a fortune in his hands. And the amounts are not equal because the men are not: each is given what he can handle, which means none of them is set up to fail.\f* — to each according to his own ability — and he went away on the journey. Immediately
\v 16 the one who had received the five talents went and worked with them and gained another five.
\v 17 In the same way the one with the two gained another two.
\v 18 But the one who had received the one went off and dug in the ground and hid his master's silver.\f + \fr 25:18 \ft Burying money was the ordinary way to keep it safe, and under Jewish law a man who buried a deposit had discharged his duty of care. He has not stolen anything, wasted anything or lost anything. That is exactly what makes the verdict on him worth reading twice.\f*
\v 19 After a long time the master of those slaves came and settled accounts with them.
\v 20 And the one who had received the five talents came and brought another five talents, saying, 'Master, you handed over five talents to me. Look — I have gained another five talents.'
\v 21 His master said to him, 'Well done, good and trustworthy slave. You were trustworthy over a few things; I will set you over many things. Come into the joy of your master.'
\v 22 And the one with the two talents also came and said, 'Master, you handed over two talents to me. Look — I have gained another two talents.'
\v 23 His master said to him, 'Well done, good and trustworthy slave. You were trustworthy over a few things; I will set you over many things. Come into the joy of your master.'\f + \fr 25:23 \ft Read the two speeches side by side: they are identical, syllable for syllable. The man who made five is not commended one word more warmly than the man who made two. The size of the return is not what is being weighed. And note what the master calls five talents — "a few things."\f*
\v 24 And the one who had received the one talent also came and said, 'Master, I knew you, that you are a hard man, reaping where you did not sow and gathering where you did not scatter.
\v 25 So I was afraid, and I went and hid your talent in the ground. Look — you have what is yours.'
\v 26 And his master answered and said to him, 'Evil and lazy slave! You knew that I reap where I did not sow and gather where I did not scatter.
\v 27 So you should have put my silver with the bankers, and when I came I would have gotten back what is mine with interest.\f + \fr 25:27 \ft The master does not argue with the man's description of him. He takes it and turns it round: if that is who you thought I was, then act like it. And what he asks for at the end is the least possible effort — not enterprise, not risk, just handing the money to a moneychanger's table so it earns something. The man did not fall short of a high standard. He did nothing at all, and that is the charge: not theft, and not waste. Lazy.\f*
\v 28 So take the talent away from him and give it to the one who has the ten talents.
\v 29 For to everyone who has, more will be given, and he will have plenty; but from the one who does not have, even what he has will be taken away \add [i.e. the same rule stated at 13:12 about hearing, now applied to working]\add*.
\v 30 And throw the useless slave out into the darkness outside; there will be weeping and grinding of teeth.'\f + \fr 25:30 \ft The word is not "wicked" here but "useless" — a man who produced no benefit. And every person thrown into the darkness outside in this book was on the inside first: a son of the kingdom (8:12), a guest at the wedding (22:13), and now a slave of the household who was trusted with his master's money.\f*
\s1 Sheep and Goats
\p
\v 31 When the son of man comes in his glory, and all the angels with him, then he will sit on the throne of his glory \add [i.e. and this is no longer "it is like" — it is stated straight]\add*.
\v 32 And all the nations will be gathered together in front of him, and he will separate them from one another as the shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.\f + \fr 25:32 \ft Sheep and goats grazed together and were parted at evening, because goats need shelter from the cold; it was a job a shepherd did every night without thinking about it. And the image comes from Ezekiel 34, where God says, "I will judge between sheep and sheep, between rams and goats" — and the thing he judges them on is how the strong ones treated the weak: "you push with side and shoulder and thrust at all the weak with your horns" (Ezekiel 34:17, 21).\f*
\v 33 And he will stand the sheep at his right, but the goats at his left.
\v 34 Then the king will say to those at his right, 'Come, you who have been blessed by my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the founding of the world \add [i.e. not a reward improvised at the door — a place made ready before anyone was born]\add*.
\v 35 For I was hungry and you gave me something to eat; I was thirsty and you gave me something to drink; I was a stranger and you took me in;
\v 36 naked, and you clothed me; I was sick and you looked after me; I was in prison and you came to me.'\f + \fr 25:36 \ft Six things, and not one of them is a religious act. No prayer, no fast, no offering, no confession of anything. It is Isaiah's list: "is not this the fast I choose — to share your bread with the hungry, to bring the homeless poor into your house, when you see the naked to cover him?" (Isaiah 58:6-7). And note the pronoun. He does not say you fed the hungry. He says you fed me.\f*
\v 37 Then the righteous will answer him, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you, or thirsty and give you a drink?
\v 38 And when did we see you a stranger and take you in, or naked and clothe you?
\v 39 And when did we see you sick or in prison and come to you?'\f + \fr 25:39 \ft Their astonishment is the proof that the thing was real. They were not keeping score, and they did not know it counted. Set that against the men who sounded a trumpet before giving alms so that people would watch (6:2). These fed a hungry man because he was hungry, and are told at the end whose face it was.\f*
\v 40 And the king will answer and say to them, 'Amen, I say to you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers of mine, you did for me.'\f + \fr 25:40 \ft "The least of these" is this book's language for the small and the overlooked — "these little ones" who must not be tripped up or thought little of (18:6, 10, 14), the ones a cup of cold water is given to (10:42). And "my brothers" is what he calls those who do the will of his Father (12:50). So the most direct reading is that the nations are judged on how they treated his own people when those people were hungry and in prison — which is the sentence he passed at 10:40-42. The needs listed, though, are the needs of anybody, and the prophets aimed this demand at everyone. Either way the mechanism is the same: he is in the person in front of you, and what is done to that person is done to him.\f*
\v 41 Then he will say to those at his left, 'Go away from me, you who have been cursed, into the fire of the age prepared for the devil and his angels.\f + \fr 25:41 \ft Read the two "prepared"s together. The kingdom was prepared for you; the fire was prepared for the devil and his angels — not for these. They are going somewhere that was never made for them.\f*
\v 42 For I was hungry and you did not give me anything to eat; I was thirsty and you did not give me anything to drink;
\v 43 I was a stranger and you did not take me in; naked, and you did not clothe me; sick and in prison, and you did not look after me.'
\v 44 Then they too will answer, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and did not serve you?'
\v 45 Then he will answer them, saying, 'Amen, I say to you, whatever you did not do for one of the least of these, you did not do for me.'\f + \fr 25:45 \ft Nothing is charged against them. They did not beat anybody, rob anybody or drive anybody out. They walked past. It is the same charge as against the man who buried the talent and the women who brought no oil: not what was done, what was not.\f*
\v 46 And these will go away into the punishment of the age, but the righteous into the life of the age."\f + \fr 25:46 \ft The same adjective stands on both halves of the sentence, in the same construction — whatever it says about the one it says about the other, and the text does not distinguish between them. On the word itself, and the two ages behind it, see the note at 18:8.\f*
\c 26
\s1 Two Days to Passover
\p
\v 1 And it happened, when Jesus had finished all these words, that he said to his disciples,\f + \fr 26:1 \ft The fifth and last time this book uses the sentence (7:28; 11:1; 13:53; 19:1), and the only time it says "all these words." The teaching is over. From here there is nothing but events.\f*
\v 2 "You know that after two days the Passover comes, and the son of man is handed over to be crucified."\f + \fr 26:2 \ft The verb is not future but present — he is being handed over, already. Passover was the night the LORD went through Egypt and passed over the houses marked with the blood of a lamb, and Israel came out of slavery (Exodus 12). He puts his own death and that night in the same sentence, and says nothing more about it.\f*
\s1 The Priests Take Counsel
\p
\v 3 Then the chief priests and the elders of the people gathered together in the courtyard of the high priest, who was called Caiaphas,\f + \fr 26:3 \ft Caiaphas held the office some eighteen years, appointed by Rome and kept there by Pilate. The high priesthood by this date was a political post in Roman gift, not the succession the Torah laid down. The two scenes run at once: Jesus telling his disciples what is going to happen, and these men in another room arranging it.\f*
\v 4 and they took counsel together to seize Jesus by deceit and kill him.
\v 5 But they were saying, "Not during the feast, or there may be an uproar among the people." \add [i.e. they intend to control the timing, and they will not]\add*
\s1 Oil Poured Over His Head
\p
\v 6 Now when Jesus was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper \add [i.e. at the table of a man named by his uncleanness]\add*,
\v 7 a woman came to him with an alabaster jar of very costly perfumed oil, and she poured it out on his head as he reclined at table.\f + \fr 26:7 \ft Pouring oil over a man's head is not perfume. It is what Samuel did to Saul and to David, and Zadok to Solomon (1 Samuel 10:1; 16:13; 1 Kings 1:39) — it is how a king was made, and the word Christ means exactly that, anointed. While the priests two miles away are planning his death, an unnamed woman performs the act his title is named after.\f*
\v 8 But when the disciples saw it they were indignant, saying, "Why this waste?
\v 9 For this could have been sold for a great deal and given to the poor."
\v 10 But Jesus knew it and said to them, "Why are you making trouble for the woman? She has done a good work for me.
\v 11 For you always have the poor with you, but you do not always have me.\f + \fr 26:11 \ft He is quoting Deuteronomy 15:11 — "there will never cease to be poor in the land, therefore I command you to open your hand" — which is a command to give, not an excuse not to. He is not waving the poor away. He is saying that this hour will not come again.\f*
\v 12 For when she poured this oil on my body, she did it to prepare me for burial.\f + \fr 26:12 \ft The word means to lay a body out for the grave — washing, wrapping, anointing. Whether she knew it is not said. He tells the room what her act was: the same oil, in the same gesture, makes a king and readies a corpse, and here it does both at once.\f*
\v 13 Amen, I say to you, wherever this good news is proclaimed in the whole world, what she did will also be told, in remembrance of her." \add [i.e. and the disciples who objected are not named in the story; she is not named either]\add*
\s1 Thirty Pieces of Silver
\p
\v 14 Then one of the twelve, the one called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests
\v 15 and said, "What are you willing to give me, and I will hand him over to you?" And they weighed out for him thirty pieces of silver.\f + \fr 26:15 \ft Thirty pieces of silver is what the Torah says a man must pay when his ox gores somebody's slave (Exodus 21:32) — the price of a slave, and no more. And it is the sum in Zechariah, weighed out to the shepherd God had sent as his wages, which the LORD calls "the lordly price at which I was valued by them" and tells him to throw to the potter (Zechariah 11:12-13). The book comes back to that at 27:3-10.\f*
\v 16 And from then on he was looking for a good moment to hand him over.
\s1 The Passover Is Prepared
\p
\v 17 On the first day of Unleavened Bread the disciples came to Jesus, saying, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
\v 18 And he said, "Go into the city to a certain man and say to him, 'The teacher says: My time is near; I am keeping the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"\f + \fr 26:18 \ft "My time" is Greek \tl kairos\tl* — not the hours on a clock but the appointed moment, the one everything has been running toward. And note that the arrangement is already made. The priests are hunting for an opportunity; he has booked a room.\f*
\v 19 And the disciples did as Jesus had ordered them, and they prepared the Passover.
\s1 One of You Will Hand Me Over
\p
\v 20 When evening came he was reclining at table with the twelve.\f + \fr 26:20 \ft Reclining is the point of the posture. A slave stands to eat; a free man leans on his elbow. The rule was that even the poorest man in Israel must recline at this meal, because the meal is about coming out of slavery.\f*
\v 21 And while they were eating he said, "Amen, I say to you, one of you will hand me over."
\v 22 And they were deeply grieved, and each one began to say to him, "It is not I, Lord, is it?" \add [i.e. asked the way you ask when you expect the answer no, and are not quite sure of it]\add*
\v 23 And he answered and said, "The one who dipped his hand with me in the bowl — this one will hand me over.\f + \fr 26:23 \ft "Even my close friend whom I trusted, who ate my bread, has lifted his heel against me" (Psalm 41:9). Sharing a dish was not incidental contact; it made a bond. To do this to a man at his own table is the deepest breach there is.\f*
\v 24 The son of man is going away, just as it has been written about him; but woe to that man through whom the son of man is handed over. It would have been better for that man if he had not been born."\f + \fr 26:24 \ft Both halves stand, and neither cancels the other. What happens is what was written; and the man who does it is not thereby excused of a thing. The heaviest sentence in this book is passed on somebody who is carrying out what scripture said would happen.\f*
\v 25 And Judas, the one handing him over, answered and said, "It is not I, Rabbi, is it?" He said to him, "You have said it."\f + \fr 26:25 \ft The others said "Lord." He says "Rabbi," and says it again in the garden (v. 49). In this book that is the outsider's word for him. And "you have said it" hands the sentence back to the man who spoke it — the same answer he will give the high priest (v. 64) and Pilate (27:11).\f*
\s1 The Bread and the Cup
\p
\v 26 And while they were eating, Jesus took bread and spoke the blessing, and he broke it and gave it to the disciples and said, "Take, eat; this is my body."\f + \fr 26:26 \ft The Passover meal already worked by interpretation: the head of the house held up the unleavened bread and said, "this is the bread of affliction our fathers ate in Egypt," and every man present was to reckon that he himself had come out of Egypt. Jesus takes that and points it somewhere else. Not the bread of Egypt. My body.\f*
\v 27 And taking a cup and giving thanks he gave it to them, saying, "Drink from it, all of you;
\v 28 for this is my blood of the covenant, which is poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins.\f + \fr 26:28 \ft Three scriptures are in this one line. Moses, having read the law to the people, threw the blood of the sacrifices over them and said, "behold, the blood of the covenant which the LORD has made with you" (Exodus 24:8) — the same words, with one substitution: my blood. Jeremiah promised a new covenant, not like the one made when God took them by the hand out of Egypt, and its mark was "I will forgive their wrongdoing and remember their sin no more" (Jeremiah 31:31-34). And Isaiah's servant "poured out his life to death... and he bore the sin of many" (Isaiah 53:12). "Many" there is not a limit; it is the Hebrew way of saying the multitude.\f*
\v 29 And I say to you, from now on I will never drink of this fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it new with you in the kingdom of my Father." \add [i.e. so the meal is unfinished, and the rest of it is somewhere else]\add*
\s1 All of You Will Be Tripped Up
\p
\v 30 And when they had sung the hymn they went out to the Mount of Olives.\f + \fr 26:30 \ft The hymn is the Hallel, Psalms 113-118, sung at the end of the Passover meal. So the last thing sung in that room was Psalm 118 — the psalm with "the stone the builders rejected has become the head of the corner" in it, which he threw at the chief priests two days ago (21:42), and "blessed is the one who comes in the name of the Lord," which he told Jerusalem it would have to say before it saw him again (23:39).\f*
\v 31 Then Jesus said to them, "All of you will be tripped up over me this night, for it has been written: 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.'\f + \fr 26:31 \ft Zechariah 13:7, and it is worth reading the whole line: "awake, sword, against my shepherd, against the man who stands next to me, says the LORD of hosts; strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered." The striking is God's own order. The scattering follows from it, and it is about to happen to the eleven men listening.\f*
\v 32 But after I have been raised I will go ahead of you into Galilee." \add [i.e. the scattering has a term on it, and he says so before it starts]\add*
\v 33 And Peter answered and said to him, "If they are all tripped up over you, I will never be tripped up."
\v 34 Jesus said to him, "Amen, I say to you, on this night, before a rooster crows, you will deny me three times."\f + \fr 26:34 \ft "Deny" is the same word as at 10:33: "whoever denies me before men, I too will deny him before my Father." Peter is being told, in advance and by name, that he is going to do the thing he was warned about.\f*
\v 35 Peter said to him, "Even if I must die with you, I will never deny you." And all the disciples said the same \add [i.e. all of them, and every one of them will run]\add*.
\s1 Gethsemane
\p
\v 36 Then Jesus came with them to a place called Gethsemane, and he said to the disciples, "Sit here while I go over there and pray."\f + \fr 26:36 \ft The name is Aramaic and means oil press — the stone weight that comes down on the olives until everything in them runs out.\f*
\v 37 And taking along Peter and the two sons of Zebedee he began to be grieved and in anguish \add [i.e. the same three who saw him shining on the mountain]\add*.
\v 38 Then he said to them, "My life within me is crushed with grief, to the point of death. Stay here and stay awake with me."\f + \fr 26:38 \ft The phrase is out of the Psalms — "why are you crushed down, my soul?" (Psalm 42:5, 11) — and out of Jonah, who says he is grieved to the point of death (Jonah 4:9). And "stay awake" is the word from the discourse two nights ago: watch, because you do not know the hour (24:42; 25:13). He is asking them to do the one thing he told them to do.\f*
\v 39 And going a little farther he fell on his face, praying and saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass by me. Yet not as I want, but as you want."\f + \fr 26:39 \ft The cup, in the prophets, is what God hands out in judgment: "in the hand of the LORD there is a cup... and all the wicked of the earth will drain it to the dregs" (Psalm 75:8); "you who have drunk from the hand of the LORD the cup of his wrath" (Isaiah 51:17); "take this cup of the wine of wrath from my hand and make the nations drink" (Jeremiah 25:15). It is the cup he asked James and John whether they could drink (20:22). The request is not a formality. He asks whether there is another way, and then does not insist on the answer.\f*
\v 40 And he came to the disciples and found them sleeping, and he said to Peter, "So you were not strong enough to stay awake with me one hour? \add [i.e. the man who said an hour ago that he would die first]\add*
\v 41 Stay awake and pray, so that you do not come into testing. The spirit is eager, but the flesh is weak."\f + \fr 26:41 \ft "So that you do not come into testing" is a line out of the prayer he taught them: do not bring us into the testing (6:13). And the "testing" is not everyday temptation — it is the crisis that finds out whether a man holds. It is a few hours away, and this is the only preparation available for it.\f*
\p
\v 42 Again, a second time, he went away and prayed, saying, "My Father, if this cannot pass by unless I drink it, let your will be done."\f + \fr 26:42 \ft The second prayer is not the first prayer repeated. It has moved: from "let it pass" to "if it cannot pass." And "let your will be done" is word for word out of the prayer he gave them (6:10). The thing he taught them to say every day is what he says here, and here is what it costs.\f*
\v 43 And he came again and found them sleeping, for their eyes were heavy.
\v 44 And leaving them he went away again and prayed a third time, saying the same word again \add [i.e. three prayers, three times found asleep; and before morning, three denials]\add*.
\p
\v 45 Then he came to the disciples and said to them, "Sleep on now and take your rest. Look — the hour has come near, and the son of man is handed over into the hands of sinners.
\v 46 Get up, let us go. Look — the one handing me over has come near."
\s1 The Arrest
\p
\v 47 And while he was still speaking — look, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a large crowd with swords and clubs, from the chief priests and elders of the people \add [i.e. "one of the twelve," said for the third time in this chapter]\add*.
\v 48 Now the one handing him over had given them a sign, saying, "The one I kiss is the man; seize him."
\v 49 And immediately he came up to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Rabbi," and he kissed him warmly \add [i.e. the greeting of a student to his teacher, used as a pointing finger]\add*.
\v 50 And Jesus said to him, "Friend, do what you came for." Then they came and laid hands on Jesus and seized him.\f + \fr 26:50 \ft "Friend" is the cool, formal word again — the third and last time this book uses it, after the grumbling labourer (20:13) and the man with no wedding clothes (22:12). All three times it is said courteously, and all three times to a man about to be found out.\f*
\p
\v 51 And look — one of those with Jesus stretched out his hand and drew his sword, and he struck the slave of the high priest and took off his ear.
\v 52 Then Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place; for all who take the sword will be destroyed by the sword.
\v 53 Or do you think that I cannot call on my Father, and he will at once put at my side more than twelve legions of angels?\f + \fr 26:53 \ft A legion was about six thousand men. Twelve of them is over seventy thousand, against a night mob with clubs — one for each of the twelve, one might say, with the ranks left over. The force is available and is not used, and the reason given in the next verse is not prudence. It is that the scriptures would then not be filled full.\f*
\v 54 How then would the scriptures be fulfilled, that it must happen this way?"
\p
\v 55 In that hour Jesus said to the crowds, "Have you come out with swords and clubs to arrest me, as against a bandit? Day after day I sat in the temple teaching, and you did not seize me \add [i.e. they have come by night, armed, for a man who has been standing in the temple courts all week]\add*.
\v 56 But all this has happened so that the writings of the prophets might be fulfilled." Then all the disciples left him and fled.\f + \fr 26:56 \ft "All" — the same word Peter used when he said that even if all the rest fell away he would not. The shepherd has been struck and the sheep have scattered, exactly as was said an hour ago on the road. Nobody is left.\f*
\s1 Before the High Priest
\p
\v 57 And those who had seized Jesus led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders had gathered together.
\v 58 And Peter was following him from a distance, as far as the courtyard of the high priest; and going inside he sat with the servants to see the end.\f + \fr 26:58 \ft "From a distance" is the whole of Peter's position. He has not run like the others and he is not with him either; and where he chooses to sit down is among the staff of the house that is trying him.\f*
\p
\v 59 Now the chief priests and the whole council were looking for false testimony against Jesus so that they might put him to death,\f + \fr 26:59 \ft The writer says it flatly. The court is not weighing evidence, it is shopping for it, and what it is shopping for is perjury — which the ninth of the ten commandments forbids. And it cannot find any that will hold.\f*
\v 60 and they did not find any, though many false witnesses came forward. Finally two came forward
\v 61 and said, "This man said, 'I am able to tear down the sanctuary of God and to build it in three days.'"\f + \fr 26:61 \ft Near enough to something he said to be usable, and wrong in the place that matters. He said the temple would come down, not that he would pull it down (24:2), and the three days belong to something else entirely.\f*
\v 62 And the high priest stood up and said to him, "Do you answer nothing? What are these men testifying against you?"
\p
\v 63 But Jesus was silent. And the high priest said to him, "I put you under oath by the living God to tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."\f + \fr 26:63 \ft "He was oppressed and he was afflicted, and he did not open his mouth; like a lamb led to the slaughter, and like a sheep silent before its shearers, he did not open his mouth" (Isaiah 53:7). The lambs had been killed that afternoon. Unable to make the false testimony stick, the high priest abandons the witnesses and puts the prisoner on oath to convict himself.\f*
\v 64 Jesus said to him, "You have said it. But I say to you, from now on you will see the son of man sitting at the right hand of the Power and coming on the clouds of the sky."\f + \fr 26:64 \ft He has been silent through the whole trial and now he answers, and the answer is two scriptures. "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool" is Psalm 110:1, the verse he used on the Pharisees three days ago (22:44); "the Power" is a way of saying God without saying God. And "coming on the clouds" is Daniel 7:13, where the one like a son of man arrives before the Ancient of Days and is handed a kingdom that will not be destroyed. A bound man tells the court that they will see him on the bench. And he says "from now on."\f*
\v 65 Then the high priest tore his clothes, saying, "He has blasphemed! What further need do we have of witnesses? Look, now you have heard the blasphemy.\f + \fr 26:65 \ft Tearing your clothes was the prescribed reaction to hearing blasphemy. It is also a confession that the trial is over: he no longer needs the witnesses he could not find, because the prisoner has said it himself.\f*
\v 66 What do you think?" And they answered and said, "He deserves death."
\v 67 Then they spat in his face and beat him with their fists; and some slapped him,\f + \fr 26:67 \ft "I gave my back to those who strike, and my cheeks to those who pull out the beard; I did not hide my face from shame and spitting" (Isaiah 50:6).\f*
\v 68 saying, "Prophesy to us, Christ — who is it that struck you?" \add [i.e. and at that moment every word he has prophesied is coming true in the next room]\add*
\s1 Peter Denies Him
\p
\v 69 Now Peter was sitting outside in the courtyard, and one servant girl came to him and said, "You too were with Jesus the Galilean." \add [i.e. the lowest-ranking person in the yard]\add*
\v 70 But he denied it in front of them all, saying, "I do not know what you are saying."
\v 71 And when he had gone out to the gateway, another girl saw him and said to those there, "This man was with Jesus the Nazarene."
\v 72 And again he denied it with an oath: "I do not know the man."\f + \fr 26:72 \ft Watch the escalation. First a denial, then a denial under oath, then a denial with a curse called down on himself. And an oath is the thing he was told never to swear: let your yes be yes (5:33-37). He is breaking his teacher's words in the act of pretending not to know him. Note also what he calls him — not Jesus, not my teacher, but "the man."\f*
\v 73 And after a little while those standing there came and said to Peter, "Truly you too are one of them, for even your speech gives you away." \add [i.e. a Galilean accent, which everyone in Jerusalem could hear a mile off]\add*
\v 74 Then he began to call down curses and to swear, "I do not know the man." And immediately a rooster crowed.
\v 75 And Peter remembered the word Jesus had said: "Before a rooster crows you will deny me three times." And he went outside and wept bitterly \add [i.e. and it is not the end of him — he was told already that the shepherd would go ahead of them into Galilee]\add*.
\c 27
\s1 Handed Over to Pilate
\p
\v 1 When morning came, all the chief priests and the elders of the people took counsel against Jesus to put him to death.
\v 2 And they tied him up and led him away and handed him over to Pilate the governor.\f + \fr 27:2 \ft The council could pass a sentence and could not carry one out; under Rome, a death sentence needed the governor. Pontius Pilate held Judea from about 26 to 36 — a real man, named in Josephus and in an inscription dug up at Caesarea. And the verb is the same one again: handed over, by Judas to them, by them to Rome, exactly as he said at 20:18-19.\f*
\s1 Judas and the Blood Money
\p
\v 3 Then Judas, the one who handed him over, seeing that he had been condemned, changed his mind and brought back the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders,\f + \fr 27:3 \ft The Greek word is not the one this book uses for turning back to God (3:2; 4:17). It means regret — being sorry about how a thing came out. What follows is what it produces.\f*
\v 4 saying, "I have sinned in handing over innocent blood." But they said, "What is that to us? You see to it."\f + \fr 27:4 \ft "Innocent blood" is a phrase with weight in the Torah: it defiles the land and it falls on the people who let it be shed (Deuteronomy 19:10; 21:8-9), and Jeremiah warned his own judges, "if you put me to death you bring innocent blood on yourselves and on this city" (Jeremiah 26:15). The man who arranged the arrest is the first witness in this chapter to say the prisoner is guiltless. He will not be the last.\f*
\v 5 And throwing the pieces of silver into the sanctuary he left, and he went away and hanged himself.\f + \fr 27:5 \ft David's counsellor Ahithophel, having gone over to Absalom and seen his advice rejected, "went to his house, set his affairs in order, and hanged himself" (2 Samuel 17:23) — the same word in the Greek. And the money goes back into the sanctuary itself, thrown at the men who weighed it out.\f*
\v 6 And the chief priests took the pieces of silver and said, "It is not permitted to put them into the treasury, since it is the price of blood."\f + \fr 27:6 \ft They will not put tainted money in the temple fund. They paid it out four days ago. This is the gnat strained out by men who have just swallowed the camel (23:24).\f*
\v 7 So they took counsel and bought with them the potter's field, as a burial place for strangers.
\v 8 For this reason that field has been called the Field of Blood to this day \add [i.e. a place in Jerusalem the first readers of this could go and look at]\add*.
\v 9 Then what was spoken through Jeremiah the prophet was fulfilled: "And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of the one who had been priced, on whom a price had been set by the sons of Israel,
\v 10 and they gave them for the potter's field, as the Lord ordered me."\f + \fr 27:10 \ft The wording is Zechariah's — the rejected shepherd paid off with thirty pieces of silver, "the lordly price at which I was valued by them," and told to throw it to the potter in the house of the LORD (Zechariah 11:12-13). The name attached is Jeremiah's, and Jeremiah is where the rest of the picture comes from: the prophet buying a potter's jar and smashing it in the valley, and saying the place would be renamed and used for burying because of the blood shed there (Jeremiah 19:1-13), and the prophet buying a field as a sign (Jeremiah 32:6-15). The citation is a weave of the two, filed under the larger prophet.\f*
\s1 Before the Governor
\p
\v 11 Now Jesus stood in front of the governor, and the governor questioned him, saying, "Are you the king of the Jews?" And Jesus said, "You say so." \add [i.e. the council tried him for blasphemy; before Rome the charge has to be sedition, and only one charge will do]\add*
\v 12 And while he was being accused by the chief priests and elders he answered nothing.
\v 13 Then Pilate said to him, "Do you not hear how many things they are testifying against you?"
\v 14 And he did not answer him, not even to one word, so that the governor was greatly amazed.\f + \fr 27:14 \ft "He was oppressed and afflicted, and he did not open his mouth; like a lamb led to the slaughter and like a sheep before its shearers, he did not open his mouth" (Isaiah 53:7). Under Roman procedure a man defended himself; that was the whole point of the hearing. Pilate has never seen anyone decline.\f*
\s1 Barabbas
\p
\v 15 Now at the feast the governor was accustomed to release to the crowd one prisoner whom they wanted.
\v 16 And at that time they had a notorious prisoner called Jesus Barabbas.\f + \fr 27:16 \ft Bar-Abba means, in Aramaic, son of the father. A number of early copies give his first name as Jesus, which is how the next verse reads here, and it makes the choice put to the crowd as stark as it can be made: two men called Jesus, one of them the son of the father in name, the other the Son of the Father.\f*
\v 17 So when they had gathered together, Pilate said to them, "Whom do you want me to release to you — Jesus Barabbas, or Jesus who is called Christ?"
\v 18 For he knew that they had handed him over out of envy \add [i.e. the governor can see the motive and proceeds anyway]\add*.
\v 19 And while he was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent word to him, saying, "Have nothing to do with that righteous man, for I have suffered much today in a dream because of him."\f + \fr 27:19 \ft Dreams are how God gets word through in this book — five of them in the first two chapters, to Joseph and to the magi. Here it is a Gentile woman in the governor's house who gets the warning, and calls the prisoner righteous, while the council that has the scriptures has condemned him.\f*
\v 20 But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas and to destroy Jesus \add [i.e. the crowd is worked on; it does not arrive at this by itself]\add*.
\v 21 And the governor answered and said to them, "Which of the two do you want me to release to you?" And they said, "Barabbas."
\v 22 Pilate said to them, "What then shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ?" They all said, "Let him be crucified!"
\v 23 And he said, "Why? What evil has he done?" But they screamed all the more, saying, "Let him be crucified!" \add [i.e. the judge cannot name the crime, and passes sentence anyway]\add*
\s1 Pilate Washes His Hands
\p
\v 24 And when Pilate saw that he was getting nowhere but that an uproar was starting instead, he took water and washed his hands in front of the crowd, saying, "I am innocent of this blood. You see to it."\f + \fr 27:24 \ft The gesture is out of the Torah, not out of Rome: when a body was found in open country and no one knew who had killed the man, the elders of the nearest town washed their hands and said, "our hands did not shed this blood" (Deuteronomy 21:6-7). A Roman governor performs an Israelite rite to get clear of a killing he has the power to stop — and the words he uses to hand the matter back, "you see to it," are word for word what the chief priests said to Judas twenty verses ago.\f*
\v 25 And all the people answered and said, "His blood be on us and on our children."\f + \fr 27:25 \ft A legal formula for taking on liability for a death: "your blood be on your own head" (2 Samuel 1:16; Joshua 2:19). What they are accepting is what he told them was coming — "all these things will come on this generation" (23:36). The scope is the one he set: a generation, not a people for ever.\f*
\v 26 Then he released Barabbas to them; but Jesus he had flogged and handed over to be crucified.\f + \fr 27:26 \ft The Roman scourge was a leather whip weighted with bone or metal, and it was the standard preliminary to crucifixion. Josephus records men who died under it before they reached the cross.\f*
\s1 The Soldiers Dress Him as a King
\p
\v 27 Then the soldiers of the governor took Jesus along into the praetorium and gathered the whole cohort around him \add [i.e. the governor's residence, and several hundred men]\add*.
\v 28 And they stripped him and put a scarlet cloak around him,
\v 29 and twisting together a crown out of thorns they put it on his head, and a reed in his right hand; and kneeling in front of him they mocked him, saying, "Greetings, king of the Jews!"\f + \fr 27:29 \ft Every piece of it is a coronation done backwards. A soldier's red cloak for the purple; a twist of thorn for the laurel; a stick for the sceptre; the kneeling that a subject does; and "greetings, king of the Jews" for "hail, Caesar." They are performing, as a joke, the thing this book has been saying since the magi asked where the newborn king of the Jews was (2:2).\f*
\v 30 And they spat on him and took the reed and struck him on the head \add [i.e. the sceptre, used as a club]\add*.
\v 31 And when they had mocked him they stripped the cloak off him and put his own clothes on him, and they led him away to crucify him.
\s1 Golgotha
\p
\v 32 As they were going out they found a man of Cyrene named Simon; this man they pressed into service to carry his cross.\f + \fr 27:32 \ft "Pressed into service" is the same word as at 5:41, where a man forced to go one mile is told to go two — the soldier's legal right to commandeer a passer-by. Cyrene is in North Africa; the man is named, and his sons are named in another account, which suggests the family was known.\f*
\v 33 And when they came to a place called Golgotha, which is called Place of a Skull,
\v 34 they gave him wine to drink mixed with gall; and when he had tasted it he did not want to drink it.\f + \fr 27:34 \ft "They gave me gall for food, and for my thirst they gave me sour wine to drink" (Psalm 69:21). He tastes it and refuses it, and goes into what follows with nothing dulled.\f*
\v 35 And when they had crucified him they divided his clothes among themselves by throwing lots,\f + \fr 27:35 \ft "They divide my garments among them, and for my clothing they cast lots" (Psalm 22:18). No fulfilment formula is attached; from here to the end of the afternoon this psalm keeps happening, and the writer leaves the reader to notice. Some later copies do attach a formula here, quoting the psalm outright — which the earliest copies do not have, and which spends the restraint the writer was exercising.\f*
\v 36 and they sat down and were keeping watch over him there.
\v 37 And they put above his head the charge against him, written: "This is Jesus, the king of the Jews." \add [i.e. Roman practice was to post the crime; the crime is who he is]\add*
\v 38 Then two bandits were crucified with him, one at his right and one at his left.\f + \fr 27:38 \ft The word is the one used for the armed insurrectionists of the period, and it is the word he threw at the arresting party — have you come out against me as against a bandit (26:55)? The man who told his own follower to put the sword away dies between two men who used theirs. "He was counted with the lawless" (Isaiah 53:12). And two men at his right and his left is what the sons of Zebedee asked for (20:21).\f*
\s1 Those Who Passed By
\p
\v 39 And those passing by were slandering him, shaking their heads
\v 40 and saying, "You who tear down the sanctuary and build it in three days, rescue yourself! If you are the Son of God, come down from the cross!"\f + \fr 27:40 \ft "All who see me mock me; they make faces and shake their heads" (Psalm 22:7). And listen to the opening words: "if you are the Son of God" is the tester's sentence in the wilderness, twice (4:3, 6), and the proposal is the same one — prove it by using the power to save yourself.\f*
\v 41 In the same way the chief priests also, along with the scribes and elders, were mocking him and saying,
\v 42 "He rescued others; he cannot rescue himself. He is the king of Israel — let him come down now from the cross, and we will trust in him.
\v 43 He has trusted in God; let God rescue him now if he wants him. For he said, 'I am God's Son.'"\f + \fr 27:43 \ft "He trusted in the LORD — let him rescue him; let him deliver him, since he delights in him" (Psalm 22:8). They are speaking the lines of the enemies in the psalm, and the psalm does not end where they stop reading: it turns at verse 22 and finishes with all the ends of the earth remembering and turning to the LORD. And their taunt is exact and true. He cannot rescue himself, because he came to give his life as a ransom for many (20:28), and a man cannot do both.\f*
\v 44 And the bandits who were crucified with him were insulting him in the same way \add [i.e. nobody there is on his side, including the two dying beside him]\add*.
\s1 Darkness, and the Cry
\p
\v 45 Now from the sixth hour darkness came over the whole land until the ninth hour.\f + \fr 27:45 \ft Noon to three in the afternoon. "On that day, says the Lord GOD, I will make the sun go down at noon and darken the earth in broad daylight" — and the next line in Amos is "I will make it like the mourning for an only son" (Amos 8:9-10). Behind that stands the ninth plague, three days of darkness over Egypt, at the first Passover (Exodus 10:22).\f*
\v 46 And around the ninth hour Jesus shouted with a great voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lema sabachthani?" which is, "My God, my God, why have you abandoned me?"\f + \fr 27:46 \ft The first line of Psalm 22, in his own language. That psalm has been running under this whole scene — the mockery, the shaken heads, the lots thrown for his clothes, the taunt about trusting God. To quote a psalm's opening line was to put the whole psalm on the table, and this one does not stay where it starts. It turns at verse 22 — "I will tell of your name to my brothers, in the middle of the assembly I will praise you" — and ends with all the families of the nations worshipping, and a people not yet born being told that he has done it.\f*
\v 47 And some of those standing there heard it and said, "This man is calling Elijah." \add [i.e. mishearing Eli, my God, as Eliyahu]\add*
\v 48 And immediately one of them ran and took a sponge and filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed and gave him a drink.
\v 49 But the rest said, "Leave him alone. Let us see whether Elijah comes to rescue him."
\s1 The Curtain Is Torn
\p
\v 50 And Jesus shouted again with a great voice and let go of his spirit.\f + \fr 27:50 \ft "Let go" is a choice of word. It is the same verb used through this book for releasing a debt and forgiving a sin, and for men leaving their nets. He is not described as expiring. He is described as letting go — which is what he said at 20:28 he had come to do.\f*
\v 51 And look — the curtain of the sanctuary was torn in two from top to bottom, and the earth was shaken and the rocks were split,\f + \fr 27:51 \ft The inner curtain shut off the Most Holy Place, where God's presence was, and one man went through it one day a year carrying blood (Leviticus 16). It is torn from the top down, which says who tore it. What the high priest's one annual entry stood for is now standing open, at the moment the blood of the covenant is poured out (26:28).\f*
\v 52 and the tombs were opened, and many bodies of the holy ones who had fallen asleep were raised;
\v 53 and coming out of the tombs after his raising they went into the holy city and appeared to many.\f + \fr 27:53 \ft "Many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth will awake" (Daniel 12:2) — and in Daniel that comes right after "a time of trouble such as there has never been" (12:1), the verse Jesus drew on at 24:21. Note the care of the sentence: the tombs open at his death, and the men come out after his raising. He goes first.\f*
\v 54 And when the centurion and those with him keeping watch over Jesus saw the shaking and the things that happened, they were very much afraid and said, "Truly this man was God's Son."\f + \fr 27:54 \ft It is said, at last, by the men who drove in the nails. God said it at the river and on the mountain; the tester used it as a lever; demons screamed it; Peter confessed it; the high priest called it blasphemy; the crowd at the foot of the cross threw it up as a taunt an hour ago. The first human beings in this book to look at him dead and say it are Gentiles in Roman uniform.\f*
\s1 The Women Watching
\p
\v 55 And many women were there, watching from a distance, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, serving him;\f + \fr 27:55 \ft Two words are used of them, and both are this book's words for discipleship: they followed him, and they served him — the very verb he used of himself, that the son of man came not to be served but to serve (20:28). Every man he called has run (26:56). These women watched him die, watched where he was buried (v. 61), and were at the tomb first (28:1); the chain of witness runs through them.\f*
\v 56 among them were Mary Magdalene, and Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee.
\s1 The Burial
\p
\v 57 When evening came a rich man from Arimathea named Joseph, who had himself become a disciple of Jesus, came.\f + \fr 27:57 \ft A rich man, and a disciple — which is the thing that was said to be harder than getting a camel through a needle's eye, and immediately after that, possible with God (19:24-26). Getting a body off a Roman cross needed the governor's leave; leaving it there overnight was what the Torah forbade (Deuteronomy 21:22-23).\f*
\v 58 This man went to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus. Then Pilate ordered it to be given to him.
\v 59 And taking the body, Joseph wrapped it in a clean linen cloth
\v 60 and laid it in his own new tomb, which he had cut in the rock; and rolling a great stone against the door of the tomb he went away.\f + \fr 27:60 \ft "They made his grave with the wicked, and with a rich man in his death" (Isaiah 53:9). Note the details the writer takes trouble over: a clean cloth, a new tomb, nobody in it before. There will shortly be a question about which grave and whose body, and every answer is being nailed down here.\f*
\v 61 And Mary Magdalene was there, and the other Mary, sitting opposite the grave.
\s1 The Guard at the Grave
\p
\v 62 On the next day, which is after the Preparation, the chief priests and the Pharisees gathered together to Pilate,\f + \fr 27:62 \ft The Preparation is the day before the Sabbath. So this is the Sabbath itself, and they have come out on it — which tells you how urgently they wanted this done.\f*
\v 63 saying, "Sir, we remembered that while he was still alive that deceiver said, 'After three days I am raised.'\f + \fr 27:63 \ft They remember it better than his own followers do; nobody on his side is at the tomb expecting anything. And the word they use for him, deceiver, is from the same root as the leading-astray he warned them about (24:4-5, 11, 24).\f*
\v 64 So order the grave to be made secure until the third day, or his disciples may come and steal him and say to the people, 'He has been raised from the dead,' and the last deception will be worse than the first."
\v 65 Pilate said to them, "You have a guard. Go, make it as secure as you know how."
\v 66 So they went and made the grave secure, sealing the stone and setting the guard.\f + \fr 27:66 \ft A cord across the stone with a seal on it, so that any movement would show, and soldiers in front of it. Every precaution against the body being taken is put in place by the people who did not want it taken — and the story they will pay to have told, three days later, is that it was taken (28:11-15).\f*
\c 28
\s1 The Grave Is Open
\p
\v 1 After the Sabbath, as it was growing light toward the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary came to look at the grave \add [i.e. the same two who watched him die and watched him buried]\add*.
\v 2 And look — there was a great shaking, for an angel of the Lord came down out of heaven and came and rolled away the stone and was sitting on it.\f + \fr 28:2 \ft The earth shook when he died (27:51) and it shakes again now. "An angel of the Lord" is the figure who stood at the other end of this book, telling Joseph what the child would be called and when to run for Egypt (1:20; 2:13, 19). And note what the stone is moved for. Not to let him out — he is already gone. It is rolled back so that the women can see in, and the angel sits down on it, on the thing the chief priests sealed.\f*
\v 3 His appearance was like lightning and his clothing white as snow.\f + \fr 28:3 \ft Daniel's language for the heavenly court: the Ancient of Days, whose "clothing was white as snow" (Daniel 7:9), and the man by the river whose "face was like lightning" (Daniel 10:6).\f*
\v 4 And in their fear of him those keeping watch were shaken and became like dead men \add [i.e. the living men set to guard a dead one go down like corpses, and the dead man is up]\add*.
\v 5 But the angel answered and said to the women, "You — do not be afraid. For I know that you are looking for Jesus, the one who was crucified \add [i.e. and he is still that; being raised does not undo the cross]\add*.
\v 6 He is not here, for he has been raised, just as he said. Come, see the place where he was lying.\f + \fr 28:6 \ft "Just as he said" is the point of the sentence. Everything else he predicted in the last week has come off exactly — the betrayal, the scattering, the three denials, the handing over to Rome. This is the last of them, and the women are invited to go in and check.\f*
\v 7 And go quickly and tell his disciples that he has been raised from the dead; and look — he is going ahead of you into Galilee. There you will see him. Look — I have told you."\f + \fr 28:7 \ft Word for word the promise he made them on the road to the garden: "after I have been raised I will go ahead of you into Galilee" (26:32). Not Jerusalem, where the council sits. Galilee, where he found them.\f*
\s1 Jesus Meets Them
\p
\v 8 And they went away quickly from the tomb with fear and great joy, and they ran to report it to his disciples \add [i.e. and both feelings belong there at once]\add*.
\v 9 And look — Jesus met them and said, "Greetings." And they came and took hold of his feet and bowed down before him.\f + \fr 28:9 \ft They take hold of him — the word is to grip, to seize — which settles the question of whether this is a ghost. And they go down in front of him. The verb is the one used of the magi at the beginning (2:11), of the men in the boat (14:33), of the Canaanite woman (15:25); and it is the verb in the sentence he threw at the tester in the wilderness — "you shall bow down to the Lord your God, and him only shall you serve" (4:10). Nobody corrects them.\f*
\v 10 Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go away into Galilee, and there they will see me."\f + \fr 28:10 \ft The angel said "his disciples." He says "my brothers." These are men who ran into the dark four nights ago and have not been seen since, and one of them swore on a curse that he had never met him. At 12:50 he said that whoever does the will of his Father is his brother. He uses the word now, before any of them has said a word to him.\f*
\s1 The Guards Are Paid
\p
\v 11 As they were going, look — some of the guard went into the city and reported to the chief priests everything that had happened \add [i.e. two sets of witnesses to the same morning, walking in opposite directions with the same news]\add*.
\v 12 And when they had gathered together with the elders and taken counsel, they gave a large sum of silver to the soldiers,\f + \fr 28:12 \ft "Took counsel" is what they did to have him killed (27:1), and silver is what they weighed out to get him arrested (26:15). The same men, the same procedure, the same currency — first to put him in the tomb, now to explain the tomb away.\f*
\v 13 saying, "Say, 'His disciples came by night and stole him while we were sleeping.'\f + \fr 28:13 \ft The story eats itself. Men who were asleep cannot say who came, and men who were awake did not let it happen. And it is precisely the story the chief priests invented four days ago as the thing that must be prevented, and posted a guard against (27:64). Now they are paying to have it told.\f*
\v 14 And if this is heard by the governor, we will persuade him and keep you out of trouble."\f + \fr 28:14 \ft They need that clause. Sleeping on watch was a capital offence in the Roman army, and the soldiers know it. The word for the freedom from worry they are promised is the word from the sermon — do not be anxious (6:25-34). What is on offer here is a quiet mind bought with a lie.\f*
\v 15 So they took the silver and did as they were taught. And this word has been spread among the Jews to this day.\f + \fr 28:15 \ft "To this day" is the writer speaking over the story to whoever is reading it, as at 27:8. He is not describing a rumour that died out; he is answering one that is still going round while he writes. That is why the guard, the seal and the bribe are in this book at all — the counter-story was the thing his readers kept being told, and he sets out where it came from.\f*
\s1 On the Mountain in Galilee
\p
\v 16 And the eleven disciples went into Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had arranged to meet them \add [i.e. eleven, and the gap in the number is not explained; it does not need to be]\add*.
\v 17 And when they saw him they bowed down before him; but some doubted.\f + \fr 28:17 \ft The word is used once before in this book, of Peter, out on the water, going under (14:31). It does not mean unbelief. It means a mind not all in one place. This is the last scene in the whole account, and the writer will not tidy it: they worship, and some of them are not sure. The men who are about to be sent to the nations are the men described all through this book as having too little trust, and they are sent anyway.\f*
\v 18 And Jesus came up and spoke to them, saying, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.\f + \fr 28:18 \ft This is Daniel 7 arriving. "To him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations and languages should serve him; his dominion is everlasting and will not pass away" (Daniel 7:14). It is given, not taken — the passive says who gave it. And it is the announcement that what he told the council they would see (26:64) has happened. The authority they were arguing about in the temple (21:23) has been settled somewhere else.\f*
\v 19 So go and make disciples of all the nations, immersing them into the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit,\f + \fr 28:19 \ft "So" — because of the authority, therefore the sending. And the boundary comes down. He had sent them once with orders to go nowhere among the nations and nowhere into Samaria, but only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (10:5-6); he had said of himself that he was sent to no one else (15:24). Now it is all the nations, which is Daniel's phrase and also Abraham's promise, that in him all the families of the earth would be blessed. "Into the name" is a Hebrew way of saying into everything the name stands for: they are being put inside a reality, not given a formula.\f*
\v 20 teaching them to keep everything I ordered you. And look — I am with you all the days, until the completion of the age."\f + \fr 28:20 \ft Note what is to be taught: not information but a way of living — "to keep," which in this book has always meant doing and not merely hearing (7:24-27). And the last words close a circle a whole book wide. It opened with a name: "they will call his name Emmanuel," which translated is, "God with us" (1:23). It ends with the man himself saying it in the first person. He does not leave; the book has no ascension in it. The final word is that he is here, for as long as there is an age to be in.\f*
